B.a3k=s(["THE LIST NOW READS ONLY WHAT CHANGED - the last piece of your 1-second delete. With the runway fixed, the remaining time was one thing: every refresh read all 4,500 items from the database (a fifth to a half a second) and then reported finding NOTHING new, over and over. It now reads just the items that actually changed since the last look.","THIS IS THE CHANGE I HAVE BEEN AVOIDING ALL NIGHT, because narrowing what the list reads is precisely how your items got hidden from you twice before. So it refuses itself in three separate ways: it only runs when the item count has not FALLEN (a fall means something was deleted somewhere, and a partial read cannot see a deletion); it forces a full read every couple of minutes regardless, so anything missed can be stale for minutes rather than forever; and after merging it counts the items independently and does the full read anyway if the two numbers disagree by even one. The merge itself can never remove anything - only a full pass may do that.",'THE RULE UNDERNEATH IT: an item missing from a partial read means nothing at all, because a deleted item simply is not in the batch. Treating "absent" as "deleted" is the exact mistake behind both times your items vanished. Ten new tests pin that, including the one that matters most - an item not mentioned in the batch must still be on your screen afterwards.','AND IF SOMETHING EVER DOES LOOK MISSING, the log now says "incremental_mismatch" with both counts at the moment it happened, so it is a fact to read rather than a mystery to reproduce.'],t.s)
B.aw9=new A.t(845,B.a3k)
B.Yv=s(["CORRECTING MY OWN 843 BEFORE YOU HIT IT. That build was supposed to make deleting instant by keeping the list's big pre-build runway only for scrolling. It would not have worked, and I caught it reading my own code: the runway was armed the moment you TOUCHED the list - and deleting starts with a tap. So the tap grew the runway back to a thousand items and the delete paid the same 1.7 seconds it always did. The fix would have done nothing for the one thing you asked me to fix.","WHAT CHANGED: the runway now arms on the first real MOVEMENT, not on contact. A tap does not move; a scroll does. So tapping delete, or tapping to create, keeps the list on its short runway and the rebuild is small - while a genuine drag still gets the full runway within the first few pixels, before a fling has any speed, which is what the scrolling fix needed. The mouse wheel arms it directly, since there is no finger to watch.","IF DELETING IS STILL SLOW ON THIS BUILD, tell me and I will stop guessing at this area - it will mean the rebuild cost is not the runway after all, and the frame log will say so with a number rather than another theory from me."],t.s)
B.atZ=new A.t(844,B.Yv)
B.a_0=s(["THIS IS THE ONE - the actual cause of your slow deletes and creates, found and fixed. Your list was told to keep 120,000 PIXELS of items built and laid out at all times. Not the eight you can see - somewhere between six hundred and a thousand of them. Every time the list refreshed, all of them were rebuilt. That is the 1.7 seconds.","HOW IT WAS PROVEN, after three of my theories were wrong: Flutter's own frame data. The worst frame spent 1673ms BUILDING and only 22ms waiting to start, with the graphics card idle at 9ms. So it was real work, in the widget layer, and none of the four things I had instrumented (all of them milliseconds). I had even talked myself into believing that 2-second number was a measurement glitch because it never varied - but a fixed-size runway is a fixed amount of work, which is exactly why it never varied. Right clue, wrong conclusion.","THE FIX KEEPS BOTH THINGS YOU WANT. That long runway exists for a good reason - it is what stopped the scroll braking when passing large items, which you reported back in v690 - but it is only needed WHILE you are scrolling, and refreshes happen when you delete or create, which is when you are not. So the list now takes its full runway the instant you touch it (or turn the wheel on Windows) and hands it back a moment after everything settles. Scrolling is untouched; deleting and creating stop paying for a thousand items they never needed.","THE CARE: the long runway is armed on touch-DOWN, while your finger is still stationary, never once the list is already moving - re-laying out under a moving finger is the exact jank this whole area has caused before. It also arms on the mouse wheel, because on Windows there is no finger to detect, and it never shrinks while a finger is down or the list is still gliding."],t.s)
B.arS=new A.t(843,B.a_0)
B.YL=s(["THE AI TITLE IS OFF THE CREATE PATH - your ask, and it explains why creating always felt a second slower than deleting (your timings: 3 seconds against 2). The title was already fire-and-forget so it never blocked you, but it fired in the same instant you posted: a network call to OpenAI, then a write that bumps the item, which repaints the list exactly while you are watching it appear. Deleting has no equivalent call, which is the whole difference. The title now waits until the create is over and finishes quietly a few seconds later. Same title, same repaint when it lands - it just no longer competes with the moment that matters.","THIS ALSO MATTERS MOST WHEN PASTING - importing several items used to fire one network call per item, all at once. Now they queue up behind the import instead of fighting it.","AND THE 2-SECOND FRAME GETS A STRAIGHT ANSWER. I have instrumented four suspects and every one came back in single-digit milliseconds, while the frame log kept insisting one frame costs two seconds. That number is suspiciously identical every time - 2.0 to 2.4 seconds, never anything else - and real work is never that consistent. So this build logs what Flutter itself knows: how long the frame spent WAITING to start versus actually working. If the two seconds is waiting, I have been chasing a ghost for two builds and will say so.","NOTHING ELSE CHANGES. Your words, your items and your ordering are untouched."],t.s)
B.aui=new A.t(842,B.YL)
B.a_5=s(["SYNC NOW SAYS WHETHER IT IS ALIVE - and the reason this build exists is uncomfortable. Your two Windows machines were showing different folder counts, and the cause turned out to be that one of them had stopped exchanging anything with the server in BOTH directions: 26 of the server's 40 newest items never arrived, and 2,408 local changes could not leave. That went on for hours with no error anywhere and nothing on screen to tell either of us.",'THE SILENCE WAS THE REAL BUG. Every "cannot sync" path in the code simply stopped quietly - no session, no credentials, nothing to report - because as far as the sync engine is concerned that is not a failure. So the only symptom was two machines slowly disagreeing. A heartbeat now records the truth every cycle: signed in or not, connected or not, when it last synced, how many changes are waiting, and any upload or download error. Whether it is working or not, the answer is in the log.',"WHAT I BELIEVE IS WRONG ON THAT MACHINE: it is signed out. There is no saved session on it, which makes the sync engine authenticate nothing while still reporting itself connected. Signing out and back in there should restore it. Your local items are NOT touched by signing out - I checked the code specifically for that before suggesting it - and none of your words are lost either way; they are stored locally and simply have not travelled yet.","NO OTHER BEHAVIOR CHANGES. This build adds one log line and nothing else."],t.s)
B.arx=new A.t(841,B.a_5)
B.XZ=s(["MEASUREMENT ONLY - nothing about the app changes in this build. After 839 your deletes were still around 3 seconds, and I was honest that my last fix missed: the database is now only about four tenths of a second of that, but ONE frame still costs nearly two seconds, and nothing in the log says WHICH piece of work that is.","SO THIS BUILD NAMES THEM. Four new numbers in the diagnostic log, each around a piece of work that walks your whole library on every refresh: applying the rebuilt list, filtering which items the window shows, counting folder contents, and working out which items are filed. The same kind of probe that caught the last two real culprits - the one that had been quietly recording the answer in your log for weeks before I thought to read it.","WHY I AM NOT GUESSING AGAIN: tonight every time I reasoned about where the time went I was wrong, and every time I measured I was right. Three of my theories died on measurement. Rather than ship a fourth guess at your list, this build makes the next log say the answer with a name and a number attached.","AND THE BIGGER ISSUE ON THIS MACHINE IS NOT SPEED - it is that it has stopped exchanging anything with the server in either direction, which is why your two machines show different folder counts. Signing out and back in on that machine should restore it, and your local items are not touched by doing so - I checked the code before suggesting it."],t.s)
B.arB=new A.t(840,B.XZ)
B.a_z=s(["CREATING AND DELETING SHOULD BE QUICKER AGAIN - your timings drove this. After 838 the database was no longer the problem (a list read was down to about a third of a second, with no waiting for a turn at all), so I went looking at the drawing side, and the log was blunt: ONE frame took 1.6 seconds to build, and a six-character note caused TWO complete rebuilds of all 4,535 items, each reporting that it found nothing new.","FIRST FIX - the list stops re-making everything. Every refresh used to build 4,535 brand-new item objects even when nothing about them had changed, so nothing downstream could tell an untouched item from a changed one and the whole list repainted. Untouched items now keep the object they already had, so only what actually changed gets redrawn. Same items, same order, same everything on screen - the wasted work is what goes.","SECOND FIX - the AI title no longer causes a second full rebuild. Posting a note rebuilt the list, then the AI title came back a moment later, changed the item, and triggered the whole rebuild AGAIN. That was half the cost of creating anything. The title now tells the list directly, in the same breath as saving, so the follow-up refresh finds nothing to do. The title still appears - it appears from that update.",'AND ONE GAP CLOSED WHILE I WAS IN THERE, found by the new tests rather than by luck: the check that decides "did anything change?" was ignoring an item\'s audio. An audio clip landing on its own could not repaint the item, so its play control would not appear until something else forced a redraw. That is the same shape as the title bug from v788 - a field the box draws that the change-test could not see. Nine new tests pin all of it, including that a real change (text, title, type, status, reply, audio) always redraws.'],t.s)
B.asz=new A.t(839,B.a_z)
B.a2X=s(['THE ANSWER TO "WHY ARE SO MANY ITEMS STACKED UP TO BE UPLOADED" - they were never real changes. Your app was saving the same untouched items over and over. One log held 1,213 saves: the same item, at the same byte length, 27 to 30 times each. Every one of those phantom saves queued an upload, bumped the item, made the app re-read your whole library, and ordered a fresh AI title - 690 title calls, about 24 per item, each a real paid API call.','WHY IT LOOPED: the editor asks "did this text change?" by converting what is on screen into the stored form and comparing. But it compared that against the item\'s RAW stored text - two different alphabets. A paragraph can be stored either as a special paragraph character or as a plain blank line, and the conversion only goes one way. So for any item stored the second way the two sides could never match, the item read as changed forever, and it saved, re-read, and still looked changed. Both sides now go through the identical conversion, so the question is about YOUR typing rather than which marker happens to be on disk.',"THIS WAS THE ENGINE UNDER TODAY'S SLOWNESS - the sluggish creating, loading and deleting, the stalls, and the upload count that would not come down. It was never your data: those writes were byte-identical and I checked your rows against the server, which matched exactly. Nothing was lost or damaged, it was pure wasted work.","THE CARE TAKEN, because this lives on the save path: sixteen tests pin it in BOTH directions. An untouched item must never re-save - stored either way, mixed markers, extra blank lines, trailing breaks. And a REAL edit must still save - a typed word, a deletion, a new paragraph, a soft line break, clearing the box, even a single character. Making this test stricter is only safe if genuine edits still land, so that is asserted, not assumed."],t.s)
B.aur=new A.t(838,B.a2X)
B.a35=s(["DELETES AGAIN - your three timed tests are what found this. 3 then 11 seconds, 2 then 9, then 2 and 2, then long again. Two things hid in that pattern: a QUEUE, because the second delete reliably waited on the first one finishing, and a floor that stayed around 2 seconds even after the queue drained. Yesterday's build fixed the wrong half of it.","WHAT I GOT WRONG IN 836: I sped up how the app REBUILDS your list after a change (that part worked - it went from 55 rebuilds in two seconds down to one), but the app was still RE-READING your entire library from the database on every single write. Around 4,500 items, each with 28 fields pulled out of stored text, on the one database connection everything else needs. So deleting one item spent that connection re-reading everything, and your next delete had to wait its turn behind it. That is the queue you timed.","THE FIX: the app now re-reads the library ONCE after a burst of changes settles, instead of once per change. Same rows, same order, nothing filtered differently - only how often it asks. Your log made this provable rather than guesswork: a query that does literally nothing waited 35.9 seconds for a turn while the real read took 1.3. The work was never slow; getting a turn was, so the fix is to stop spending turns.","HONEST LIMITS: the deep cause is that syncing and reading share one connection, so a heavy sync catch-up - right after you update a machine, for instance - can still make things crawl. This build reduces how much the app competes with itself, it does not remove that sharing. If deletes still drag once your machines are in sync, the log now has the numbers to prove where it goes, and I would rather have those than another theory."],t.s)
B.aua=new A.t(837,B.a35)
B.Xy=s(["THE LIST AND THE DELETES SHOULD BOTH FEEL FASTER - this is the fix for your question, why the list gets worse as the number of items grows. Your own log convicted it: in one two-second window the app rebuilt your entire library FIFTY-FIVE times, burning over a full second of the drawing thread, with 90 of 122 frames stuttering. Across the session that climbed from 348ms to 1127ms per window. That is the same starvation that made items not appear after you hit send - the app had already fetched your list in a tenth of a second and then took minutes to get a turn to draw it.","THREE CHANGES, and the first one is almost embarrassing: the app already had a throttle to stop that storm, but it sat AFTER the expensive work instead of before it. So all 55 rebuilds happened and about 49 of them were thrown away without ever reaching the screen. The throttle now comes first. Second, when one item changes, only that item is rebuilt instead of all 4,518. Third, deleting no longer makes the app re-read your whole library to be told about the deletion it just performed - it recognises its own work.","WHAT DID NOT CHANGE, deliberately: not one thing about WHICH items the list shows. Every filter is untouched, because narrowing what the list reads is exactly how items got hidden from you twice before, and I am not repeating that to win a benchmark. The delete shortcut is arithmetic, not a guess - it only applies when the numbers match its own deletions exactly, and anything unexpected at all falls back to the full re-read. Nine tests pin that it fails safe, including the case where a new item of yours arrives in the same moment as a delete.","HOW WE WILL KNOW: the same probe that convicted this is still running, so the next log says plainly whether the number came down. If deletes or the list still drag, that log is the evidence and I want it rather than another theory."],t.s)
B.aqc=new A.t(836,B.Xy)
B.X2=s(['THE FOLDER YOU ARE IN OWNS THE ITEM - reverting yesterday\'s v834 choice, on your word: "when I am in a folder and use the dictate box to enter text and hit send it should for sure create that item in the folder I was in." v834 stopped an item written inside Queue or Processed from being filed there, because being in the Queue folder is the ONLY record that you approved something - so writing a note there silently marked it approved. I was right about the approval and wrong about the placement, and you watched your items vanish out of the view you were typing in. Placement wins: write in a folder, the item is in that folder, Queue included.','AND THE APPROVAL GATE STILL HOLDS, because it no longer has to guess. Tapping Implement already writes a real timestamped record on the item; it just was not what decided anything. Now it is. Your items stay where you put them, and I pick my next job by asking "did he tap Implement?" instead of "is it sitting in the Queue folder?" - so an item you drop in the Queue to get it out of your head can never again jump the line above work you actually approved.',"A PROBE FOR THE DISAPPEARING-ITEM BUG - the other half of this build, and it changes no behavior. It adds ONE number to the diagnostic log, and that number is the whole point: how long the app waits to get a turn at the database, measured right beside how long the read itself takes.","WHY IT MATTERS: the list rebuild has been logging reads of 16 to 452 SECONDS, and until now those two things were indistinguishable in a single number - waiting versus working. I already proved the database itself is innocent (the identical read finishes in a tenth of a second outside the app) and that getting a turn is instant when the app is quiet. What I still need is that same measurement at the moment it goes WRONG. If waiting is also slow then, the cause is proven outright, because a do-nothing query cannot be slow for any other reason.","SO IF IT STARTS EATING ITEMS AGAIN, please do NOT restart the app - that is exactly when the log is worth the most, and I can pull it straight off the machine. Your words were never at risk in any of this: every item you reported was on the server, correct and undeleted. The bug is in showing them to you, not in keeping them."],t.s)
B.avm=new A.t(835,B.X2)
B.a18=s(['WRITING IN THE QUEUE FOLDER NO LONGER PUTS THE ITEM IN THE QUEUE - your question ("If I enter a new item without hitting implement why is that item showing that it is #1 in the queue?") and your priority bug were the same bug, and I caught it live in your own data this morning: your three test items went straight to the TOP of the queue, above real work, with no Implement tap anywhere in their threads.',"WHAT WAS HAPPENING: two rules that are each right on their own were fighting. A folder you are working in files what you write there - that is how folder dictation is supposed to work. And being in the Queue folder IS the approval; there is no separate flag. So typing a thought while standing in the Queue quietly granted it an approval you never gave, and it jumped the Implement gate that exists to stop exactly that.","THE RULE NOW: Queue, Processed, In Progress and Done are STAGES, not inboxes - you cannot approve something by standing next to the approved pile, and you certainly cannot write something that is born finished. An item you create in any of those files one level up into the Claude folder instead: still a Claude item, still sent, still right where you were working, and it waits for your Implement tap like everything else. Every other folder is untouched, including Brainstorm - writing an idea while sitting in Brainstorm still parks it in Brainstorm, because that one is you filing, not the app inventing a status."],t.s)
B.arn=new A.t(834,B.a18)
B.a80=s(["TOOLTIPS ON THE WINDOWS CONTROLS - your move-forward order, first pass. Hover any icon-only control for a beat and it names its click: the pin (Pin to top / Unpin), the item number (Copy item ID), the fold chevron, the trash can (Move to Trash), the headphones (Add to / Remove from listening list), the type badge (Change item type), the back-to-top arrow, and the folder pane arrows and plus. The rule applied everywhere: a control that already says its name in text gets NO tooltip - repeating it would be noise.","AND THE NEW-ANSWER MARK SHOWS IT NAVIGATES - your follow-up ask. On Windows the cursor becomes the pointing hand over it and a hover tooltip reads Open the full item, so the single-click behavior you asked for in v821 now announces itself. All of this is desktop-only: the phone has no hover, and nothing about any tap changed."],t.s)
B.auD=new A.t(833,B.a80)
B.a0i=s(["A POSTED ITEM CAN NO LONGER VANISH FROM THE LIST - your count-goes-up-but-no-item report, caught live in your own log. The list rebuild re-reads your whole library (4,494 items), and under heavy load today those rebuilds ran NINE TO EIGHTY SECONDS - so a rebuild that started before you hit send finished after it, and painted the list from a snapshot that predates your item. The count uses a quick separate query, which is why it knew better. The fix: every just-posted item now rides a protection window - a stale rebuild GRAFTS it back in instead of wiping it, the same shield deletes have had since v546, pointed the other way.","THE FEEL THIS BUYS: send an item, it appears below the box and STAYS, no matter what the rebuild machinery is doing behind you. Deletes were already instant to the eye; now posts are too. The rebuild itself still runs behind as the checker - its cost on a loaded machine is real and is its own project - but it can never again be the reason your words are not on screen."],t.s)
B.avF=new A.t(832,B.a0i)
B.a3C=s(["THE ID NUMBER SHOWS ITS COPY BUTTON ON HOVER - your approved Windows ask. Mouse over any item number and a small copy glyph fades in beside it with the pointing-hand cursor; one click copies the number, same confirmation as before. The glyph holds its space invisibly, so the bar never shifts under your cursor. On the phone nothing changes - tap-to-copy stands."],t.s)
B.avV=new A.t(831,B.a3C)
B.a2w=s(['SAYING "NEW NOTE" NOW VISIBLY SWITCHES THE BOX - your report an hour ago: it flashed but the type never changed, and back-to-back thoughts read as one item. You had found the last hole in the type system: the spoken new-note command carried no type signal at all, so the fresh box just kept whatever the folder or memory said. It now carries one - the exact machinery "new task" got in v828 - so "new note", "next note", and even the drive-time mishearing "no notes" all flip the box to a plain note the moment they land, in any folder, over any previous type.','THE DELIBERATE EDGES: "resume dictation" does NOT retype - resuming means continue as before, so it only re-arms the box you had. "New note in cash flow" both retypes AND files to the folder. And a "new task" or "message for Claude" spoken in the same breath still wins over the note switch - the more specific command always takes the box.','YOUR TEST: dictate a thought, say "new note", dictate another - two separate note items, visibly typed as notes while you talk. Then the sequence from your report: "new note", "new claude", "new task" - the badge should follow you through all three now, out loud or by button.'],t.s)
B.apR=new A.t(830,B.a2w)
B.a2h=s(["PICKING A TYPE NOW WINS EVERYWHERE - your report from the Claude Inbox: clicked New Note and the item would not switch from a Claude item to a note. You found a real hole: your pick had a home the folder rule could not see, so inside any Claude-side folder the folder always outranked you. The ladder is now explicit, in one place, in this order: YOUR PICK first, then a spoken Claude address, then the folder default, then the remembered type. A pick beats every default on every surface - the badge, the typed save, and the spoken close all read the same answer.","TWO SIBLINGS FIXED IN THE SAME SWEEP, because they were the same disease: picking Task or Meeting on a Claude-addressed box now clears the address (it used to stand, and the box could still save as Claude), and a type picked AFTER you tap Off can no longer retype the item that is still finishing in the background - the closing item keeps the type its box showed at the moment you stopped, and your new pick waits for the next box."],t.s)
B.ap8=new A.t(829,B.a2h)
B.ZR=s(['SAYING "NEW TASK" NOW WORKS EXACTLY LIKE EVERY OTHER ITEM - your Big Bug order, built. The double cursor and the text-landing-in-the-dictation-box both came from one root: "new task" ran through a second capture machine that predates the composer, so one sentence could drive TWO boxes at once. No more. Spoken "new task" now does precisely what tapping Task does - the box you are watching becomes the task, one box, one caret, and your next words land in it.','THE SPOKEN "CREATE TASK ... END TASK" COMMAND IS UNTOUCHED - that flow (which writes your OneNote checkbox page) is the capture machine\'s whole point, and it keeps it. What changed is only "new task" and "next task", which now retype instead of capturing. One guard to know: "that next task" and friends stay plain words - a leading the/that/my blocks the command, because stealing words is worse than missing a command.','YOUR VERIFY, the exact recipe from your report: say "new note", then "new claude", then "new task". The box should follow you through all three, never vanish, and exactly ONE cursor should blink - in the box your words are landing in. Seven tests pin the new routing; if the double cursor survives this build, the log now records a task_switch line at the exact moment, and the next fix comes from that evidence.'],t.s)
B.ape=new A.t(828,B.ZR)
B.a1g=s(['THE RECORDING NOTIFICATION NOW COUNTS - your approved alert. While dictation runs, the persistent notification reads "Voicley - Recording - 47 min" and keeps counting, so a mic you forgot about announces its own age instead of sitting there looking the same as one you started a moment ago. Tap it to come straight back to the app. It updates once a minute on purpose: a per-second rewrite of a system notification spends battery on a number nobody reads that closely.',"ON THE ICON BADGE you also asked about: the honest answer is that badges are the LAUNCHER's decision, not the app's - Samsung honours them but they can be switched off per-app, so a badge can never be the only signal. The counting notification works regardless, which is why it went first. Pairs with the five-minute auto-off from earlier today: the notification makes a forgotten mic visible, the auto-off makes it stop."],t.s)
B.avn=new A.t(827,B.a1g)
B.Zj=s(["THE FULL-SIZE CLAUDE ITEM NOW CARRIES EVERYTHING THE BOX DOES - your report on 27060: the box was showing controls the full view did not have. It was right, and backwards: the screen you OPEN to work an item had fewer buttons than the one you only glance at. Implement, Processed, Complete, Edited and Send-now now render in the full-size item as well - from ONE shared definition, so the two surfaces cannot drift apart again. Combined with the grid that landed in v822, the full view now has the ticket strip, the stage grid, every action button, the whole conversation and the reply box."],t.s)
B.apE=new A.t(826,B.Zj)
B.a3_=s(["JUMP BACK TO THE TOP - your approved arrow. Scroll a folder or the inbox down a couple of screens and a small round up-arrow fades in at the bottom right; tap it and the list glides back to the top. It fades away again near the top so it never sits over your items when it has no job. It drives the list through the same public scroll call everything else uses - the drag and glide machinery is untouched.",'THE ONENOTE ICONS ARE GONE - your ask: "Let\'s get rid of the Onenote icons anywhere on the UI." The little book glyph that sat on every OneNote-linked item is retired. It followed the master OneNote off-switch you asked for: with syncing optional, a permanent OneNote button on every item was chrome for a road you mostly do not take. Opening a page still lives in the item menu for the times you do.'],t.s)
B.apn=new A.t(825,B.a3_)
B.Xv=s(['THE PROGRESS GRID GETS THE FULL WIDTH - your approved fix for "Still can\'t really see the progress grid on mobile." The grid and the action buttons stopped sharing one row: the grid now spans the box edge to edge, and Implement, Processed, Complete and the sent-for-implementation controls sit on their own line underneath, wrapping to a second line on a narrow box instead of squeezing anything. Same information, room to breathe.'],t.s)
B.apB=new A.t(824,B.Xv)
B.a3l=s(['THE ID NUMBER IS BIGGER AND COPIES ITSELF - both of your asks in one: the item number on every box is up a size, and TAPPING it copies it to the clipboard with a little "#12345 copied" confirmation. One deliberate choice to know about: you asked for selectable-for-copying, and this is tap-to-copy instead - the number sits on the bar that is also the drag handle, and a text-selection gesture there would fight the drag you use constantly. Tap-to-copy gets you the same thing in fewer moves; if you specifically want press-and-select anyway, say so and I will find it a safer home first.'],t.s)
B.au9=new A.t(823,B.a3l)
B.a2B=s(['THE GRID IS IN THE FULL VIEW - your approved one-liner: "Include the grid in the fullsized items." A Claude item opened full-size now shows the same stage grid its box wears - Sent, Received, Queue position, Building, Processed, Done, each with its stamp - right under the ticket strip. Same single source of truth as the box, so the two can never tell you different stories about one item. Brainstorm-parked ideas draw no grid in either place, as designed.'],t.s)
B.aum=new A.t(822,B.a2B)
B.a19=s(['THE NEW-ANSWER MARK IS A DOOR NOW - your approved one-liner: tap the clay "New answer" dot on any Claude box and the item opens full-size in a single click, straight to the conversation where the answer lives. The tap is guarded so it only opens the item - it can never fold the box underneath by accident. Opening it marks the answer read, exactly as before.'],t.s)
B.avM=new A.t(821,B.a19)
B.a4p=s(["FOUR MORE SCREENS GET THE TRASH TREATMENT - your Build it on the sweep. Files, Recordings, Projects and Project detail all carried the exact flaw Trash had: the entire list built at once and laid out again on every frame of a scroll. Files holds your WHOLE library, so it was the one that would have hurt first as the library grows. All four now build only the rows on screen, through one shared lazy list that keeps the identical look - same card, same corners, same hairlines - and keys rows by item so a deletion mid-list can never leave the row below wearing the wrong state."],t.s)
B.atj=new A.t(820,B.a4p)
B.a7x=s(['ENTER SENDS YOUR REPLY TO CLAUDE NOW - your report: "In full sized view the reply still doesn\'t send when I hit enter." Found and fixed, and it was my miss: when v808 taught Enter to send in the full-size view, it taught the NOTE BODY editor - the Reply-to-Claude field is a separate multi-line field, and on a multi-line field Enter quietly inserts a newline instead of firing send. The send-on-enter wiring on it was dead code from day one. Bare Enter now sends the reply; Shift+Enter still gives you a new line. Same fix everywhere the reply field appears - the full view and the conversation in the box share one widget, so they cannot drift apart.'],t.s)
B.api=new A.t(819,B.a7x)
B.a56=s(["SEARCH INSIDE THE CLAUDE AREA - your approved feature, with the both-scopes answer you gave this morning. A search box now sits at the top of every Claude folder with a toggle beside it: THIS FOLDER filters exactly the view you are standing in as you type, ALL CLAUDE searches every Claude item everywhere - Queue, Processed, Done, Brainstorm, and Claude items in your own folders. It opens on This-folder because that matches what your eyes are on; widening is one tap.",'THE PART THAT MAKES IT ACTUALLY FIND PAST BUGS: both scopes search MY ANSWERS on each item, not just your words. The version numbers and fix histories live in the answers - search "v814" and the Trash-scrolling item comes up even though you never said v814 yourself. Tapping a result opens the full item, where the conversation and Reply already live - so "when I do find a past bug I could easily add to it" works today: open it, speak, it re-queues. The whole-library search from v622 is untouched.'],t.s)
B.asP=new A.t(818,B.a56)
B.a0F=s(['THE APP NOW TELLS YOU WHEN THE CONNECTION IS COSTING YOU - your approved indicator. An amber line appears above the dictate box the moment network trouble starts biting, and it names the COST rather than the internet: "Bad connection - recording, not transcribing yet" when the live engine cannot reach its server (your words are still being captured, and the wording says the safe part first on purpose), or "Bad connection - saved here, not synced yet" when your words cannot leave the device. It appears only while you are recording, and it clears itself once the trouble has been quiet for twenty seconds - no nagging about a blip that already healed.',"Why this earns its place: the night you filed it, your Fold sat with sync 38,000 characters behind and 353 voice commands dropped - and nothing on screen said a word. Built without touching the live engine at all: the engine already logs every stall, drop and DNS failure to the network diagnostics, and the notice simply reads that log. Upload failures stay off it deliberately - those already have their own retry button on the item."],t.s)
B.auH=new A.t(817,B.a0F)
B.a1i=s(['THE MIC NOW SHUTS ITSELF OFF AFTER FIVE QUIET MINUTES - AND ALWAYS SAYS SO. Your approved timer, with your rule built in: "it starts its timer after the last time" you did anything. The old silence clock only counted from your last spoken words, so typing into the box or working the app with the mic open could read as silence and trip the timer mid-use. Both silence clocks now restart on ANY activity - words landing, typing, tapping. When five genuinely quiet minutes pass, the mic stops fully and the dictate box says "Mic stopped after 5 quiet minutes" right where you look while dictating. Never silent, never mid-thought.',"This is the companion to the one-button control: off, listening, and a stop that announces itself. The 90-second soft pause (which parks capture but keeps the mic warm) still exists underneath and now obeys the same any-activity rule. Meetings and tasks are exempt from BOTH - deliberate long-form recording never gets cut. The five minutes is fixed in this build; making it settable in Settings is the named next step."],t.s)
B.ax0=new A.t(816,B.a1i)
B.a0m=s(['DRAG TO THE EDGE AND THE LIST SCROLLS - your report: "I\'m unable to drag up to the top or to the bottom to move the list to place the item that I wanna move in a spot that is not visible." Found and fixed, and the cause was my own earlier fix. When you reported the OPPOSITE problem months ago (holding still near the bottom made the list scroll on its own), I made the scroll zones ignore a finger until it had wiggled 24 pixels inside them. That refused the exact gesture the zones exist for: drag to the edge and HOLD. You arrived, you held still, and it sat there forever.',"THE RULE NOW TELLS THE TWO APART BY TIMING. A finger that is already at the edge the instant a drag starts is the old accident - it still has to move a little before anything scrolls, so your press-and-hold complaint stays fixed. A finger that ARRIVES at the edge later in the drag travelled there on purpose - it scrolls the moment it arrives and keeps scrolling while you hold. Both of your complaints - the old one and this one - are pinned by tests now, so neither can quietly come back while fixing the other. That is exactly how this bug happened the first time."],t.s)
B.auJ=new A.t(815,B.a0m)
B.a1w=s(['TRASH SCROLLS PROPERLY NOW - your report: "Trash is super slow in scrolling." The Trash screen was building EVERY deleted item at once, whether or not it was on screen, and then laying all of them out again on every single frame of a scroll. With a 30-day retention that is hundreds of full item boxes, each one wrapped in a swipe handler. It now builds only what you can actually see, which is what every other fast list in the app already does.',"While I was in there I checked for the same mistake elsewhere and found it in four more places - the Files list, Recordings, Projects and Project detail all build their whole list eagerly too. Those hold your entire library, so they are the same slowness waiting to happen. I did NOT change them in this build: you reported Trash, that is what this fixes, and touching four more lists is its own version with its own testing. They are written down and queued."],t.s)
B.au6=new A.t(814,B.a1w)
B.a4o=s(['ONE BUTTON - your approved redesign, and the biggest change in a while. The three-segment control is gone. In its place: one button that reads DICTATE in grey when your words are not being captured, and LISTENING in red - breathing with your voice - when they are. You picked the colour ("implement with your color recommendations"), so red it is, matching the recording border you already see. The green retires with the segment it belonged to, which leaves ONE colour in the app meaning "your voice is being captured" instead of two.','COMMANDS-ONLY IS GONE AS A MODE, and that is the real win. It let the microphone stay live while your words were NOT becoming text, and next to Off it was nearly impossible to tell apart. It is the state behind the eaten words, the burst dropped after a Done, and the parked dictation box - your Fold\'s log carried 353 dropped commands in a single day. You lose nothing: "new note", "create task" and "message for Claude" have always worked mid-sentence, so nothing ever needed to BE in that mode. Its only unique power was the failure.','WHAT YOU GIVE UP, said plainly: the deliberate "stop taking my words but keep the mic warm" pause. It is now off, then on - one tap of the button you kept. And if some other path ever parks a session in the old commands state, the button reads GREY, because grey means "your words are not becoming text" and that is the truth there - one tap resolves it instead of stranding you.','NEW STAYS for now, deliberately. It is the only one-tap "close this, start fresh"; you already have that spoken and as the check on the box, so it is a duplicate you can retire on purpose rather than discover missing.',"NOT IN THIS BUILD: the five-minute auto-off after real silence. It is designed and it is next - I would rather ship the button you can see and test than bundle a timer that turns your microphone off with it, on the same night."],t.s)
B.awX=new A.t(813,B.a4o)
B.a61=s(['THE PIN IS IN - your approved feature, built as you described it: a pin sits just to the LEFT OF THE DATE on every item. Click it and the item rides to the top of the list; click it again and it comes back down. It is a property of the ITEM, not of one screen, so a pinned item is at the top in the main window and stays at the top in every folder it lives in - your "system wide feature".',"YOU HANDED ME THE TWO OPEN QUESTIONS, so here are the answers I picked. UNPINNING RETURNS THE ITEM TO ITS NATURAL PLACE rather than dropping it just under the pinned block. Your version would have left ex-pinned items stranded near the top in no order, and after a dozen of those the top of the list becomes a graveyard and the pinned block stops being the thing your eye trusts. A pin is a temporary lift; letting go should put the item down where it belongs.","AND ON ORDERING: pinned items sort among themselves by the exact same order you already drag, so arranging inside the pinned block works with the machinery you know - no second system, and it is also WHY unpinning can put an item back where it was (its place was never overwritten to lift it).","STILL TO COME on this feature, so you are not surprised: dragging an item DOWN OUT of the pinned block to unpin it is not in this build - use the pin itself for now. That one reaches into the drag machinery next to the scrolling you asked me not to disturb, so it gets its own careful pass. Also: pinning syncs between your devices only after I run the small database change (017) on the server - until then the pin works perfectly on the device you press it on."],t.s)
B.ax1=new A.t(812,B.a61)
B.Xm=s(['THE CLAUDE STATUS LADDER FITS A PHONE NOW - your report "Claude items with the new table are not rendering correctly", and then the part that actually solved it: "On Mobile the table doesn\'t have enough room." Twice before (v774, v787) I tried to make the label, the badge and the time share ONE line and both times only bought a little room. The honest answer is that on a phone - and worse in a folder, where the box is narrower still - that line cannot hold all three. So each rung now STOPS competing: below phone width the time drops to its own line underneath and the label gets the full width instead of a leftover sliver. Wide screens are untouched. Twelve tests render the worst case ("SENT FOR IMPLEMENTATION" with a "#12 of 16" badge and a timestamp) at seven widths from 200px up and fail if anything overflows, if the label gets cut, or if the time or badge goes missing.'],t.s)
B.avB=new A.t(811,B.Xm)
B.Xb=s(['THE QUEUE NUMBERS COUNT REAL WORK NOW - your catch: "the 2nd item in the que list is show #2 of 22 and the item above it hasn\'t been implemented yet... We should instead completely skip the non implemented items when determine the que positions." You were reading it exactly right. A Brainstorm idea parked in the list, or an item already Processed or Done that still carried an old Queue row, was silently holding a position and inflating the total - so real work read one number lower than it was and the "of N" was never the number of things actually waiting. Positions and the total now count PENDING WORK only. Nothing is stored to make that true: it is read from which folders an item sits in, so there is no second copy to drift.','THE CLAUDE AND NOTE BARS ARE MORE SHADED - your ask: "The Claude top bar needs to be a bit more shaded, the notes bar also but not as much as the claude bar." Both are up from the same faint wash every type shared; Claude reads heaviest, notes a step under it. Task and Meeting are deliberately untouched - you did not ask for them, and a change you did not ask for is one you have to re-review. Say lighter or darker and it is one number each.'],t.s)
B.apk=new A.t(810,B.Xb)
B.Y1=s(['ENTER SENDS, SHIFT+ENTER MAKES A NEW LINE - your instruction: "Inside of a full size view make enter send the item and shift enter give it a new line." In the full-sized view, Enter now saves the item and closes it; Shift+Enter drops to a new line exactly as before. Dictation is untouched - spoken "new line" and "new paragraph" still work everywhere; this only changes what the physical Enter key does in that one view.',"And an honest note on the Windows drag-down-then-sideways you approved: it is NOT in this build. Reading the code showed it is not a new gesture but a hand-off in the middle of one, which Windows/Flutter only allows one owner for - the right fix reaches into the drag machinery next to the scrolling you asked me not to disturb, and it can only be proven by feel on your desktop. It stays in your Queue, unbuilt and honest, with its own session ahead of it."],t.s)
B.arf=new A.t(808,B.Y1)
B.a6W=s(['YOUR TYPED WORDS SURVIVE TASK CLOSES NOW - the real bug behind your Fold recipe ("dictate create new task, enter text, dictate create new task"). Reading the code with your recipe in hand found something worse than a hidden box: closing a task you had TYPED into overwrote it with what the microphone alone had heard - in the box, on the tasks page, and in the saved item. Every close now keeps your typed words and adds the final spoken ones, the same merge the live box uses. Four tests walk your exact recipe step by step. (The vanished-box half still has the v806 tripwire watching - if it recurs, the log names the holder.)',"THE FULL VIEW'S QUEUE NUMBER NOW MATCHES THE BOX - your report: \"The fullsized window Queue #'s are off.\" The full view computed its number with its own COPY of the counting logic, and that copy was missing the v781 fix (memberships pointing at deleted items still counted there). Same cure as the type bug: the second copy is deleted, both views read one derivation, disagreement is now impossible.","ARROW KEYS PAGE BETWEEN ITEMS IN FULL VIEW - your pick #1, approved: Left/Right (and PgUp/PgDn) move to the previous/next item, the keyboard twin of the swipe. Typing in a text field keeps its arrow keys - the caret wins, paging gets what's left.",'ITEM NUMBERS ARE DARKER ("Make the item #\'s a bit darker they are hard to read") - up from the faintest grey to label strength, still quieter than your words. AND THE NEW-ANSWER DOT CLEARS INSTANTLY when you open an item ("can you make it faster") - it listens for the mark now instead of waiting for the row\'s next repaint.'],t.s)
B.axl=new A.t(807,B.a6W)
B.X1=s(['ONENOTE HAS A MASTER OFF-SWITCH - your ask on the OneNote item: "Not as excited about Using the OneNote functionality that this was originally built around... Is there a way to turn it off?" Settings now has "Sync to OneNote". Flip it off and Voicley writes NOTHING to OneNote - no live streaming, no task-page writes - while everything in the app and the sync between your devices stays exactly as it is. It ships ON (nothing changes until you flip it), and flipping it also clears the "Dictate 2" remnant: that line in the full view was your active project\'s old OneNote page label, and with the switch off the destination reads "This device".','AND A TRIPWIRE FOR THE VANISHING DICTATE BOX - your bug: "create a new task item, then create another task item behind it, the dictation box goes away." I could not reproduce it from the spoken path (that one has tests), so this build adds the honest next step: whenever the dictate box hides, the log now records exactly WHICH open item is holding it. If it happens again on this build, the log names the culprit and the fix comes from evidence instead of guessing. The law stands: the dictate box always exists.'],t.s)
B.auR=new A.t(806,B.X1)
B.a6A=s(['THE WINDOWS TITLE BAR NOW TELLS YOU WHAT YOU ARE RUNNING - your ask, approved tonight: "Let\'s put the version in the windows title bar and another message if there is an update available and the version of the update." It reads "Voicley v805", and the moment a newer build lands on the shelf it becomes "Voicley v805 - v806 available". It re-stamps on launch, every 10 minutes, and every time you click back into the window. Born the hard way: your desktop ran v782 ALL DAY today and nothing on screen said so.','CLAUDE FOLDERS DEFAULT THE TYPE SELECTOR TO CLAUDE - your ask ("If I am in a claude folder you should default the item type of claude"), then your nudge when the selector still said Note: "This isn\'t happening." The truth: the SAVE half has existed since v727 - items created in Claude folders already became Claude items - but the selector kept showing Note, the exact badge-vs-outcome split v755 was about, alive in one more branch. Now the selector, the badge and the save all read the folder: composing in Claude, Queue, Questions, Processed or Brainstorm shows Claude up front. Your manual pick still beats it, always.','THE WORD "BRAINSTORM" NOW FILES FOR YOU - type it at the start of a title, or lead a new item with it, and the item LINKS itself into the Brainstorm folder. LINKS, per your rule from tonight: it also stays exactly where it already was - one item, two doors. Strict on purpose: only the word "brainstorm" leading - "brainstorming ideas" or a sentence about brainstorming does nothing, because a false link that hides an item\'s real home is worse than dragging one by hand.',"AND THE STRAIGHT STORY ON TONIGHT'S OUTAGE, for the record: your data was never gone. Two items I created from my desk this afternoon were missing one internal field, and the app's Claude engine crashed every time it read them - on every machine that synced them. That is why items sat at \"Sending...\" (they actually SENT; only the receipt back to your screen died), and why the desktop looked empty after its update. I repaired the two rows on the server, every device healed itself on its next sync, and my desk tools now refuse to create incomplete items. The full post-mortem is in the regression ledger."],t.s)
B.aqu=new A.t(805,B.a6A)
B.a2C=s(['THE BRAINSTORM FOLDER IS LIVE - built as discussed on the thread and approved. A new Brainstorm folder sits under Claude on every device: "a topical per item area for new ideas that I am not ready to put into the queue and process." Drop any Claude item in and it steps OUTSIDE the pipeline - never auto-filed into Queue, Questions or Processed, even if you press Implement, even if the conversation reads done. Nothing is stored on the item itself: being in the folder IS the state, so there is no second copy to disagree - the disease behind six of this week\'s bugs.','A parked idea\'s box wears a quiet indigo BRAINSTORM chip instead of the status ladder - an idea has no pipeline to climb. The conversation still works exactly as everywhere else: replies flow, my answers land, the new-answer mark still lights. When an idea is ready, drag it out of the folder - your move IS the graduation, and the ladder comes straight back. Two of your calls defaulted the cautious way for now: creation is by drag or my desk command only (no spoken "brainstorm note" yet), and Brainstorm answers light only the per-item mark, not the Queue badge.','A "PROCESSED" BUTTON ON QUEUED ITEMS - you asked "Should we put a button labeled processed in items that are in the que?" three minutes after having to ask me in words to move one. Approved and built: any Claude item sitting in the Queue now has a green Processed button beside Implement. One tap makes the move the pipeline makes when work finishes - on your say-so, whatever the thread thinks. Your tap counts as YOUR placement, so the sweep can never drag the item back. It shows on all queued items deliberately: calling something finished should not require the conversation\'s permission.',"ALSO IN THIS BUILD: answers from my desk now land on your items within seconds (the fast lane you tested this evening on item #9850) - that was server-side, no update needed, noted here for the record."],t.s)
B.auV=new A.t(804,B.a2C)
B.Ym=s(['THE CLAUDE BOX HEADER READS THE WAY YOU ASKED: the Claude label, then the New-answer mark, then the item number. You: "Lets put your new answer notification to the right of the claude label and the new item number to the right of that." The mark MOVED up from the footer - one place, not two.','THE EDITED-RESEND BUTTON STOPS LIGHTING ON PUNCTUATION IT ADDED ITSELF. You, item #9846: "The edited - resend button just showed up right after hitting send." Your data showed exactly one character of difference: the app\'s own polish step added a period to the stored item, and the comparison counted that as your edit. Now only WORDS decide - case, punctuation and line breaks are cosmetic. Change a word and the button still appears; the app tidying itself no longer does. Existing false-flagged items, including #9846, heal themselves the next time their box draws.','AND WORTH KNOWING: your Windows app has been running v782 all day, declining its update prompt - none of today\'s fixes are on it, which is also why the "Review Later" folder felt slow there. Say yes to the prompt (or restart the app) and it catches up to this build.'],t.s)
B.awo=new A.t(803,B.Ym)
B.a0g=s(["THE LIST REBUILD STORM IS CONTAINED - this is the fix for item #9837, the focus jump, and the scrolling feeling broken again. Your log this afternoon showed the app rebuilding its entire list of 4,365 items over and over, each pass taking about a second, with worst frames of a second and a half. When you tapped an item, the list was being rebuilt underneath your finger between the touch and the tap resolving - so focus resolved against a list that had moved. That is also exactly the mechanism the scroll work fought: content moving mid-gesture.","The storm had a trigger - the item-number change I ran at lunch, syncing through all your rows - but the pile-up was always possible: nothing stopped a second full rebuild starting while the first was still running. Your log showed 43 of them stacked in one stretch.","Now at most ONE rebuild runs at a time, and however many requests arrive while it runs, they collapse into exactly one follow-up. COALESCED, NEVER DROPPED - that distinction is the whole design. Dropping requests would mean your list silently stops refreshing, which is the items-vanishing failure and far worse than churn. The follow-up reads the same database a moment later, so the newest data always lands. Eleven tests pin it, including the one that matters most: a request is never lost.","Being straight about the boundary: this contains the storm, it does not cure the disease. The app still re-reads your whole library for a single changed row - roughly a second per pass at your size, growing as your library grows. That is the real root of the scroll arc, it is a bigger piece of work, and I will walk you through it before starting rather than diving in."],t.s)
B.aqS=new A.t(802,B.a0g)
B.a1J=s(['ITEM #5789 - THE SECOND COPY IS DELETED, WHICH IS WHAT "PERMANENTLY" ACTUALLY MEANS. You: "I am entering as a Claude item, and you are creating a note. This is a bug that has repeated itself over and over and over again, and you need to get it fixed permanently."','Every fix in this arc kept two copies of one fact in step. "Is this box addressed to Claude?" was stored in two places, and different code wrote each one: the type dropdown wrote one copy, and the code that decides what to SAVE read the other. So picking Claude while the microphone was running set a flag the save never looked at.',"It has been producing the right item since yesterday - but only because I taught the save to fall back on what the badge is showing. A fallback covering a broken primary signal is exactly how this kept coming back: the real answer stayed wrong, and the next change to the fallback exposed it again.",'So the second copy is gone, not synchronised. There is now one place that holds this, everything reads it, and two copies cannot disagree when there is only one. Same treatment the "already posted" guard got in the last build. 1,188 tests green, including the truth table that holds every past attempt at this bug together by name.'],t.s)
B.aqv=new A.t(801,B.a1J)
B.a1Q=s(['UPDATING THE APP NO LONGER LEAVES OLD TEXT IN THE DICTATION BOX. You: "When I updated the app it again put text from a previous item inside of the dictation box."',"There is a safety net that mirrors the open dictation box to disk, so a crash can never lose typed words - they are the only words in the app with no audio behind them. It is supposed to forget that text the moment the words become an item. It did that properly for TYPED notes and not for DICTATED ones, and dictating is nearly everything you do.",'Two things went wrong together. The "forget it" write was delayed by about three-quarters of a second to avoid hammering storage while you talk - and an app update kills the app, which is exactly the moment a delayed write gets lost. The backup guard that would have caught it was only ever kept in memory on the dictated path, so it died with the app too. Next launch, your last dictation came back looking like something still open.',"Now the safety net itself knows what has already become an item and refuses to hand it back - so even if a clear is missed, your words cannot reappear. Both halves happen in one place, so a future path cannot get one and forget the other, which is exactly how this happened. Ten tests, including the ones that matter in the other direction: a genuinely unfinished note still comes back, and anything you added after posting still comes back. Losing real words would be far worse than showing you a duplicate."],t.s)
B.asu=new A.t(800,B.a1Q)
B.a1b=s(['THE TYPE YOU CHOOSE STICKS - INCLUDING WHILE YOU ARE DICTATING. You: "I changed it to note, I speak into it, and it changes it to a Claude item. Now I can\'t even get it to change to stay as a note." That is the one that mattered, and this is the cause.','When a Claude item posted, the app wrote "the next box is a Claude box" directly - overruling whatever you had chosen. The bit that makes this ugly: one line above it sat a careful version of the same write, added in an earlier fix specifically so an observation could never overwrite your pick. Two writes doing the same job, side by side, one guarded and one not. The unguarded one won, and it fired whenever a Claude item finished posting - which is why the box could flip WHILE you were talking into it.','The unguarded writes are gone. Nothing you asked for is lost: rapid-firing Claude items still keeps the box on Claude, because that now rides on your PICK rather than on the app watching itself. Only an explicit choice can set the type now - your pick, a spoken "message for Claude", or a folder. The app can fill it in when you have not chosen; it can never overrule you.',"And the whole thing is pinned as one truth table. Every attempt at this bug - five of them, including one of mine from an hour ago - is now a named test, and they all have to pass together. A sixth attempt cannot quietly undo a fifth, which is what kept happening."],t.s)
B.as2=new A.t(799,B.a1b)
B.a__=s(['PICKING NOTE WORKS AGAIN - AND THIS ONE WAS MY FAULT, IN THE BUILD YOU JUST INSTALLED. You: "I changed it to note, I speak into it, and it changes it to a Claude item. Now I can\'t even get it to change to stay as a note." Yesterday\'s fix made the box save whatever type it was SHOWING. Correct on its own. But I folded that inferred type into the same switch that handles a spoken "message for Claude" - and that switch is deliberately the one thing that outranks your pick. Since the box infers Claude whenever your last item was one, which for you is nearly always, choosing Note became impossible.',"That is exactly the mistake I had described in writing an hour earlier: one value carrying two meanings. An explicit address and an inferred default are different facts and now live in different places, with the default sitting BELOW your pick where you can override it. Five new tests pin it, including one that sweeps every pick against every inferred type and asserts your pick always wins - and one that checks yesterday's fix still holds, so neither can quietly undo the other.",'NEW APPROVED WORK NOW LANDS AT THE TOP OF THE QUEUE. You: "When I create a new Claude item from the inbox, and hit the implement button. It is putting it in the queue, but at the bottom of the list. Seems like it should go on top of the list." You are right. Pressing Implement is you saying "do this" about the thing in front of you, and burying it under everything approved earlier is the app arguing with that. Your drag order still wins - this only decides where a new arrival starts.'],t.s)
B.apD=new A.t(798,B.a__)
B.ZM=s(['REMOVED, at your word: tapping the "Folders" heading no longer opens the reorder screen. You: "When I am in a Folder item and hit the arrow key on the top left of the app, it is bringing me to a folders view and it shouldn\'t. We should remove that."',"The heading looked like a label, so tapping it was usually an accident - and the cost only turned up later. That screen stayed on the stack, so opening a folder afterwards made the BACK arrow return you to it instead of home. You were landing somewhere you never chose to go.","The \u21c5 button right next to the heading is the deliberate way to reorder folders, and it is unchanged - as is the long-press action. Dragging a folder onto the heading still moves it to the top level; only the tap is gone."],t.s)
B.as5=new A.t(797,B.ZM)
B.a1T=s(['EVERY ITEM NOW HAS A NUMBER YOU CAN SAY. You: "add an item # to each item for ID purposes, that way when I have an issue with an item I can give you the id #." It is live. Your whole library is numbered oldest-first: #1 is your very first item from Jul 6, and the newest is #5795. It shows right of the item type label on the box - the spot the timer used to occupy - and in the full-sized view under the title, exactly where you asked.','From now on "item 4,812 is doing the thing" is all I need. Today every report meant working out which item you meant from a time or a title, and you have four items with near-identical titles right now - twice this week I chased the wrong one.','The numbers come from the server, deliberately. You create items on four devices and sometimes offline; if each device picked "the next number" two of them out of contact would both mint 413, and a duplicate is worse than no number at all - you would say 413 and I would fix the wrong item. So an item made offline shows no number until it syncs, then gets one permanently. It never changes afterwards, and a deleted item keeps its number rather than handing it to someone else.'],t.s)
B.ap6=new A.t(796,B.a1T)
B.a6f=s(["FOLDERS NOW SHOW THEIR ITEM COUNT TOO. You: \"Let's put the folder item count in between the dictation box and the items below the dictation box like we are doing for the inbox view.\" It is the same piece of the screen the Inbox uses, handed the folder's own count - so the line between the composer and the history reads identically wherever you are.",'REMOVED, at your word: the rule to the right of the item count. You: "In the inbox view let\'s get rid of the line to the right of the item count in between the dictate box and the items below." The count on its own marks that boundary perfectly well; the line running to the right edge was furniture.'],t.s)
B.aqW=new A.t(795,B.a6f)
B.X7=s(['PRESSING IMPLEMENT IN THE FULL VIEW NOW VISIBLY LANDS. You: "After I hit the implement button in fullsized view it still shows the same implement button" and "You are putting an implement button next to your replies, don\'t think we need those." Both are the same thing. Pressing Implement posts your approval as a NEW message on the conversation - nothing about the message you approved changes - so the button sat there looking exactly as it did before, which reads as the tap having done nothing, and invites a second tap and a second approval.',"An entry that already has your approval after it has nothing left to authorise, so its button now goes away. The one case it deliberately keeps: post a NEW question onto a thread you approved earlier, and THAT entry still gets its own button - which was the whole reason for keeping the button in v784.","Nine tests, including your exact sequence: ask, approve, and the ask stops offering."],t.s)
B.arm=new A.t(794,B.X7)
B.a4K=s(['THE EDITED - RESEND BUTTON ACTUALLY WORKS NOW. You: "When I make changes to the text of a Claude item, in the box, I am not seeing the button to resubmit. Yesterday you were phantomly marking items that hadn\'t been changed and ever since I mentioned that, I have not seen that button again." Both halves of that sentence were right, and they turn out to be the same bug seen from two sides.','For the button to know your words changed, the app has to remember what was SENT. The path every dictated Claude item takes recorded that the item went out - but never recorded what went out. So dictated items had no baseline at all, and "edited since sent" could never become true for them. The reason you saw the button before is worse, not better: it was comparing your item against the APPROVAL line, which never matches, so every approved item wore it. That is the phantom marking. Fixing that removed the wrong comparison without ever adding the right one.',"Now the send records the item's own words, so an edit shows the button and a resend clears it. And every Claude item already in your library - which has no record of what was sent - quietly adopts what it says today as its starting point the first time you look at it, so the whole back catalogue becomes watchable from here. It cannot detect an edit you made yesterday, and it will not pretend to: guessing there is exactly the phantom marking you told me about."],t.s)
B.atm=new A.t(793,B.a4K)
B.Y5=s(['THE ITEM IS NOW WHATEVER THE BOX SAID IT WAS. You: "You are doing it again. I am entering as a Claude item, and you are creating a note. This is a bug that has repeated itself over and over and over again, and you need to get it fixed permanently." You are right that it kept coming back, and I found it in your data: your 12:15:09 dictation saved as a plain note and never reached me, and you re-dictated the identical words at 12:17:29 to get them through. You had to say it twice.','WHY IT KEPT COMING BACK. Every previous fix patched one input, and the badge and the save were asking different questions - so they could always disagree somewhere new. The BADGE decided from three things: did you address it to Claude, are you in a folder, and what type was the last item you made (which is the "remember my last type" behaviour you asked for). The SAVE looked only at whether you had addressed it to Claude, and knew nothing about the remembered type at all. So a box that opened as Claude because your previous item was one showed the Claude badge honestly and then saved a Note, just as honestly.',"THE RULE IS NOW ONE SENTENCE: the item is whatever the box said it was. The save is handed the same answer the badge painted, so they cannot drift apart - it is not two places that have to agree, it is one answer used twice. Your explicit pick still beats everything, exactly as before: choose Note on a box showing Claude and you get a Note.","AND IT COVERS TASK AND MEETING, not just Claude. Fixing only the type you reported would have left the identical bug waiting in the other two - which is precisely the mistake that made this a four-time bug. Fifteen tests, including one that sweeps every combination and asserts the saved type equals what you were shown."],t.s)
B.aq0=new A.t(792,B.Y5)
B.Zb=s(['MOVING A FINISHED ITEM OUT OF PROCESSED NOW STICKS. You: "I just dragged an item out of processed and into the new folder Need to be tested and it stayed in the processed folder. Should that be able to move?" It should, and your drag worked perfectly - the item really did land in the new folder. The background pass put it back in Processed a moment later, so it looked like the move failed.',"Same cause as the Done fix in the previous build, and the same cause as v751 before that: the app files finished work into Processed, then keeps re-filing it every time it notices the item is not there. Taking something OUT of a folder is you saying it does not belong there, and re-adding it silently overrules you. The Queue learned that rule back in v751. Processed never did. It has now: an item is filed into Processed ONCE, and after that wherever you put it is where it stays.","Being straight about this one: it is the same mistake as the previous build, one folder over, and I found it an hour after writing myself a rule to check exactly this. The rule was right and I did not run it. It is now written into the code itself at the function that keeps breaking - six times so far - rather than in a document I have to remember to open."],t.s)
B.av4=new A.t(791,B.Zb)
B.a3x=s(['PRESSING DONE REALLY DOES TAKE IT OUT OF PROCESSED. You: "I hit done on an item in processed and it put it into done folder but didn\'t move it out of processed. Should it be doing that?" No, it should not - and the Done button was innocent. It moves the item correctly. The background pass that files finished work then put it BACK.','That pass knew about two folders, Queue and Processed, and had never been told your Done folder exists. So the moment you moved something into Done it read as "finished and filed nowhere" and helpfully filed it into Processed again. You moved it out, it moved it back. 17 of your items were sitting in both folders because of it.',"This is my code fighting you, which is the worst kind of bug because it silently undoes a decision you made - and it is the same one as v751, when items you dragged out of the Queue kept coming back. One folder further along, and I did not carry the lesson across. Done is now TERMINAL: once an item is in there the app files it nowhere, ever, whatever else it thinks. It only clears the stale rows the move left behind - so your 17 double-filed items clean themselves up on the next check-in, with nothing for you to do.","The Done button also now clears a SECOND Processed row if an item somehow had two, so you see the right thing the moment you tap instead of waiting for the next pass. Sixteen tests pin it, including a sweep of every possible combination proving nothing can re-file an item you have closed out."],t.s)
B.atr=new A.t(790,B.a3x)
B.a5r=s(['ANSWERING A QUESTION NOW CLEARS "NEEDS YOU" ON THE BOX. You: "I have answered the question and pressed implement on that reply and inside of the fullsized item it shows that it is 5 on the que but the box view just says needs you even though I have answered." Both views were reading different things and only one was right. Two separate faults had to line up to produce it, and both are fixed.',"FIRST: back in v767 I fixed the opposite bug - pressing Implement was wiping out my question, so I ruled that a later message of ANY kind does not answer a question. That is correct for an approval, which is plumbing. It is wrong for what you actually did, which was ANSWER, in words, and then approve. Under that rule the item stayed blocked on you forever. The app now tells an approval line apart from your own words: your words clear the question, an approval on its own does not.",'SECOND: the item\'s saved status could not come back down from "needs you" even once the question was answered. Statuses only ever move forward, so nothing can walk finished work backwards - but "needs you" had been slotted into that ladder ABOVE "received", which made it a one-way trap with only one exit: marking the whole thing done. It is not a stage, it is an interruption laid over whatever stage the item had reached, and it now clears back to where the item actually is. Nothing else about forward-only changed.','Your existing stuck items heal themselves - the next time the app checks in, any item wearing a stale "needs you" drops back to its real state. Fourteen tests pin both halves, including your exact thread.'],t.s)
B.avZ=new A.t(789,B.a5r)
B.Yt=s(['RENAMING AN ITEM NOW SHOWS UP IN THE LIST. You: "I just edited the item from 11:22am in the full sized view and when I went back to the list it didn\'t save and update with my changes." Your rename DID save - the server had it a minute before you reported this, and I checked that before touching any code. What failed was the list. The body editor, when it saves, tells the main list in the same breath. The title editor wrote to the database and told nobody. And the check that decides whether the list needs redrawing compared the item type, its status, my reply and the WORDS - but not the title. So a title-only change was invisible to it and your old title stayed on screen.',"That is the same mistake as bug #94 back on 07-18, which added the words to that same check for exactly this reason and stopped one field short. Both title editors now tell the list, the check can see the title, and nine tests pin it - including your exact case: same words, new title.",'AND A TRACE, so the next one takes seconds instead of twenty minutes. There was NO record anywhere of an edit being saved. You have reported "my edit did not save" more than once and there was nothing to check afterwards, which is why answering this one meant digging through the server by hand. Every title save and every body save now writes a line to the log.','REMOVED, at your word: the Reply button on the collapsed box. You: "Let\'s remove the reply on the box view." You asked first whether it was needed there and I said no - on a collapsed box the conversation is not on screen, so it was offering to answer something you could not read. Reply still lives on the OPEN box, where you can see what you are replying to.'],t.s)
B.aqk=new A.t(788,B.Yt)
B.a7X=s(['REMOVED, at your word: the ticking timer beside the item type selector. You: "There is a timer next to the item type label and selector. It is running now on the Fold for a note and the dictate function is not on. Please remove it." You were right about more than the clutter - it was counting from the moment the section started and never stopped, mic or no mic, so on an idle note it sat there climbing and reporting recording time for a box that was recording nothing. A number that is wrong whenever you are not talking is worse than no number. The red border already says "live", honestly. The timer stays on Task and Meeting boxes, which only exist while that unit is genuinely capturing.',"ALSO REMOVED, and you did NOT ask for this one, so here it is plainly: taking the timer out uncovered a second, invisible one-second refresh in the same box. It was rebuilding the entire dictate box - the one holding the text field you type into - once every second for as long as the app was open, to redraw a number that already redrew itself. It is gone. Nothing on screen changes and no feature is affected. I am not claiming this was behind the cursor trouble; it simply had no business being there.",'THE CLAUDE STATUS TABLE FITS ON A PHONE. You: "On Mobile the table doesn\'t have enough room." The timestamps were carrying a date on every row - Jul 28 - 11:25 AM, seventeen characters fighting a twenty-three character stage name for the same width. Stamps from TODAY now show just the time, which gives nine characters back to the words. Older stamps keep their date, because there the date is the useful part. And on a genuinely narrow box the longest stage name wraps to a second line instead of being cut to SENT FOR IMPLEME.',"A RULE I AM HOLDING MYSELF TO, after you asked whether I remove things without your permission: from here, any build that removes anything you can SEE says so on its own line here and quotes the sentence of yours that authorized it. If a removal cannot cite one of your sentences, it does not ship - it comes to you as a question first. This entry is the first one written that way."],t.s)
B.avo=new A.t(787,B.a7X)
B.a_S=s(["A CLAUDE ITEM TELLS YOU WHEN THERE IS SOMETHING NEW TO READ. You asked three times today whether I had replied - and on at least one of those I HAD, on the item, and nothing on your screen had changed to say so. Boxes now show a small clay dot and the words New answer when the answers on that item have grown since you last opened it. Opening the item clears it; you are never asked to dismiss a marker you did not ask for.","It is deliberately quiet, and deliberately separate from NEEDS YOU. That one means I am blocked and cannot continue without you. This one means there is information here. Two different urgencies, two different marks - putting them together would make the urgent one stop meaning anything. And it never reorders your queue: items with new answers stay exactly where you dragged them, because that order is your priority list and my replies have no business rearranging it."],t.s)
B.av9=new A.t(786,B.a_S)
B.Y0=s(["A MANUAL WAY TO CLEAR FINISHED WORK OUT OF THE QUEUE. Open the Queue folder and, when there is finished work sitting in it, a green button appears at the top right showing how many. One tap moves them to Processed. It only ever moves FINISHED work - moving unfinished items would make Processed lie about what has been built, which is worse than a stuck item - and if there is nothing to move the button does not appear at all rather than sitting there doing nothing. The automatic move is supposed to make this unnecessary, and it has failed you four separate ways in two days. A system with that record should have a manual override, so a stuck item costs you one tap instead of a report and a wait."],t.s)
B.at6=new A.t(785,B.Y0)
B.a0J=s(["THE IMPLEMENT BUTTON ONLY APPEARS WHERE IT MEANS SOMETHING. You asked whether you have to press it for a reply to count. You do not - every message you send reaches me whether you approve it or not - so a button offering to implement your ANSWER was implying your words might otherwise be ignored. It is now offered only on a genuine new ask. It no longer appears on answers to my questions, on the approval messages themselves (those were showing up as your own entries, each with a button of its own), or on finished entries. It stays where it earns its place: a thread carrying several asks, where pressing it names which one to build."],t.s)
B.ar4=new A.t(784,B.a0J)
B.a0C=s(["THE GREEN MIC IN TASK AND MEETING BOXES IS GONE - THE ONE YOU ACTUALLY MEANT. The one removed in v777 was in the dictation box; this one lived inside a live Task box (green because Task is green) with a matching amber one in Meeting boxes. It only reappeared for you because v778 finally made Task picks work, so you were creating real Tasks again. You said the main dictate button should cover it, and it does: Dictate and Commands on the bottom bar are the same pause and resume - Commands keeps the session alive and stops capturing. Nothing is lost."],t.s)
B.aw5=new A.t(783,B.a0C)
B.a6O=s(['APPROVED WORK CAN FINALLY LEAVE THE QUEUE. You asked why a finished item was still sitting at the top of the Queue. The app was asking the NEWEST message on a conversation whether the work was done - and on anything you press Implement on, the newest message IS the approval, which is not a "done" message. So every item you approved read as unfinished forever and could never move to Processed. It now reads the whole conversation, the same way the rest of the app already did.','THE QUEUE COUNTS ONLY REAL ITEMS. It was showing things like "#36 of" when there were nowhere near 36 items in it. The count included leftover references to items you had deleted - invisible on screen but still taking up positions, which also pushed newly approved work to the very bottom. The position and the total now count only items that actually exist, and the 19 leftover references in your Queue have been cleared out.',"A NOTE CAN NO LONGER BE SENT ONCE PER DEVICE. A message that failed to send gets rescued automatically - but that rescue ran on every device you own, with nothing saying which device was responsible. One unsent note became three copies, one from each device, milliseconds apart. Before rescuing anything the app now asks whether that exact text already arrived, and links to it instead of sending again."],t.s)
B.aqJ=new A.t(782,B.a6O)
B.a2I=s(["MY ANSWERS REACH ITEMS THAT ARE ALREADY CLOSED. You spotted this: I wrote a long explanation onto a Claude item and you could not see any of it. The reason was a real bug, not a slip. The part of the app that copies my answers onto your items only looked at items that were still open - so once an item was marked finished on your device, nothing I wrote afterwards could ever appear on it. The text existed on the server and had no route to you. It now reads finished items too. Same cause as the queue fix a few versions ago, different part of the code: I taught one half about finished items and left the other half with the identical hole."],t.s)
B.avY=new A.t(779,B.a2I)
B.a_P=s(["PICKING TASK OR MEETING NOW ACTUALLY MAKES A TASK OR MEETING. Choosing either from the type dropdown on an idle box gave you a Note - the badge said Task, the saved item was a Note. The pick was being stored on the recording session, and there IS no session when the mic is off, so on a blank box it was written nowhere and silently lost. Claude never had this problem because its type is kept somewhere that exists whether you are recording or not; Task and Meeting now get the same. Seven tests, including the exact case you hit."],t.s)
B.awz=new A.t(778,B.a_P)
B.a7y=s(["THE GREEN MIC BUTTON IS GONE. You asked for it twice and it is out of the dictation box everywhere. CORRECTION to what this entry first said: I warned that this removed the only way to dictate inside a folder. That was wrong. The Off / Commands only / Dictate bar sits on the top edge of the dictate box, and that box is in folders too - so dictating into a folder works exactly as it did. I had checked whether folders have a BOTTOM bar, found none, and stopped looking."],t.s)
B.auT=new A.t(777,B.a7y)
B.a2z=s(["THE FOLDERS PANE NO LONGER APPEARS WHEN YOU HIT BACK. It was never opening itself. The pane closed only when you navigated FROM it - so if you reached a folder page any other way, the pane stayed open behind it, hidden by the full-page folder view, and hitting back simply revealed it. It now closes on any change of screen, however it happened: forward, back, or the system back gesture."],t.s)
B.avO=new A.t(776,B.a2z)
B.a6I=s(['THE DICTATION BOX COLOURS NOW MATCH YOUR ITEMS. You asked me twice to look at this, and there was a real mismatch: the same type was two different colours depending on where you looked. A Task was GREEN in your item list but ORANGE in the dictation box. A Meeting was AMBER in your list but INDIGO in the box. Notes and Claude items already matched. Nobody chose that - the box had its own hand-typed colours that had drifted from the ones your items use, and the new type dropdown I built copied the wrong ones. There is now one answer to "what colour is this type" and the box, the dropdown and your item list all ask it.'],t.s)
B.arb=new A.t(775,B.a6I)
B.a4j=s(["THE STATUS ROWS NO LONGER BREAK ON NARROW BOXES. The longest stage name - SENT FOR IMPLEMENTATION - was competing with its badge and its timestamp for the same space, and on a narrow box one of the three lost. The name and its badge now share one slot and the time keeps its own room, so the row shortens gracefully instead of breaking. If you still see an item formatted wrong after this, tell me which one and what it looks like - this is the most likely cause but I am fixing it from the code, not from a picture of your screen."],t.s)
B.atL=new A.t(774,B.a4j)
B.Z3=s(["CHOOSING A BLUE NOTE STAYS A BLUE NOTE. You said you had tried to make a new blue item a number of times and kept getting a Claude item instead. Two different things were writing the same setting: your PICK, and the app watching what it had just posted. Posting a Claude message quietly re-remembered Claude - so right after any Claude item, the next box turned back into a Claude box and undid the Note you chose. You have made dozens of Claude items, which is exactly what made it stick. Your pick now wins: the app may fill that memory when it is empty, but it can never overwrite a choice you made. Rapid-firing Claude items still works the same way, because that comes from your pick and picks persist."],t.s)
B.aqC=new A.t(773,B.Z3)
B.a2l=s(["FINISHED ITEMS REALLY DO LEAVE THE QUEUE NOW - THE SECOND HALF OF THE FIX. You reported this twice, and you were right both times: v761 was not enough. The list of items the app sweeps deliberately leaves OUT anything already marked finished - and the Queue-to-Processed move lives inside that same sweep. So the moment an item was marked done it dropped out of the list, and the move that was supposed to file it could never run. A race the item always lost: marking it finished is exactly what made it invisible to the pass that files it. The sweep now reads finished items too."],t.s)
B.arp=new A.t(772,B.a2l)
B.a_U=s(["A PAUSE BUTTON IN THE QUEUE FOLDER. Open Queue and there is now a Pause button at the top right. Paused means I stop picking up new work from it; press again to resume. The hard part was not the button - a pause has to reach ME, not just your screen, or you would sit there reading PAUSED while I carried on working. So the state is stored as a folder named Paused inside your Claude folder, which syncs to every device and to the server the same way everything else does. That also means you can SEE the pause in your folder list instead of trusting an invisible switch.","One deliberate exception: while paused, work I have already finished still moves through to Processed. Freezing that would only lose the receipts for work that is already done - a pause is about what I START."],t.s)
B.auC=new A.t(771,B.a_U)
B.ZD=s(["DELETES: THE REDUNDANT WORK IS GONE, AND THE REST IS NOW MEASURED. Your log showed the app rebuilding your ENTIRE library - all 4,287 items - after every delete cycle, which is why deleting got slower as your library grew rather than being slow from the start. Those rebuilds also fire on every sync checkpoint and every save, and most of them find nothing new. The app now checks two numbers first - how many items exist and the newest timestamp - and when they are unchanged it skips the whole rebuild instead of re-reading everything. Nothing is filtered out; the skip only happens when the data is provably identical, so it cannot hide an item.","The rebuilds that DO have to run are now timed, so the next slow delete says whether the cost is reading, converting or comparing - and the next fix goes where the time actually is instead of where I guess it is."],t.s)
B.aq4=new A.t(770,B.ZD)
B.a5q=s(["THE TYPE LABEL IS A REAL DROPDOWN NOW. Tapping Note / Task / Meeting / Claude inside the dictation box used to slide a sheet up from the bottom of the screen - your eye left the box, picked, and came back. The menu now drops from the label itself, right where you tapped. Underneath it still goes through the same one path as the plus menu and the spoken commands, so switching type here behaves identically - it is a different door onto the same room, not a second way of doing it."],t.s)
B.atO=new A.t(769,B.a5q)
B.a55=s(["THE EDITED-RESEND BUTTON WAS APPEARING WHERE IT SHOULD NOT - MY BUG, FIXED. You said it seemed to be firing on its own, and your inbox proved it: two of your messages had been posted to me twice. The cause was mine from v761. The app remembers what text it last sent you so it can tell when you have edited an item - but I recorded that for EVERY kind of send, and three of the four send something else: pressing Implement sends an approval line, tapping an answer sends your choice, a reply sends a new message. So after you pressed Implement, the app thought the approval line was your item text, decided the item no longer matched, and grew an orange Edited-Resend button on it. One tap sent a duplicate. Only a genuine send of the item text now counts.","AND RESEND ASKS FIRST. A mis-tap on that button posts a real message to me, so it now confirms before sending, the same way Implement has always asked."],t.s)
B.ash=new A.t(768,B.a55)
B.a8_=s(["MY QUESTIONS TO YOU STOPPED SHOWING - FIXED. You asked whether the question system was working. It was not, and your own data showed why: I asked you about the green mic at 6:29pm and the item was correctly marked as needing you, then you pressed Implement on it at 6:41pm and that newer message overwrote the flag. The question was still there; the box just stopped saying so. An item took its state from the NEWEST message on its conversation, so any later message of any kind buried an open question. It now reads the whole conversation: finished work wins, an unanswered question beats everything else, and no later message can hide it. Ten tests, built from your actual thread."],t.s)
B.ase=new A.t(767,B.a8_)
B.a3q=s(["THE STATUS LADDER USES YOUR WORDING. You asked what APPROVED meant on an item, and the answer is that I used the wrong word. It meant you pressed Implement - you authorised the build - but APPROVED reads like the work itself was approved, which is what DONE means. You settled this back in v765-era feedback: say SENT FOR IMPLEMENTATION. The button has said that for a while and your mockup labels the row the same way; the new ladder was the only place still saying APPROVED. Now they match."],t.s)
B.ath=new A.t(766,B.a3q)
B.a2D=s(["THE IMPLEMENT BUTTON NOW SAYS WHY IT IS GREEN. You asked whether a green Approved button on a fresh item was intended - it is not. Green means an approval exists, and there are only two ways it can: your tap on this device, or the server telling this device you tapped it somewhere else. I checked your live data and found no item borrowing an approval that belonged to a different item, so I could not reproduce it - and guessing a third time is how the cursor bug ate three weeks. The button now records which of the two turned it green, so the next time you see it the log names the cause instead of costing another build."],t.s)
B.axp=new A.t(765,B.a2D)
B.a3f=s(["THE DATES AND TIMES IN THE STATUS BOX LINE UP. They were right-aligned, which lined up the right edge and left the left one ragged - the day and the hour are one or two digits, so every row started somewhere different. Each stamp is now padded to a fixed width with a digit-wide space, so the times form a clean column on both edges. Nothing about how they read has changed."],t.s)
B.apu=new A.t(764,B.a3f)
B.Yr=s(['ANSWER MY QUESTION WITHOUT OPENING THE ITEM. When I am blocked, the box used to say only "NEEDS YOU - open to answer", so the question itself was a screen away. The box now shows the question and its choices right there, and one tap IS your answer on that thread. If it is a free-text question it says so and points you at Reply instead of drawing buttons that do nothing.','THE STAGE LIST SHOWS ONLY WHAT HAS HAPPENED. Your call, and the right one: instead of six rows on every box, you see the stages an item has actually reached, with a "+N more" tap when you want the whole path. A brand-new item still shows one live row, never an empty panel.'],t.s)
B.awP=new A.t(763,B.Yr)
B.a6N=s(["CLAUDE BOXES NOW SHOW THE WHOLE PATH, BUILT FROM THE MOCKUP. Instead of one status line and a run-on trail, every box shows a ladder: SENT, RECEIVED, APPROVED, QUEUE, PROCESSED, DONE - each on its own row with its own date and time. Stages that have not happened yet are greyed with a dash where the time will go, so you can see where an item is AND what is still ahead of it without opening anything.",'THE QUEUE ROW SHOWS DEPTH, NOT JUST POSITION. It reads "#2 of 13" - your place in the line and how much is ahead of it. Drag the item in the Queue folder and the number follows. The PROCESSED row names the release it shipped in, and the DONE row says "your call", because that move is only ever yours.',"WHEN I AM BLOCKED THE LADDER STOPS AT NEEDS YOU. The rungs below it are not drawn at all - they cannot advance until you answer, and greying them out would suggest waiting is enough."],t.s)
B.aqi=new A.t(762,B.a6N)
B.a0n=s(['FINISHED WORK NOW ACTUALLY LEAVES THE QUEUE. You were right that completed items were not moving. It was not slow - the move was unreachable. The rule that stops me putting an item back after you drag it out was checked FIRST and skipped everything below it, and the Queue-to-Processed move lives below that line. Two different questions were sharing one gate: "leave it where he put it" is about filing IN, and it can no longer block a finished item from moving ON. Items sitting in Queue AND Processed at once get the stray Queue row dropped on the next sweep.','EDITED SINCE SENT, AND A RESEND BUTTON. Changing the words of an item you had already sent me did nothing and said nothing - it read one way on your screen and another in my queue, with neither of us able to see the difference. The box now shows an orange "Edited - Resend" button, and only when the words really differ. Tapping it posts the current text onto the same conversation. Re-wrapping or stray spaces do not count as an edit, and anything sent before this update is never flagged.'],t.s)
B.awT=new A.t(761,B.a0n)
B.Y4=s(["THE DICTATE BOX NO LONGER SHOWS A CURSOR IT CANNOT BACK UP. On Windows, that blinking caret in the unfocused box is drawn by the app - it is not a live field - so when focus was lost you got a cursor that looked ready over a box that could not take a key. On desktop it now appears only while dictation is actually running. Clicking the box still types. On the phone nothing changes: there the caret honestly marks where your spoken words land.","EVERY LOST CURSOR NOW SAYS WHY. The rule that decides whether the box re-claims focus has six inputs and was logging two - and only when it said yes. Every time it declined, which is the case that leaves you unable to type, nothing was written. All six inputs and the outcome are now recorded on every single focus loss, so the next occurrence names its own cause instead of costing another round.",'THE MOVE-TO INDICATOR READS PROPERLY AGAIN. Item names go straight into that one-line label while you drag, and a dictated title carries the invisible break marks that "new line" and "new paragraph" leave behind. A single-line label cannot break on those, so it drew them - that was the garble. Names are now flattened for display, and over-long ones shorten at a word.',"CLAUDE ANSWERS CAN BE COPIED, AND LINKS ARE CLICKABLE. In the full-size view both your message and the answer are now selectable text - right-click Copy on Windows, long-press on the phone - and any web address in them opens in your browser."],t.s)
B.aw2=new A.t(760,B.Y4)
B.a0S=s(["THE CURSOR STOPS JUMPING BACK TO THE DICTATE BOX \u2014 AND THIS TIME IT IS PINNED. Clicking an item below no longer pulls the cursor up. The reason this kept coming back: the app judged a lost cursor by WHO took it, and that cannot tell \u201che clicked the box\u201d from \u201che clicked an item below\u201d \u2014 both look identical. It now reads WHERE YOUR CLICK LANDED, and all three cases (click the box, click an item, no click at all) are locked by tests, so fixing one can no longer break another."],t.s)
B.asw=new A.t(759,B.a0S)
B.a74=s(["PICKING TASK OR MEETING RETYPES THE BOX \u2014 IT NO LONGER REPLACES IT. Choosing Task from the menu was opening the voice-capture box built for SAYING \u201cnew task\u201d: a green mic appeared and your dictate box vanished. Buttons retype, voice captures. The badge\u2019s promise now binds the save too, so a box labelled Task produces a task."],t.s)
B.aqX=new A.t(758,B.a74)
B.a_h=s(["YOUR WORDS ALWAYS POST. The duplicate guard was swallowing real notes: post \u201cTest.\u201d and a follow-up starting with the same word vanished; paste a longer version of something already posted and it never appeared. The rule is narrowed to the one true leftover shape \u2014 a long fragment of what was just posted \u2014 and pinned by tests using today\u2019s exact cases. When in doubt, the note posts: a near-duplicate costs one swipe, a lost note costs your words."],t.s)
B.auO=new A.t(757,B.a_h)
B.Y7=s(["THE BOX SPEAKS ALL FOUR TYPES. Picking Task or Meeting used to change nothing you could see \u2014 the badge only knew Note and Claude, which read as \u201cnot letting me choose.\u201d The badge now says Note, Task, Meeting or Claude, each in its own colour.","A TYPE PICK ALWAYS LANDS. Choosing a type during a live dictation left the previous Claude flag standing, so the box could ignore your choice. A pick is a statement about THIS box \u2014 it takes effect mic on or off."],t.s)
B.atM=new A.t(756,B.Y7)
B.a1K=s(["THE BOX NO LONGER LIES ABOUT WHAT IT WILL MAKE. It said \u201cNote\u201d and produced a Claude item, because the label read one flag while the save weighed three \u2014 including the remembered last type. Both now read the same answer, so the badge is a promise the save keeps."],t.s)
B.auN=new A.t(755,B.a1K)
B.a3v=s(["REMOVING AN ITEM FROM THE QUEUE WORKS ON THE FIRST TRY. v751 stopped items coming back, but only for ones it had filed itself \u2014 everything already sitting in your Queue was unknown to it, so the first removal of each would still have bounced. Anything seen in the Queue now counts as queued, so out means out immediately."],t.s)
B.atu=new A.t(754,B.a3v)
B.Z4=s(["THE TYPE LABEL IN THE DICTATE BOX IS NOW A DROP DOWN. Tap \u201cNote\u201d or \u201cClaude\u201d and pick what you want \u2014 the box retypes on the spot, and the next box remembers it, same as picking from \uff0b."],t.s)
B.arE=new A.t(753,B.Z4)
B.WV=s(["A NEW ITEM APPEARS EVEN WHILE YOUR CURSOR IS IN THE BOX. The list refuses to rebuild under a live caret \u2014 right, because a rebuild would steal it \u2014 but that also hid the item you had just created. New items are now ADDED below while the rebuild waits: nothing moves, nothing reorders, your typing is untouched."],t.s)
B.atJ=new A.t(752,B.WV)
B.a0s=s(["ITEMS YOU TAKE OUT OF THE QUEUE STAY OUT. My sweep was re-filing them \u2014 right the first time, wrong every time after, because removing one is you saying it does not belong there. An item is queued once; after that your placement stands.","MOVING AN ITEM NO LONGER OPENS THE ONE ABOVE IT. An open row leaving the list was handing its cursor to its neighbour, which then opened itself. The move paths now drop the cursor first, exactly as delete has since v453.","THE PURPLE COMMANDS-ONLY BAR IS GONE from above the dictate box. The same warning still appears inside the box, where you are actually looking."],t.s)
B.as3=new A.t(751,B.a0s)
B.a1h=s(["A QUESTIONS FOLDER \u2014 YOURS. Anything you ask me now files itself into a Questions folder inside Claude, at the back of its own queue, and NOTHING takes it out but you. Detection is simple and predictable: ends with \u201c?\u201d or starts with what / why / how / can / is / are / should. Press Implement on one and it becomes work instead \u2014 it moves to the Queue.","MY BLOCKED ITEMS RIDE THE TASK QUEUE. When I need an answer from you, that item sits in the Queue wearing its NEEDS YOU flag, instead of hiding in a folder of its own \u2014 one list to watch, as you asked."],t.s)
B.aun=new A.t(750,B.a1h)
B.a01=s(["QUEUE #1 IS NOW THE NEXT THING TO BE WORKED. Newly approved items were landing at the TOP of the Queue folder \u2014 right for a library, wrong for a queue \u2014 so the item you sent last was labelled #1. New work now joins the BACK of the line, and dragging still overrides it: your order beats arrival order."],t.s)
B.av7=new A.t(749,B.a01)
B.WY=s(["NO MORE TUG-OF-WAR WITH A DIALOG. Open the new-folder box while the dictate box has your text and the two were fighting over the keyboard. A dialog is something you opened \u2014 while it is up, the dictate box stands down completely and lets you type in the dialog."],t.s)
B.asX=new A.t(748,B.WY)
B.a14=s(["FOLDERS UNFOLD LESS EAGERLY. Resting a dragged item on a folder still opens its subfolders \u2014 but it now takes 0.9 seconds instead of 0.5, so crossing a folder on the way somewhere else no longer unfolds it."],t.s)
B.avf=new A.t(747,B.a14)
B.a3G=s(["THE CLICK-ONCE-TO-TYPE BUG, NAMED AT LAST. Your log shows the box losing the cursor to a focus SCOPE \u2014 the framework re-parenting focus, not you clicking anywhere. The guard that re-claims the cursor skipped those cases whenever they happened within a quarter-second of your click on the box, which is exactly when they happen. A scope is never something you clicked, so it is now always treated as a steal and the cursor comes straight back."],t.s)
B.asg=new A.t(746,B.a3G)
B.a37=s(["THE BOX CLEARS THE INSTANT YOU HIT ENTER. Your words were being held in the box until the save, the Claude send and the folder filing all finished \u2014 seconds, on a busy sync \u2014 even though the item had already appeared below. It clears immediately now, and if a save ever fails the words drop straight back in."],t.s)
B.auW=new A.t(745,B.a37)
B.a_3=s(["THE LOOP CLOSES: A DONE BUTTON. Queue fills itself when you approve, Processed fills itself when the work lands \u2014 and now a finished item carries a green Done button that moves it out of Processed into your Done folder, in one tap. Done stays yours: the app files into it only when you press that button."],t.s)
B.aw3=new A.t(744,B.a_3)
B.Zv=s(["DRAG A SUBFOLDER STRAIGHT TO A SPOT AT THE TOP LEVEL. The drop strips between root folders only accepted folders that were ALREADY at the top level, so a subfolder aimed there was rejected and fell into the row underneath instead. They now take any folder: it is promoted out of its parent and seated exactly where you aimed, in one drop."],t.s)
B.asm=new A.t(743,B.Zv)
B.a1U=s(["THE DUPLICATE-AS-A-NOTE IS DEAD. Your 10:01am note read \u201cWith the proper With the proper time and focus\u2026\u201d and your 10:02am Claude item held the same words: the box kept a TAIL after the Claude message closed and posted it as a fresh note. The old guard only caught a leftover that matched EXACTLY, so a tail with a doubled opening slipped through. Now a leftover is recognised by content, and a tail that follows a Claude message stays a Claude message instead of being demoted to a note. Genuinely new words still post \u2014 that rule is pinned by tests."],t.s)
B.at_=new A.t(742,B.a1U)
B.ZH=s(["CLICKING AN ITEM IN A FOLDER STOPS YANKING YOU BACK. Inside a folder, every click looked to the app like \u201cno user input\u201d \u2014 so the box you had been typing in re-claimed the cursor and pulled you back to the top. Folders now feed the same click clock Home always had, so a click is treated as what it is: yours."],t.s)
B.auy=new A.t(741,B.ZH)
B.a66=s(["ANYTHING YOU CAN SAY WEARS QUOTES. The \u201cCreate dictation\u201d button now matches the spoken hints \u2014 if the words activate dictation, they are quoted."],t.s)
B.ar1=new A.t(740,B.a66)
B.ZJ=s(["THE BOX KEEPS THE LAST TYPE \u2014 ONE RULE, EVERYWHERE. Whatever you last created here, the next box is that: dictated or typed, on Home or inside a folder. It was three separate rules before, which is why it worked after some actions and reverted after others. A type folder (Claude and its subfolders) still decides its own type; an ordinary folder no longer forces \u201cnote\u201d."],t.s)
B.ate=new A.t(739,B.ZJ)
B.ZL=s(["THE BOX TAKES TYPING AFTER EVERY POST. Create an item and the fresh box is ready for the next one \u2014 no clicking into it first. That was the last place the caret drew without the keyboard being handed over.","THE MICROPHONE BUTTON IS OFF THE INBOX BOX. It was mine and it was redundant there \u2014 the On/Off/New control is the mic. It stays in folders, where it is the only way to dictate."],t.s)
B.awb=new A.t(738,B.ZL)
B.a32=s(["FOLDER NAMES ARE READABLE AGAIN. Names were sharing the row\u2019s free space with an invisible spacer, so each one got about half of what was left \u2014 worse the deeper the folder sat. The name (and its chevron) now take all the room actually available; counts and toggles keep their right edge."],t.s)
B.ar9=new A.t(737,B.a32)
B.a6q=s(["EVERY QUEUE FILING NOW CARRIES ITS RECEIPT. When an item moves into Queue, the log records which approval authorised it and when \u2014 so \u201cthat looked queued before I pressed Implement\u201d can be settled with evidence instead of memory."],t.s)
B.avd=new A.t(736,B.a6q)
B.a7_=s(["THE CLAUDE BOX TAKES TYPING RIGHT AWAY. Picking \u201cNew Claude message\u201d drew the caret but never actually took the keyboard, so you had to click the box first. It now takes focus for real.","\u201cSENT FOR IMPLEMENTATION\u201d. Your wording \u2014 pressing the button is you authorising the work, not the work being finished. PROCESSED is what says finished.","AN ITEM THAT NEEDS YOU SAYS SO. A clay \u201cNEEDS YOU \u2014 open to answer\u201d line sits at the top of that item\u2019s box, so you can see WHICH one is waiting without opening anything."],t.s)
B.au0=new A.t(735,B.a7_)
B.XA=s(["THE BLANK NOTE IS ACTUALLY GONE. v730 aimed at it and missed: your 8:26am blank was saved ELEVEN SECONDS BEFORE the Claude item it belonged to, so the test could not have fired yet. A session addressed to Claude no longer saves an empty note at all.","THE BOX KEEPS THE TYPE. Post a Claude message from the Inbox and the next box is still a Claude box \u2014 rapid-fire without re-picking every time."],t.s)
B.aqU=new A.t(734,B.XA)
B.a0V=s(["A QUESTIONS FOLDER THAT EMPTIES ITSELF. When Claude is blocked on you, that item files itself into a Questions folder inside Claude \u2014 and the moment you answer, it leaves on its own. Your design, exactly.","THE CLAUDE ROW TELLS YOU. A clay \u201c2 need you\u201d badge sits on the pinned Claude row whenever questions are open, and disappears when the last one is answered."],t.s)
B.auv=new A.t(733,B.a0V)
B.a1L=s(["PICKING \u201cNEW CLAUDE MESSAGE\u201d ON A BLANK BOX WORKS AGAIN. v730 stopped the \uff0b from switching the mic on \u2014 but nothing then retyped the box, so the pick did nothing you could see and the item came out as a note. The blank box now becomes a Claude box the moment you pick it, and speaking into it lands as a Claude message.","PROCESSED NAMES ITS RELEASE. \u201cPROCESSED \xb7 Jul 27 \xb7 7:51am \xb7 v730\u201d \u2014 the version taken from the reply that closed the item, so each finished item carries its own receipt."],t.s)
B.aph=new A.t(732,B.a1L)
B.Zk=s(["THE IMPLEMENT BUTTON SAYS IMPLEMENTED \u2014 EVERYWHERE. Pressing it on the phone left Windows showing a plain Implement button, because that green state was remembered only on the device you pressed. It is now read from the approval on the thread itself, so every device shows \u201cIMPLEMENTED \xb7 Jul 27 \xb7 4:53am\u201d.","TURNING DICTATION OFF NO LONGER TURNS YOUR CLAUDE MESSAGE INTO A NOTE. The box you typed into decides what it is \u2014 switching the mic off is not a change of mind about what you wrote."],t.s)
B.apr=new A.t(731,B.Zk)
B.a7w=s(["THE BLANK NOTE BESIDE EVERY CLAUDE MESSAGE IS GONE. Your log showed it: an empty note saved 15 seconds before each Claude item \u2014 the box that was open when you started talking, whose words went to the Claude box instead. It is no longer saved, because its words were never lost.","THE \uff0b NO LONGER TURNS THE MIC ON. Creating an item opens the box in that type and waits \u2014 dictation starts only when you start it. If you are already dictating, nothing changes.","FINISHED WORK REACHES PROCESSED. An item approved before the pipeline existed never passed through Queue, so it never moved out. Done + approved now files straight into Processed.","THE PROCESSED / QUEUE LABEL CAN\u2019T BE CUT OFF. It has its own line above the status trail instead of competing with five timestamps for the same two lines."],t.s)
B.aw1=new A.t(730,B.a7w)
B.a6Q=s(["SEARCH IS ACTUALLY BACK ON WINDOWS. v727 said it was fixed; the code never made it into the build. It is in this one \u2014 checked in the file, not taken on trust.","A DICTATION CAN NO LONGER BE DROPPED AS \u201cBLANK\u201d. v725\u2019s no-blank-boxes rule judged silence using the live engine\u2019s recording; on the Whisper engines that recording lives elsewhere, so a box whose words were still being transcribed could have been discarded. When it cannot be proven empty, the item is kept \u2014 always."],t.s)
B.ar6=new A.t(729,B.a6Q)
B.a27=s(["THE INBOX BOX REMEMBERS. Make a Claude message from the Inbox and the next box opens as a Claude message too \u2014 until you pick something else. Rapid-firing no longer means re-choosing the type every time. Folders still decide their own type, and a filtered view still wins.","TYPING IN A CLAUDE FOLDER MAKES A CLAUDE MESSAGE. v727 shipped only the spoken half of this by mistake \u2014 dictating there worked, typing there still made a note. Both halves work now."],t.s)
B.aud=new A.t(728,B.a27)
B.a3J=s(["SEARCH IS BACK ON WINDOWS. v721 dropped it from the desktop sidebar while leaving it on the phone \u2014 my mistake, and exactly what the comment above that code warns about. Restored.","THE FOLDER DECIDES THE TYPE. Talking or typing in the Claude folder \u2014 or in Queue, Processed, or any subfolder you make there \u2014 creates a CLAUDE message and sends it, instead of a note.","ONE CLAUDE FOLDER. The old \u201cClaude messages\u201d folder folds into your current Claude folder, contents and subfolders intact, and the empty shell is removed. Nothing is deleted that holds anything."],t.s)
B.aus=new A.t(727,B.a3J)
B.X8=s(["THE QUEUE NUMBER IS ON THE BOX. A Claude item now leads its status line with where it sits \u2014 \u201cQUEUE #3\u201d while it waits, green \u201cPROCESSED\u201d once the work is done \u2014 so you can scan your whole queue from the main window without opening anything.","EVERY STATUS CARRIES ITS DATE AND TIME. Queue shows when you approved it, Processed shows when it finished, and the full-size ticket strip spells out the day too (\u201cPROCESSED \xb7 Jul 27 \xb7 5:51am\u201d)."],t.s)
B.avU=new A.t(726,B.X8)
B.Xn=s(["FOUND YOUR MISSING FOLDERS \u2014 there were THREE folders named \u201cClaude\u201d. A cross-device race made duplicates, and because they shared a name the app picked a different one each time: the pipeline filed Queue and Processed into one while your pinned row opened another. They were never lost, just filed in a twin. Duplicates now fold into the original (oldest wins, everywhere, on every device) and Queue/Processed come home inside it.","NO MORE BLANK BOXES. An item that closes holding no words is never created in the first place \u2014 with one deliberate exception: if its audio actually contains speech (a network drop can leave your words in the recording only) the item stays, because losing words is never allowed."],t.s)
B.atV=new A.t(725,B.Xn)
B.a_i=s(["THE PIPELINE SPEAKS YOUR LANGUAGE. The folders are named Queue and Processed now, exactly as you specced \u2014 approved work waits in Queue (its order is the work order), finished work lands in Processed for your review, and moving to Done is your call, by hand, on purpose. Your overnight items rename over automatically, and everything lives inside your Claude folder.","THE IMPLEMENT BUTTON REMEMBERS WHEN. After you press it, the button reads \u201c\u2713 Approved Jul 27 \xb7 4:51am\u201d \u2014 the date and time of your tap, right on the item.","I READ YOUR QUEUE ORDER. When several items are approved, I work them top-down in the order you arranged the Queue folder \u2014 no telling me which is next."],t.s)
B.ats=new A.t(724,B.a_i)
B.a1N=s(["\u201cUPLOADING 32 ITEMS\u201d NOW TELLS THE TRUTH. While you are dictating, uploads deliberately wait (recording owns the phone) \u2014 but the pill said \u201cUploading\u2026\u201d anyway, which read as stuck. It now says \u201c32 items wait for dictation to end\u201d, and the moment the mic goes off they drain."],t.s)
B.awF=new A.t(723,B.a1N)
B.Z5=s(["THE PIPELINE IS LIVE. Press Implement and the item files itself into a Building folder inside Claude; when the work completes it moves to Recently finished \u2014 the folders ARE the board, on every device. Both folders create themselves the first time they are needed.","THE TICKET STRIP. An approved item\u2019s chat now opens with BUILDING \xb7 #N in queue (drag items in the Building folder to reprioritise \u2014 the number follows), turning green RECENTLY FINISHED when it ships.","ONE-TAP ANSWERS. When Claude asks a question with choices, the choices appear as buttons \u2014 tap one and that IS your reply. No typing."],t.s)
B.apX=new A.t(722,B.Z5)
B.a2e=s(["ONE DICTATE BOX EVERYWHERE. The green mic that folders had is now on the Inbox box too \u2014 every composer is the same box with the same controls: tap the mic to start talking into it, tap the red stop to finish. The bottom 3-way on Home still works; this is a second way in, not a replacement.","MEETINGS AND NOTES ARE GONE FROM THE LIBRARY LIST \u2014 the type rows under Inbox are the one home for types now. Search and Listening remain."],t.s)
B.aqB=new A.t(721,B.a2e)
B.a5E=s(["THE BLANK SCREEN ON DOUBLE-CLICK IS FIXED. Your log caught the crash: the animation that flies the top bar between screens died mid-flight, killing the push half-way \u2014 blank screen, and the dead route then held the cursor hostage (your unkillable blinking caret). The flight is off; opens are instant and can\u2019t crash.","NO PATH CREATES ITEMS BELOW THE DICTATE BOX ANY MORE. The folder screens\u2019 \uff0b was the last one \u2014 it now points the dictate box at the chosen type, same as everywhere. Your rule, everywhere, no exceptions.","\u2715 CLEARS WORDS, NOT THE TYPE. Clearing a Claude box keeps it a Claude box.","TIMES AND STATUS ARE BIG AND DARK \u2014 in the box trail and in the chat entries. Important info, no longer hidden."],t.s)
B.auG=new A.t(720,B.a5E)
B.a1_=s(["CLAUDE TIMES ARE YOUR TIMES. The chat stamps were server time \u2014 five hours ahead. Everything converts to local now.","THE BOX SHOWS THE WHOLE TRAIL. A Claude item\u2019s box now carries the time for each status \u2014 Sent, Received, Done \u2014 under your original question, with the current state highlighted; the full conversation stays in the max view.","THE REPLY BOX TAKES THE CLICK. Tapping anywhere on the reply bar lands the caret, so typing works the first time on Windows.","THE PINNED CLAUDE FOLDER SITS ABOVE LIBRARY on the desktop sidebar too \u2014 it had slipped under the Library header there, reading as a Library entry."],t.s)
B.axt=new A.t(719,B.a1_)
B.Z6=s(["TOP-LEVEL FOLDERS CAN BE REORDERED AGAIN. The drop target for \u201cplace above this folder\u201d was eight pixels tall \u2014 everywhere else on a row meant \u201cmove inside it\u201d, which is also how folders kept nesting where you never aimed. Now: top third of a row drops above it, bottom third drops below, only the middle nests, and the blue line shows exactly where it will land.","DELETE IS INSTANT. The box used to stay on screen until the database write finished \u2014 seconds, on a busy desktop \u2014 which read as a dead button and invited the re-presses behind the stacked Undo bars. The box now leaves the moment you press; the write follows behind, and a failed write brings it straight back.","THE \uff0b FOLLOWS YOUR RULE AGAIN: creating a new item points the dictate box at that type \u2014 it never inserts a row below. My v709 change broke that; reverted.","THE CLAUDE CHAT ACTUALLY SHOWS UP \u2014 AND WITH EVERYTHING. The conversation was fetching fine (your log proved it) but rendering into a box with no height; fixed. Each entry now carries its full trail: Sent \u2192 Received \u2192 Needs you / \u2713 Complete, each with its time."],t.s)
B.atA=new A.t(718,B.Z6)
B.a2d=s(["THE FOLDER DICTATE BOX IS FULL WIDTH. It was carrying its own side padding on top of the screen\u2019s, which made it 32px narrower than the item boxes under it."],t.s)
B.auQ=new A.t(717,B.a2d)
B.a1n=s(["CLAUDE ITEMS ARE A REAL CHAT NOW. The full-size view shows the conversation as dated messages \u2014 yours and Claude\u2019s, each with its own time and state (Sent \xb7 Received \xb7 Needs you \xb7 \u2713 Complete), an Implement button on every entry you wrote, and a proper reply box pinned at the bottom instead of the big blank text area.","CLAUDE CAN NOW SAY \u201cNEEDS YOU\u201d. When Claude is blocked on a question, the entry turns orange with the question right there \u2014 so work never silently stalls waiting for an answer you did not know was wanted.","ONE CLAUDE IN THE PANE, NOT THREE. The old \u201cClaude\u201d Library entry opened a retired chat screen with no dictate box \u2014 that is what you kept landing on. It is gone; the type row and your pinned Claude folder remain, and the folder opens with the same dictate box as everywhere else.","THE FOLDER EXPAND ARROW IS BACK ON THE RIGHT of the folder name, where it used to be."],t.s)
B.au3=new A.t(716,B.a1n)
B.ZX=s(["THE DUPLICATE ITEMS ARE EXPLAINED AND FIXED. Your 8:54 and 8:55 pair were two real rows with identical text, and the log showed the same 154 characters posted twice \u2014 both at the very start of a session. The guard against re-posting lived only in memory, so an app restart wiped it, and the saved draft was then restored and posted again. Your desktop has been restarting all evening for updates, which is why you kept seeing this and I never did. The draft is now cleared the instant a note posts, and the guard survives a restart.","THE CIRCLE HANDLES ARE GONE FROM FOLDER ITEMS. The bar on each item is the handle everywhere, as it already was on the main screen; folder rows keep a grip because they have no bar."],t.s)
B.avH=new A.t(715,B.ZX)
B.a5P=s(["THE \uff0b CAN MAKE A CLAUDE MESSAGE AGAIN. Two of my own bugs from the other night: a new item created from the \uff0b was saved but never joined the list on screen, and a new Claude message was filed into the Claude folder the instant it was born \u2014 which takes it out of the Inbox, so it vanished as you watched. New items now appear where you made them and stay unfiled until they are sent."],t.s)
B.avG=new A.t(714,B.a5P)
B.a0R=s(["WINDOWS: THE BLINKING CURSOR YOU COULD NOT TYPE INTO. Your desktop log showed the box losing focus to the route\u2019s own scope over and over, with no click behind it \u2014 a teardown stealing the cursor. The box then fell back to its read-only view, which paints a fake caret, so you got a blinking cursor over a field that could not take keys. It now re-claims the cursor when \u2014 and only when \u2014 nothing was clicked. Clicking away still means you left.","THE \uff0b ALWAYS ASKS WHICH TYPE. It creates nothing on its own any more; the menu opens every time, with the type you are viewing at the top of the list.","YOUR EXISTING CLAUDE MESSAGES ARE LINKED INTO THE NEW CLAUDE FOLDER \u2014 a link, not a move, so whatever folder they are already in keeps them too. Runs once, automatically.","THE DICTATE BOX BUTTON NOW SAYS \u201cCREATE DICTATION\u201d."],t.s)
B.aq6=new A.t(713,B.a0R)
B.a4q=s(["A PERMANENT CLAUDE FOLDER IN THE PANE, above the Library rows. Every new Claude item links itself into it, so it works like an inbox for Claude work, and you can build whatever folder structure you want underneath it. It is a real folder \u2014 it also still appears in your folder tree, where dragging and expanding already work. Note: your existing 33 Claude items are still in the old \u201cClaude messages\u201d folder; nothing was moved for you.","FOLDERS CAN GET OUT OF A FOLDER AGAIN. There was no drag anywhere that moved a folder to the top level \u2014 dragging one up to the top dropped it INTO whatever row was there instead, silently. Now: drop a folder on the FOLDERS header to send it to the top level, or use the new \u201cMove to Root\u201d line in its \u22ef menu.","A MOVE TELLS YOU WHERE IT WENT. It used to just say \u201cMoved\u201d. It now names the destination, so a wrong drop is obvious immediately instead of looking like nothing happened."],t.s)
B.auA=new A.t(712,B.a4q)
B.a2S=s(["THE VIEW YOU ARE IN SETS THE TYPE. Press Dictate while the Inbox is filtered to Claude and you get a CLAUDE box \u2014 clay badge and all \u2014 instead of a note you then have to convert. Same for the \uff0b: inside a type view it skips the menu and makes that type on one tap, so you can fire off several in a row. Unfiltered, \uff0b still asks which kind."],t.s)
B.asd=new A.t(711,B.a2S)
B.a73=s(["THE WAY BACK OUT OF A TYPE VIEW. Tapping Notes, Tasks, Meetings or Claude filters the Inbox \u2014 but the header still said \u201cInbox\u201d, both rows lit up in the pane, and tapping Inbox did nothing because you were already on that screen. The header now reads \u201cInbox \u203a Claude\u201d, only the type row lights up, and tapping Inbox clears the filter and takes you back.","THE FULL-SIZE ITEM IS CLEANER. The summary block is gone for now, read-aloud moved to the top right where every other screen keeps it, and the audio player is about 40% shorter \u2014 same controls, far less of the screen on a short note."],t.s)
B.apw=new A.t(710,B.a73)
B.a2L=s(["THE \uff0b WORKS AGAIN \u2014 AND THE INBOX HAS ONE. The folder scrollbar was drawn on top of the \uff0b beside FOLDERS, so the scrollbar was eating your taps; the pane now keeps a gutter and every trailing control there is clickable again. Separately, the Inbox never had a \uff0b at all, so a Claude message could only be made by voice or inside a folder. There is now one at the top of the main screen: note, task, meeting, or Claude message, born with the cursor already in it.","YOU CAN SEE WHERE YOU ARE. The location sits right after the \u2630 \u2014 \u201cInbox\u201d on the main screen, and folder screens keep their name in the bar even after the big title scrolls away.","THE EMPTY BOX INVITES TYPING TOO. With the mic off it now reads \u201cType or press Dictate to start\u201d, because the box takes typing either way."],t.s)
B.atB=new A.t(709,B.a2L)
B.a10=s(["THE MAIN SCREEN SAYS \u201cINBOX\u201d. Every folder already showed its name at the top; the screen you live in showed nothing. Now it is labelled, so it is always obvious where you are.","FIRST ONE THROUGH THE NEW GATE. You dictated this request into a Claude item and pressed Implement \u2014 that approval is what authorized the build, and this line is the proof the loop works end to end."],t.s)
B.ars=new A.t(708,B.a10)
B.a11=s(["CLAUDE ITEMS ARE CONVERSATIONS NOW. A Claude box keeps every answer instead of replacing the last one, so one item holds the whole back-and-forth about a feature or a bug. Tap REPLY to send what is in the box as the next message on that same thread \u2014 dictate the follow-up straight into the box and press Reply.","THE IMPLEMENT BUTTON \u2014 you decide when I build. Every sent Claude item now has an Implement button. Until you press it, I only discuss and plan the item; pressing it (with a confirm, so a mis-tap can not do it) sends a timestamped approval on the thread and turns the button green. Nothing gets built without it.","NAME YOUR CLAUDE ITEMS. Claude items finally have an editable title, so a long conversation is findable by the name you gave it instead of by its first sentence."],t.s)
B.awy=new A.t(707,B.a11)
B.a7o=s(["RE-FLICKS NO LONGER PAUSE. Touching a flying list used to stop it dead for the whole time your finger was down, then start over. Now a finger landing on a fast glide lets it keep coasting while the touch declares itself: flick again and the speeds ADD (no more losing 10% every time), hold still and it stops as it always did.","THE DRAG HOLD GETS ITS SNAP BACK. Holding an item\u2019s bar to drag it now arms in 0.3 seconds instead of the half-second it was raised to this morning during the flick hunt. The real thief turned out to be elsewhere \u2014 and it is caught now \u2014 so the extra caution was just costing you time on every drag.","THE SCROLL WORK IS WRITTEN DOWN. Every mechanism behind the last two days of scrolling fixes is now recorded in the repo so it can never be re-litigated from scratch."],t.s)
B.atI=new A.t(706,B.a7o)
B.a5X=s(["FIXED: FLICKS POPPING THE KEYBOARD. Your diagnosis connected it: when the system fails to read a flick as a scroll \u2014 the same failure behind the missing glides \u2014 the item under your finger takes it as a TAP, focuses for typing, and up comes the keyboard. There is now a referee that cannot be argued with: a gesture that traveled far was never a tap, so any focus it produced is revoked on the spot and the keyboard stays down. Real taps are untouched."],t.s)
B.avg=new A.t(705,B.a5X)
B.YH=s(["FLICKS NOW LAUNCH INSTANTLY AND STACK UP. Correcting yesterday\u2019s conclusion with a better experiment: the stalls hit the middle of the screen too, so the scrollbar was a contributor, not the cause. Two changes built on what the log proves works: the glide now launches ONE FRAME after your finger lifts (it waited far longer before, so your next rapid flick kept catching a list that had not started moving), and repeated flicks ADD \u2014 each flick inherits the speed of the glide it caught, or of the flick before it that never got to launch. Flick-flick-flick builds speed the way it should. A flick in the opposite direction still stops the list dead, as it must."],t.s)
B.arP=new A.t(704,B.YH)
B.a1m=s(["THE FLICK MYSTERY, SOLVED \u2014 IT WAS THE SCROLLBAR. A controlled test on your own phone settled it: identical fast flicks glide perfectly in the middle of the screen and die at the right edge. The scrollbar was INTERACTIVE \u2014 its touch zone runs down the right edge where your thumb naturally flicks, it was claiming those touches for itself, and tapping its track moves exactly ONE SCREEN \u2014 your very words. Every earlier theory tested clean because test swipes ran dead-center. The scrollbar is now display-only: it shows where you are, and every touch belongs to the list. Flick anywhere \u2014 including the edge."],t.s)
B.ar8=new A.t(703,B.a1m)
B.a1P=s(["FIXED: THE APP FREEZING \u2014 my bug, introduced this afternoon. The new touch-quieting checked only ONCE whether it was safe to wake the list back up; if that one moment looked busy, it never checked again, the screen stopped updating forever, and the app read as dead. It now keeps checking until the list is truly at rest, and after three seconds it wakes up no matter what \u2014 a briefly stale row is nothing, a dead screen is everything.","FIXED: FLICKING A MOVING LIST STOPPING IT. Catching a glide does not count as a new scroll internally, so the flick-helper thought the previous fling was still this gesture and refused to help \u2014 that was your repeatable stall. Catch-flicks now get a fresh slate the instant your finger lands. Also: the helper can no longer start a glide while any finger is still on the glass."],t.s)
B.avs=new A.t(702,B.a1P)
B.ZN=s(["THE FLICK FIX, AT THE ROOT. Your report drew the line exactly: glides that start run clean to the end \u2014 only the flick itself dies. A fast flick lasts a twentieth of a second, and the quiet-the-app protection was arming only once scrolling REGISTERED \u2014 which for a flick is after it is already over. So your quickest gesture ran against the busiest moment, its touch readings collapsed into one or two points, and the phone concluded the speed was zero. Now the app goes quiet the INSTANT your finger touches the glass, before anything else can win the race \u2014 so the flick is measured honestly and glides natively. The rescue from the last build stays as a backstop."],t.s)
B.asS=new A.t(701,B.ZN)
B.a2T=s(["QUICK FLICKS FINALLY GLIDE. Your phone\u2019s log gave the answer outright: the swipes that stalled were your FASTEST ones \u2014 up to 8,600 pixels a second \u2014 while the ones that worked were slower and four times longer. Speed was never the problem; DURATION was. Under about a tenth of a second, a flick delivers so few movement readings that the phone cannot work out how fast it went, decides the answer is zero, and starts no glide. The momentum was real and simply got rounded away. The app now measures your flick itself and hands that momentum back when the system throws it out \u2014 so a fast flick travels, which was the whole point of flicking."],t.s)
B.awd=new A.t(700,B.a2T)
B.a7B=s(["SWIPING AGAIN NOW SPEEDS THE LIST UP INSTEAD OF SLOWING IT DOWN. You spotted the real thing: flick a moving list and it got SLOWER. Touching a gliding list stops the glide \u2014 correct \u2014 but the next flick was starting from a standstill, throwing away the speed already there, and a second flick is usually lazier than the first. So hurrying it made it slower, every time. The list now carries that speed into the new flick, the way a well-behaved list should: flick, flick, flick and it accelerates.","THE LINE ABOVE YOUR ITEMS NOW SHOWS THE COUNT instead of the word \u201cEARLIER\u201d. The word only restated what the position already told you; the number answers something real, in the same place."],t.s)
B.apv=new A.t(699,B.a7B)
B.a6M=s(["CLAUDE\u2019S ANSWERS ARE READABLE NOW. The reply was small, faded AND italic \u2014 three separate strikes against the one line you come back to the box to read. It is now full body size in normal text colour, upright, and shows up to six lines instead of three so a real answer is not cut off mid-sentence. The round-trip times got the same treatment: bigger, and no longer the palest grey in the app."],t.s)
B.awl=new A.t(698,B.a6M)
B.a78=s(["THE EMPTY BOX NOW SAYS THE RIGHT THING FOR THE MOMENT YOU ARE IN. Before Dictate is pressed it reads \u201cPress Dictate to start\u201d \u2014 the button is the only thing between you and a note, so that is the one useful instruction. Once you press it, it changes to \u201cSpeak or type\u201d and the two spoken shortcuts appear beneath it. Those shortcuts only show while something is actually listening; \u201csay new note\u201d was never useful advice with the mic off."],t.s)
B.aro=new A.t(697,B.a78)
B.Ze=s(["MEASURING THE STALLED SWIPES PROPERLY. Yesterday\u2019s attempt to record how fast your finger was moving came back as zero on EVERY swipe \u2014 including ones that glided six thousand pixels \u2014 so it was measuring nothing. This build times the gesture itself and works out the speed of its final stretch, which is the number your phone actually uses to decide whether to glide. One more stalled swipe from you and it says plainly whether the momentum is being thrown away, or whether your finger had already come to rest before lifting \u2014 which stops the glide in every app ever made and would mean the remaining stops are not a defect at all."],t.s)
B.avu=new A.t(696,B.Ze)
B.a5a=s(["THE EMPTY BOX NOW SAYS \u201cSPEAK OR TYPE\u201d \u2014 your wording, and better than mine: it names what you do rather than which button to press, and it covers both ways in three words.","Also, one more scroll measurement: the app now records how fast your finger was moving when you lifted it. That single number separates \u201cyou stopped before letting go\u201d, which is normal everywhere, from \u201cthe momentum was thrown away\u201d, which is a bug. The next stalled fling answers it."],t.s)
B.awp=new A.t(695,B.a5a)
B.ZT=s(["THE LIST NOW HOLDS COMPLETELY STILL WHILE YOU SCROLL. Your phone\u2019s new gesture log finally showed the difference: swipes driven by a script on that same phone glide perfectly (a hundred-plus frames of momentum), while YOUR swipes register barely one or two movements and no momentum at all. The list was too busy to feel your finger \u2014 because while the mic or sync is live, the whole stack was rebuilding several times a second, and those rebuilds were landing during your swipe. That only happens when dictation is running, which is why every scripted test of mine looked fine. Now, for the length of a gesture, the list is handed back exactly as it was \u2014 no work at all \u2014 and refreshes the instant your finger lifts. Your dictation box is above the list and keeps running normally throughout."],t.s)
B.at1=new A.t(694,B.ZT)
B.a4X=s(['THE SWIPE THAT BECAME A GRAB. Every item\u2019s colored bar is a drag handle, and it armed after only 220 milliseconds of your finger resting on it \u2014 but a normal scroll often starts with a rest of about that long. When it did, the bar took the gesture as a LIFT and the list simply did not move: your swipe "stopped" the scroll because it never became a scroll. Bars run the full width of every row, so that is most of what a thumb lands on, and it explains the "sometimes" \u2014 it depended on how long you lingered. Holding to drag now takes half a second, the same as press-and-hold everywhere else on your phone. Dragging items to folders is unchanged apart from that extra beat.'],t.s)
B.awO=new A.t(693,B.a4X)
B.a3B=s(['FOUND IT: THE APP WAS KILLING YOUR OWN FLING. Your diagnosis was exactly right \u2014 the swipe brings the scroll to a stop, sometimes. Here is why: when a new item arrived while you were mid-glide (a sync, an AI title, a fresh capture), the app nudged the scroll position to keep your place. That nudge cancels the glide outright \u2014 the list does not slow down, it stops dead. "Sometimes" because it only happened when something landed during that particular swipe. Now nothing may move the list while you are moving it: your finger owns the position, period. The place-keeping still works when your hand is off.'],t.s)
B.auY=new A.t(692,B.a3B)
B.YG=s(["THE SWIPE ITSELF WAS THE PROBLEM \u2014 YOUR HUNCH WAS RIGHT. Two things I had added were firing in the gaps BETWEEN your swipes: the app rebuilt the entire list at the end of every single scroll (to release a hold that keeps rows from shifting under your finger) even when nothing had changed, and its one-time measuring pass was re-running every time you left the screen and came back. Both are gone: the release is free when nothing moved, and the measuring happens once per app start, full stop. The guessing this arc has been chasing is already fixed \u2014 the measurements now show none of it \u2014 so what was left really was my own scaffolding landing under your thumb."],t.s)
B.auS=new A.t(691,B.YG)
B.a5K=s(["THE SCROLLING SEESAW ENDS. Both phones were finally telling the same story: whatever part of the list is not measured yet gets GUESSED, and the guess keeps getting corrected under your finger \u2014 that is the drag at the end on the Fold and the roughness on the S23. One setting could never suit both, because the S23 wraps text harder, so the same items make a much taller list on that screen. Now there is no setting to get wrong: the list opens with a small runway so it appears instantly, then a moment later \u2014 while you are reading the top, never during a scroll \u2014 it quietly measures the whole stack. After that there is nothing left to guess, on either phone."],t.s)
B.arr=new A.t(690,B.a5K)
B.a7Q=s(["SCROLLING: SPLITTING THE DIFFERENCE, WITH MEASUREMENTS. The last build cured the S23 by taking back what had cured the Fold \u2014 the two phones wanted opposite things, because the real cost is per ROW, and the S23 was spending several times longer building each one. Removing the swipe pane made every row lighter for both, so the middle ground is back on trial: enough runway to cover your whole inbox, at a fraction of the burst that choked the S23. Both phones are logging as you scroll, so the next report settles it with numbers rather than another guess."],t.s)
B.asH=new A.t(689,B.a7Q)
B.a_t=s(["SWIPE-TO-DELETE IS GONE from the main list. Deleting now happens where you mean it \u2014 the trash button on the box, or the \u22ef menu. A destructive action should not be one stray sideways finger away on the screen you scroll all day. Bonus: every row got lighter, since each one was carrying a swipe pane it never showed."],t.s)
B.asG=new A.t(688,B.a_t)
B.a2W=s(["TAPPING DICTATE PUTS THE CURSOR IN THE BOX. You can see where your words are going, and if you want to type instead you can start immediately \u2014 no extra tap. The keyboard deliberately does NOT pop up: you pressed Dictate to talk, not to type, so the caret appears and the screen stays whole (tap the box itself and the keyboard opens as always). And if your cursor is already inside an item you are editing, Dictate leaves it there \u2014 your speech keeps going into that item, exactly as before."],t.s)
B.ar0=new A.t(687,B.a2W)
B.a4k=s(["FIXED: SCROLLING STOPPING DEAD ON THE S23. Its own measurements caught what your Fold hid: the last scrolling change asked the app to measure your ENTIRE library in a single frame \u2014 one to two SECONDS of work on that phone, against thirteen milliseconds on the Fold. A fling runs on a clock, so a two-second frame burns the whole glide at once and the scroll simply stops. The app now prepares about three screens ahead instead of everything, which was always the safer answer for any phone that is not your fastest one.","FINISHING AN ITEM NO LONGER THROWS AWAY YOUR PLACE. Tapping \u2713 Done while scrolled down used to yank the list back to the top. The new item still goes in at the top \u2014 but the list stays exactly where you were, with the same words under your eye. (That jump existed so a fresh box would be visible; the dictate box has been pinned above the list for a while now, so it was only ever costing you your place.)"],t.s)
B.apL=new A.t(686,B.a4k)
B.a5Q=s(["CLICKING OUT LEAVES YOUR WORDS ALONE. Tapping an item while the dictate box has words no longer posts it. The box keeps your words and stays live \u2014 come back and it is exactly as you left it. Only \u2713 Done, Enter, saying \u201cnew note\u201d, and Send turn what is in the box into an item. An EMPTY box still evaporates when you click away.","AND THEY SURVIVE A CRASH. Because a note can now stay open indefinitely, the app quietly mirrors the open box to storage as you write; if it is ever killed \u2014 force-quit, battery, crash \u2014 the words are waiting in the box next time you open it.","CLEAR GETS AN UNDO. After you tap Clear, an Undo appears next to it for thirty seconds and puts every word back."],t.s)
B.awJ=new A.t(685,B.a5Q)
B.a5o=s(["SCROLLBAR, TRUED UP. With the thumb finally steady, one old flaw became visible: at the very top of the list the thumb rested a little low, while the bottom was right. The track was quietly reserving space for the phone\u2019s status bar \u2014 even though the list doesn\u2019t sit under it. The track now spans exactly the list it scrolls: top means top."],t.s)
B.axk=new A.t(684,B.a5o)
B.a7G=s(["THE LAST STRETCH OF THE LIST. Round 11 ended the guessing for the first twenty thousand pixels of your inbox \u2014 your verdict: \u201cMUCH better\u201d \u2014 but your list turned out to be longer than the measured runway, so the far end still ran on guesses and the slowdown retreated to the tail. The runway now covers the whole list, whatever its length. Measuring only ever costs what your list actually contains, so short lists pay nothing extra."],t.s)
B.apA=new A.t(683,B.a7G)
B.a4B=s(['GUESSING IS OVER. Round 10\u2019s counter caught the slowdown in the act: flinging past tall items, the list\u2019s idea of its own length was lurching by up to two thousand pixels at a time \u2014 twenty-six corrections in one two-second window. Every "slows down past big items" you felt was those corrections landing mid-glide. The cure: the list now measures essentially your whole inbox up front, so heights are FACTS, not guesses \u2014 no corrections, steady scrollbar, flings that land where your finger aimed. This is only affordable because earlier rounds made settled rows nearly free to keep around. The counter stays on: if it reads zero on your next scroll, this case is closed.'],t.s)
B.atv=new A.t(682,B.a4B)
B.a2U=s(["CHASING THE SLOWDOWN PAST BIG ITEMS. Your report narrowed it to one mechanism: the first time the list scrolls past a tall item it has never measured, it GUESSES the height, then corrects the guess mid-glide \u2014 tiny position fixes that feel like braking even at a perfect frame rate (and exactly why fully collapsed items feel smooth: uniform rows need no guessing). This build counts those corrections on your device as you scroll, and prepares rows even further ahead so first-time measuring happens beyond the screen instead of under your finger. If the counter confirms the story, the lasting fix is teaching the list every item\u2019s REAL height so it never guesses \u2014 that\u2019s the next piece."],t.s)
B.ap9=new A.t(681,B.a2U)
B.YO=s(["THE FREEZE YOU ASKED FOR \u2014 WORD FOR WORD. The measurements settled it: your latest choppy scroll had ZERO slow frames \u2014 the app was painting at full speed while things still felt off. What you were feeling was the CONTENT moving: titles, sync arrivals, and background saves landing mid-swipe and nudging rows under your finger. So, exactly as you prescribed: the moment a scroll starts, the list takes a snapshot and NOTHING may move a settled row while the window is in motion \u2014 everything that arrived lands in one clean update when your finger settles. The live dictation box stays live throughout; your words never wait."],t.s)
B.aqb=new A.t(680,B.YO)
B.ZV=s(["CAUGHT IT \u2014 BY ITS ABSENCE. Round 7 proved no single step is slow: every stopwatch stayed under 25ms while the frame stalled at 204ms. The log\u2019s confession was elsewhere: while you dictate, the app was re-reading your ENTIRE library \u2014 five thousand items \u2014 NINE times in under three seconds, once per saved chunk. No step slow, but tens of thousands of throwaway objects per second, and the cleanup pauses from that churn are exactly the random stalls no stopwatch can catch. Fix: rapid-fire changes now coalesce into at most one library refresh every 0.3 seconds (the first one still lands instantly), and the list reuses one shared index instead of rebuilding lookup tables on every repaint. The churn drops several-fold; the stalls should go with it."],t.s)
B.axe=new A.t(679,B.ZV)
B.a60=s(["THE HUNT, SHARPENED: round 6\u2019s stopwatches came back \u2014 all four suspects innocent (the worst was 14ms; the stall is 200+). So the net widens and tightens at once: the per-word pipeline that runs on every flicker of live speech now carries its own stopwatch, and ANY measured step that takes over 50ms is logged BY NAME with a timestamp the moment it happens, instead of hiding inside a two-second average. The next stall signs its work."],t.s)
B.au2=new A.t(678,B.a60)
B.a4C=s(["THE HUNT NARROWS: round 5\u2019s numbers cleared the list itself \u2014 its worst rebuild is now 13ms \u2014 but caught a single frame elsewhere burning 283ms while dictating. Four named suspects now carry their own stopwatches: re-reading the whole library after each save, weaving a finished chunk into your text, the transcript cleanup pass, and the box\u2019s follow-the-words scroll. The next choppy moment writes their names and times to the log \u2014 the culprit gets fixed by name, not by hunch."],t.s)
B.atf=new A.t(677,B.a4C)
B.a0H=s(["SCROLLING, ROUND FIVE \u2014 FOUND THE TRIGGER. All night the fixes made each refresh cheaper, but the real problem was HOW OFTEN refreshes fire: the whole list redraws on every flicker of the live transcript \u2014 several times per second while the mic is on \u2014 a trigger none of the earlier holds could see. Now a settled item is handed back as the exact same object when nothing about it changed, and the app skips it entirely: those rapid ticks redraw only the live box. Also added: the diagnostics now count how often the list rebuilds and what it costs, so the next report names the culprit with numbers instead of a hunch."],t.s)
B.aw0=new A.t(676,B.a0H)
B.a1G=s(["A MOVING LIST IS ALWAYS CATCHABLE. The last surface that could steal a scroll was the text of the item you are actively editing \u2014 land a finger there while the list was gliding and the text field grabbed the drag. Now, while the list is in motion, even that field yields: your finger catches and moves the LIST, and the field goes back to scrolling its own text the moment the list settles."],t.s)
B.aqD=new A.t(675,B.a1G)
B.a_A=s(["SCROLLING, ROUND FOUR \u2014 THE BIG ONE. The measurements showed round 3 helped the averages but not the spikes, which pointed at the last standing cost: every screen refresh was constructing the FULL visual anatomy of every item in your inbox \u2014 hundreds of items, borders, swipe panes, text processing and all \u2014 then keeping only the handful on screen. Now each row builds itself, lazily, only when it actually scrolls into view \u2014 and things like the cursor ring or selecting an item repaint just that one row instead of the whole list. This is the same idea as your suggestion tonight: while the window moves, settled rows are left alone."],t.s)
B.au7=new A.t(674,B.a_A)
B.a7i=s(["SCROLLING, ROUND THREE \u2014 THE INSTRUMENTS FOUND TWO REAL COSTS. First: every row was re-deriving the ENTIRE sub-item map \u2014 and rows with sub-items built an index of your whole library \u2014 every single time it repainted. That work is now done once and shared by all rows, so a row costs a lookup instead of a rebuild of everything. Second: the list was preparing three screens of rows ahead of your finger, and any change mid-scroll re-prepared ALL of them in one frame \u2014 that burst was the stutter the measurements kept showing. The runway is now sized so the burst fits inside a frame. Same look, same behavior, less work per frame."],t.s)
B.asb=new A.t(673,B.a7i)
B.a0z=s(["SMOOTHER SCROLLING, GUIDED BY THE INSTRUMENTS: your phone\u2019s own frame measurements pointed at one culprit \u2014 every long item was laying out its ENTIRE text just to show ten lines, and those layout spikes were the stutter. Rows now hand layout only what can possibly show. The full text is untouched \u2014 open any item and it\u2019s all there."],t.s)
B.axs=new A.t(672,B.a0z)
B.a33=s(["CHASING THE CHOPPY SCROLLING WITH INSTRUMENTS INSTEAD OF GUESSES: the app now measures its own frame times as you scroll and quietly records where any stutter comes from \u2014 the app\u2019s own layout work, or the phone\u2019s renderer. Costs nothing while things are smooth. One choppy scroll from you and the diagnostics log tells us which fix is the real one."],t.s)
B.avv=new A.t(671,B.a33)
B.a4S=s(["SMOOTHER, STEADIER SCROLLING. Three changes under the hood: list refreshes now WAIT until your finger stops moving instead of landing mid-swipe and re-measuring every row under it; rows are prepared well ahead of the screen so a fast fling doesn\u2019t hitch on each new item; and the live task/meeting boxes no longer grab your swipe \u2014 a drag over them moves the whole list, with their newest words still following along by themselves."],t.s)
B.arl=new A.t(670,B.a4S)
B.a5N=s(["THE DICTATION BOX REALLY SCROLLS NOW. The box was showing a fixed window onto your text \u2014 it could be aimed at the newest words, but it could not be SWIPED, and the aiming itself kept misfiring. It is a true scrolling view now, the same kind the task and meeting boxes have always used: the newest line stays anchored at the bottom while you talk, and you can swipe up any time to read back through everything. No measuring, no guessing \u2014 the anchoring is physics."],t.s)
B.ax7=new A.t(669,B.a5N)
B.a82=s(["THE BOX SCROLLS UP WHEN IT FILLS. Once your words pass the eighth line, the box now follows wherever they are landing \u2014 including when your cursor sits in the MIDDLE of the text. Dictated words were being placed correctly but the view never chased the cursor below the eighth line, so the newest words landed out of sight. The view follows the cursor\u2019s line now; the cursor itself is never moved while you edit."],t.s)
B.arI=new A.t(668,B.a82)
B.XB=s(["THE INBOX SHOWS ITS COUNT \u2014 [n] on the right of the Inbox row in the left pane, in the same style as the type rows under it, always live.","THE DICTATION BOX BREATHES NOW: it rests at two lines, grows with your words up to eight, and after you send it starts fresh at two again. Past eight lines your newest words stay in view and the older ones scroll off the top.","SMOOTHER LIST SCROLLING OVER BIG ITEMS: swiping across an item whose text is longer than its box used to spend the first part of your swipe scrolling the text INSIDE the item before the list would move. At rest, items no longer grab the swipe at all \u2014 the whole window scrolls in one motion. The text inside an item still scrolls when you are actually in it editing, where your cursor needs to travel."],t.s)
B.arT=new A.t(667,B.XB)
B.a_1=s(["FIXED: AI TITLES VANISHING SECONDS AFTER THEY APPEARED. While your cursor was in a box, the app held back list refreshes \u2014 and when the cursor left, it replayed the held one. But that replay was a snapshot from BEFORE your title arrived, so it painted the title right back off. The app now re-reads the latest data instead of replaying anything old, so what lands stays.","FIXED: TAPPING THE DICTATION BOX SOMETIMES DID NOTHING until you had clicked an item first. An earlier \u201coptimization\u201d of mine skipped part of the tap\u2019s work when no item held the cursor \u2014 turns out that part was carrying the hand-off. It runs every time again."],t.s)
B.aqm=new A.t(666,B.a_1)
B.a5m=s(["FIXED: THE KEYBOARD CLOSED AFTER YOUR FIRST TYPED CHARACTER. The last build drew the empty-box instructions as a layer that existed only while the box was empty \u2014 so your first letter tore that layer down, and the typing area was rebuilt out from under your keyboard. The layer is permanent now and just goes invisible when words exist. Typing is back to normal."],t.s)
B.ap7=new A.t(665,B.a5m)
B.Z7=s(["NO MORE \u201cSPLIT IN 2 ITEMS\u201d WHEN YOU SAY NEW PARAGRAPH. That offer exists for pasted documents \u2014 several notes arriving at once. Since the blank-line fix, saying \u201cnew paragraph\u201d looked like a paste to it. It now watches HOW the paragraphs arrived, not how the text looks: only a real paste \u2014 a whole document landing in one jump \u2014 brings the offer up.","TAPPING AN EMPTY DICTATION BOX NO LONGER SHIFTS. The instructions and the typing area used to be two different pieces of text that swapped places when you tapped in. They are one piece now, drawn in the same spot whether you are typing or not.","AND THE TWO-LINE JUMP IS GONE: with text exactly the height of the box, the app could not decide whether it overflowed, and the answer flipped as you tapped around \u2014 changing which line you saw. It now requires half a line of REAL overflow before anything moves, and text always wraps in whole lines, so the answer can never sit on the fence again."],t.s)
B.awr=new A.t(664,B.Z7)
B.a5D=s(["THE TEXT NO LONGER JUMPS WHEN YOU TAP THE DICTATION BOX. Before you tap, the box shows the END of your words. Tapping handed over to a real text field that started from the top \u2014 same words, different place, which is what you were seeing move. The field now opens exactly where you were already looking, with the cursor at the end."],t.s)
B.atS=new A.t(663,B.a5D)
B.a1j=s(["TAPPING THE DICTATION BOX IS QUIETER. The last build told your items to release the cursor every single time you tapped the box \u2014 even when none of them had it. That was needless work on the control you touch most. It now only speaks up when an item actually holds the cursor."],t.s)
B.avi=new A.t(662,B.a1j)
B.a7C=s(["SMOOTHER SCROLLING, AND NO MORE TEXT JUMPING WHEN THE KEYBOARD OPENS. The dictation box was re-measuring its text on every single frame \u2014 every frame of a scroll, and every frame of the keyboard sliding in and out. That is why the words shifted when the keyboard appeared and shifted back when it left, and part of why the list felt like it was fighting you. It now measures only when the words themselves change, so nothing can re-settle while the screen is already moving."],t.s)
B.asZ=new A.t(661,B.a7C)
B.a3t=s(["THE GRID NO LONGER JUMPS WHEN YOU TOUCH SOMETHING. While your cursor is in a box, Voicley holds back any rebuild of your item list so it cant yank the page under you \u2014 but those held rebuilds were piling up and then all landing at once the next time you tapped or deleted something, which is why the grid seemed to shift for no reason. The held rebuild now lands when you STOP typing, a moment where a little movement is expected, instead of ambushing your next tap."],t.s)
B.aqq=new A.t(660,B.a3t)
B.a7L=s(["THE \u2922 EXPAND BUTTON IS GONE FROM THE DICTATION BOX. Opening a note full-size is something you want for a note you have finished, not for the couple of lines you are still saying \u2014 and it is one tap away the moment the note posts."],t.s)
B.apM=new A.t(659,B.a7L)
B.Zy=s(["TAPPING THE DICTATION BOX NOW TAKES THE CURSOR BACK. If you had clicked into one of your items, the cursor got stuck there \u2014 tapping the dictation box did nothing, and the only way out was collapsing the item. The box now asks the item to let go first, the same way saying \u201cnew note\u201d always has."],t.s)
B.aq2=new A.t(658,B.Zy)
B.a_b=s(["THE APP NOW CHECKS ITS OWN UPDATER AT STARTUP. Tonight\u2019s \u201cupdate didn\u2019t start\u201d was a missing piece of plumbing that nothing noticed until you pressed the button three times. Voicley now tests that piece quietly every launch, and if it is ever missing again it says so right next to the Check for updates button \u2014 with what to do about it \u2014 instead of letting you find out the hard way."],t.s)
B.apJ=new A.t(657,B.a_b)
B.Zq=s(["FIXED: \u201cUPDATE DIDN\u2019T START\u201d. The in-app updater was downloading the new version perfectly and then failing silently at the very last step \u2014 handing the file to Android to install. A piece of the app\u2019s plumbing had gone missing from recent builds, so the install screen never opened. This build is a clean rebuild with that plumbing restored, and updates work from here on."],t.s)
B.atp=new A.t(656,B.Zq)
B.a0d=s(["THE AUTO-MEETING IS GONE FOR GOOD. A dictation that ran past a few minutes used to turn itself into a Meeting \u2014 and because a meeting owns the live text, your dictation box would silently disappear mid-session. That is what was happening when the box \u201cvanished\u201d. Nothing was ever lost; your words carried on into the meeting. The whole feature is now removed, along with its setting.","If you WANT a meeting, say \u201cnew meeting\u201d or make one from the left \u2014 the app just will not decide it for you any more."],t.s)
B.atT=new A.t(655,B.a0d)
B.a0f=s(["THE DICTATION BOX IS DOWN TO TWO LINES \u2014 as small as it can get while still showing the line you are speaking and the one before it, which is what lets you notice a wrong word as it lands. Everything else on screen is your items.","At that height the two command hints no longer fit, so the empty box just says \u201cPress Dictate or type in box\u201d. The commands are still listed in Settings."],t.s)
B.aqM=new A.t(654,B.a0f)
B.a1E=s(["YOUR WORDS START AT THE TOP OF THE BOX AGAIN. Since the box became a fixed height, short text was sitting at the BOTTOM of it with empty space above \u2014 so it looked like your dictation started halfway down. It now starts at the top and grows downward like any editor, and only once it fills the box does it stick to the bottom so your newest words stay in view.","THE DICTATION BOX IS SMALLER \u2014 three lines instead of seven, giving most of the screen back to your list of items.","THE INSTRUCTIONS IN THE EMPTY BOX ARE TWICE THE SIZE, and shorter: \u201cPress Dictate or type in box\u201d, \u201cSay \u2018new note\u2019\u201d, \u201cSay \u2018new task\u2019\u201d. It says Dictate now, matching the button."],t.s)
B.awU=new A.t(653,B.a1E)
B.a57=s(["BIGGER BUTTONS: Off \xb7 Commands only \xb7 Dictate are a quarter taller, and so is the \u2713 Done button \u2014 easier to hit without giving back the space we saved.","A QUIETER DICTATION BOX: the date and time are gone from the top of it (you know when you are talking; every saved item still keeps its own timestamp), and the \u201c\u2192 project name\u201d label is gone from the bottom left. The line above the box still speaks up when your words are heading somewhere you would NOT expect \u2014 which is the only time it was telling you anything.","NO MORE COMMAND CHIPS WHILE YOU TALK: the \u201cnew note\u201d / \u201cnew task\u201d / \u201cend dictation\u201d buttons no longer appear at the top when dictation starts. They showed up exactly when you were mid-sentence and pushed the box you were watching down the screen. The empty box now teaches those commands before you start, which is when you can actually read them."],t.s)
B.asC=new A.t(652,B.a57)
B.a2f=s(["SAYING \u201cNEW PARAGRAPH\u201d NOW SHOWS THE BLANK LINE IMMEDIATELY. The paragraph was always being saved correctly \u2014 you just couldn\u2019t SEE it until your next word arrived, because an empty last line had nothing in it to draw. Now the gap appears the moment you say it, with the cursor sitting on it.","THE EMPTY DICTATION BOX TELLS YOU WHAT TO DO: press Dictate or type in the box, then talk. It also names the two commands worth knowing on day one \u2014 \u201cnew note\u201d to finish this one and start another, and \u201cnew task\u201d to make a task instead. It all disappears the instant your first word lands."],t.s)
B.awj=new A.t(651,B.a2f)
B.WX=s(["THE DICTATION BOX IS A FIXED SIZE NOW \u2014 SEVEN LINES, EMPTY OR FULL. It used to grow from one line upward as you spoke, which pushed everything below it down the screen right when you started talking. Now it is already the size it is going to be, so nothing moves when your first words land.","SCROLLING THROUGH A LONG ITEM NO LONGER DEAD-ENDS: if an item is tall enough to scroll inside itself, reaching its top or bottom used to stop your finger cold \u2014 you had to lift off and swipe again to move the list. Now the leftover motion carries straight through into the list, so one swipe keeps going from inside the item out into the rest of your notes. (The live recording box has worked this way since July; saved items just never got it.)"],t.s)
B.aup=new A.t(650,B.WX)
B.a_9=s(["THE DICTATION BOX CAN\u2019T DISAPPEAR ANY MORE: it used to be one more entry in the same list as your items, so every time that list rebuilt itself \u2014 and while your phone catches up on old recordings it rebuilds constantly \u2014 the box could vanish until something forced a repaint. Pulling down to refresh \u201cfixed\u201d it because refreshing repaints. The box now lives ABOVE the list instead of inside it, where nothing that happens to your items can touch it. It also stays put while the list scrolls under it.","Your words were never at risk in any of this \u2014 the box going missing was a drawing problem, not a saving one."],t.s)
B.awZ=new A.t(649,B.a_9)
B.a6T=s(["THE COMMAND BUTTONS ARE HALF THE HEIGHT: Off \xb7 Commands only \xb7 Dictate now put their icon BESIDE the word instead of above it, so the control is a slim bar across the top of the dictation box rather than a row of tiles. Same words, same full-width tap targets \u2014 half the space.","THE APP NOW TELLS YOU WHEN IT\u2019S USING A WORSE MICROPHONE: if Voicley\u2019s own dictation engine can\u2019t start, it quietly switches to the one built into your browser or phone so you still get words. That switch used to be invisible \u2014 which is why dictating in a browser looked like the app was broken, when really it was your browser\u2019s speech recognition dropping and repeating words. Now it says so, and in a browser it points you at the installed app, which has the full engine."],t.s)
B.aqG=new A.t(648,B.a6T)
B.a24=s(["THE SCREEN HOLDS STILL WHILE YOU TALK: Off \xb7 Commands only \xb7 Dictate moved to the TOP of the dictation box. They were underneath it, so every new line you spoke pushed the buttons \u2014 and everything else \u2014 further down the screen. Now your words grow away from the controls instead of through them, and the buttons stay exactly where your thumb left them.","AND THE BOX STOPS GROWING: the dictation box now tops out at six lines instead of ten. Past that it keeps your NEWEST words in view and lets the older ones scroll off the top, so your list of items never gets shoved down the screen mid-sentence. Nothing is trimmed \u2014 tap into the box, or open the item, to see all of it.","THE BOX YOU TALK INTO LOOKS LIKE A TOOL: it now sits raised off the page, and a quiet \u201cEARLIER\u201d line separates it from the items below. One is the thing that makes notes; the rest are notes you already made.","THE ITEM MIC MOVED ONTO ITS BAR: when you click into an item, its \u201cDictate here\u201d button now sits on the item\u2019s coloured top bar instead of under the text \u2014 so turning it on no longer makes the item taller and nudges everything below it."],t.s)
B.av3=new A.t(647,B.a24)
B.a_D=s(["PRESSING DICTATE IS SAFE AGAIN \u2014 REALLY THIS TIME: v645 fixed one cause of the grey screen; testing on a real device found a second. The animated red dot I\u2019d put on the Dictate button couldn\u2019t survive the button moving into the dictation box, and it crashed the display when your words arrived. The dot is gone: the Dictate button simply turns RED while the mic is live, heating toward orange as you speak. Same signal, nothing to break.","LIGHTER ITEM BARS: the coloured bar across the top of each item is half as strong, so it still tells you the type at a glance without competing with your words.","QUIETER EDITING: two internal errors that fired when you clicked out of an item being edited are fixed. Your text always saved correctly \u2014 these only made noise in the logs."],t.s)
B.ava=new A.t(646,B.a_D)
B.a1d=s(["FIXED: PRESSING DICTATE GREYED OUT THE WHOLE SCREEN. Two builds ago I moved the red \u201cmic on\u201d marker onto the Dictate button, and to do that I had the floating red pill ask which screen you were on \u2014 but that pill sits outside the part of the app that knows the answer, so asking crashed the display the instant recording started. It now gets told by the screen instead of asking. Your recording and your items were never affected \u2014 only the drawing stopped."],t.s)
B.aqp=new A.t(645,B.a1d)
B.a6m=s(["ONE BOX, AND IT OWNS THE MIC: \u201cSay or type your text here\u201d is now inside the real dictation box \u2014 the separate card above it is gone. And Off \xb7 Commands only \xb7 Dictate moved INSIDE that box too, just under \u2713 Done. The box that takes your words now holds the controls that steer them, so they can\u2019t drift apart.","A MIC ON THE ITEM YOU\u2019RE EDITING: click into any item\u2019s text and a mic button appears under it. Tap it and your dictation goes into THAT item; tap it again and your words go back to the new-note box \u2014 the mic doesn\u2019t stop, it moves. Dictating into an existing item is now something you press, not something a stray cursor decides for you."],t.s)
B.asE=new A.t(644,B.a6m)
B.a_6=s(["LESS ON THE MAIN SCREEN: Notes, Tasks, Meetings and Claude now live on the LEFT, right under Inbox \u2014 each with its count in brackets and a \uff0b to make one. Tap a row to see just that type. The filter row and the create buttons are gone from the inbox screen entirely, so it\u2019s now the dictation box and your items, nothing else.","THE BOX SAYS WHAT IT\u2019S FOR: \u201cSay or type your text here\u201d in faded text, and talking simply makes a new note \u2014 no label above it announcing that. The \u201c\u2192 New note\u201d line under the controls is gone too; it only appears now when your words are going somewhere you might NOT expect, like into an existing item or when the mic is in commands-only mode."],t.s)
B.awS=new A.t(643,B.a_6)
B.Zc=s(["FIXED: ONLY ONE ITEM SHOWING, WITH A GREY BLOCK BELOW IT. A change I made two builds ago put an internal marker on every row in the list; those markers collide when the list redraws, and the rest of the list fails to draw \u2014 which a release build paints as a plain grey box. That\u2019s why you could only reach one item and nothing would scroll, on Windows and in the browser alike. The markers are gone. Nothing was ever wrong with your items \u2014 they were all there the whole time, just not being drawn."],t.s)
B.ark=new A.t(642,B.Zc)
B.a1C=s(["THE RED \u201cMIC ON\u201d MARKER MOVED INTO THE DICTATE BUTTON: instead of a floating red pill hovering over the main screen, the Dictate button itself now wears the pulsing red dot while the microphone is live. The floating pill still appears on every OTHER screen \u2014 Settings, a folder, the reader \u2014 because there the button isn\u2019t visible and that pill is the only thing that can tell you the mic is still hot."],t.s)
B.aq1=new A.t(641,B.a1C)
B.a_8=s(["GESTURES NOW FOLLOW YOUR POINTER, NOT YOUR DEVICE LABEL: the app used to guess how you were touching it from what kind of machine it thought it was on \u2014 which is wrong in a browser, where a tablet in desktop mode claims to be a PC. That guess is what made folders unscrollable on your tablet. It now watches what\u2019s actually touching the screen: a finger gets touch behaviour, a mouse gets mouse behaviour, and a browser assumes touch until it sees otherwise. Dragging a folder with a real mouse is instant again, while a finger swipe scrolls."],t.s)
B.avX=new A.t(640,B.a_8)
B.ZA=s(["FOLDERS SCROLL BY FINGER IN THE BROWSER \u2014 THE REAL FIX: v638 wasn\u2019t enough. Every folder row was set up so a straight up-or-down drag picks the folder up to reorder it \u2014 safe with a mouse (the wheel scrolls) but on a tablet that IS your scroll gesture, so the list never got it. In the browser, reordering a folder now needs a short hold first, which leaves plain finger swipes free to scroll.","THE DICTATE BUTTON IS THE MIC METER: the button now breathes with your voice \u2014 brighter and hotter the louder you speak \u2014 and turns red saying \u201cMic lost\u201d if the microphone is taken by another app. The bar meter and \u201cMic active\u201d strip underneath are gone, giving that space back to your items.","ONE PLACE TO CREATE: the \uff0b has left the filter row. Making a new note, task, meeting or Claude message now lives only in the dictation box at the top \u2014 one door instead of two."],t.s)
B.atX=new A.t(639,B.ZA)
B.a6z=s(["SCROLLING WITH YOUR FINGER WORKS IN THE BROWSER: on a large Android tablet, Chrome reports finger touches as a MOUSE \u2014 and the web app only drag-scrolled for touch, so lists like the folder pane simply refused to move under your finger. The web app now drag-scrolls for every kind of pointer. Phone, Windows and iOS builds are unchanged."],t.s)
B.apF=new A.t(638,B.a6z)
B.a5A=s(["\u201cNEW PARAGRAPH\u201d NOW MAKES A REAL BLANK LINE: while you\u2019re dictating into an existing item, saying \u201cnew paragraph\u201d gave you a single line break \u2014 you had to close the item to see the blank line appear. The open item now shows the blank line as you speak it, exactly like the closed one. Paragraphs are still saved the same way underneath, so OneNote keeps splitting them correctly."],t.s)
B.awq=new A.t(637,B.a5A)
B.a38=s(["THE MAIN SCREEN NOW READS TOP-DOWN: Dictate/Commands/Off has moved to the TOP, with the destination chip and status line right under it, then the composer, then your filters and items. Everything that explains what the app is doing now sits where your eyes land first instead of at the bottom edge.","THE COMPOSER IS A REAL CARD: \u201cTalk or type your next item\u2026\u201d now sits in its own framed box with the \uff0b Note \xb7 Task \xb7 Meeting \xb7 Claude buttons on its footer \u2014 the visible birthplace of items. It glows with an accent border while your words are heading into a new item."],t.s)
B.ata=new A.t(636,B.a38)
B.a4y=s(["YOU CAN ALWAYS SEE WHERE YOUR WORDS ARE GOING: a destination chip now sits right above the Dictate/Commands/Off control and always answers the one question that matters \u2014 \u201c\u2192 New note\u201d, \u201c\u2192 \u2018Cash flow ideas\u2019\u201d when you\u2019re adding to an existing item, or \u201ccommands only\u201d. Beside it, one plain sentence says what the app is doing right now.","THE ITEM YOU\u2019RE DICTATING INTO SAYS SO: when your words are going into an existing item, that item\u2019s colored bar reads \u201cdictating here\u201d (and \u201cpaused here\u201d in commands mode). Dictating into an existing item was always possible \u2014 now it\u2019s obvious.","CREATING AND FILTERING NO LONGER LOOK ALIKE: the counts row is labelled VIEW and is purely a filter. Creating lives on its own strip below it \u2014 \u201cTalk or type your next item\u2026\u201d with \uff0b Note \xb7 Task \xb7 Meeting \xb7 Claude pills. Tap the line or a pill to start one; the \uff0b menu still works exactly as before.","THE APP TEACHES ITSELF: an empty inbox now shows the loop in three lines (turn on \u2192 talk \u2192 say \u201cnew note\u201d between thoughts), and while you dictate, small hints show the voice commands. The hints retire themselves after a few sessions, or immediately if you tap the \u2715."],t.s)
B.asY=new A.t(635,B.a4y)
B.a2Z=s(["SELECTED COLLAPSED ITEMS AREN\u2019T DARK ANYMORE: selecting a collapsed item used to tint the whole row a murky blue \u2014 the selection outline was showing through the row\u2019s translucent bar. The bar now goes solid while selected, so you get a crisp blue outline and the row keeps its normal brightness."],t.s)
B.aso=new A.t(634,B.a2Z)
B.a_Z=s(["CREATE BUTTONS NOW OWN YOUR WORDS: on web and Windows, pressing \u201cnew task\u201d or \u201cnew note\u201d while your cursor sat inside an existing item could send your dictation into THAT item \u2014 and then deliver the same words a second time when the unit closed. Pressing a create button now releases the old item\u2019s claim and pulls the cursor out, so the box you just created is always where your words land. Clicking into an item to dictate there still works exactly as before."],t.s)
B.asM=new A.t(633,B.a_Z)
B.a2k=s(["ONE HANDLE EVERYWHERE \u2014 THE CIRCLES ARE GONE: Windows and web now work like mobile. Every item\u2019s colored bar shows the grip dots and is the handle: drag it to file or reorder, double-click to open. Boxes get the full width the circle column used to take.","CLICK A BAR TO SELECT: clicking an item\u2019s bar selects it (the whole box outlines in blue). Ctrl+click adds or removes one item; SHIFT+CLICK selects everything between your last click and this one, just like Windows Explorer; Ctrl+Shift+click adds that range. On the phone, taps on bars now toggle items in and out of the selection \u2014 multi-select is back on the main screen. Headphones, the fold arrow, and the other bar buttons keep doing their own jobs.","COLLAPSED ITEMS \u2014 CLICK TO SELECT, CLICK AGAIN TO RENAME: the first click on a collapsed item selects it; click the title of an already-selected item to edit it in place \u2014 the same rhythm as renaming a file in Explorer. Double-click still expands."],t.s)
B.aps=new A.t(632,B.a2k)
B.a6w=s(["AUTO-MEETING MOVED TO ADVANCED: the \u201cAuto-meeting after N minutes\u201d setting now lives in Settings \u2192 ADVANCED with its own card, next to segmentation \u2014 it\u2019s a set-once option, so the main settings stay lean. Behavior unchanged."],t.s)
B.aqK=new A.t(631,B.a6w)
B.a_C=s(["PLAYING A FOLDER STARTS AT THE TOP: playback used an internal stored order instead of the order on your screen, so a folder you\u2019d never hand-reordered could start reading a couple of items down. Folder play now follows the screen exactly \u2014 newest-first by default, your dragged order once you\u2019ve reordered \u2014 subfolders first, then items, every folder by its own setting.","THE LISTENING PANEL\u2019S JUMP LANDS ON THE ITEM: tapping the panel to go to what\u2019s playing could drop you at the TOP of the main screen when the item sat far down the inbox. The list now travels to the item wherever it is and settles right on the glowing box."],t.s)
B.atb=new A.t(630,B.a_C)
B.Xe=s(["PLAYING THE INBOX FOLLOWS ALONG: the main screen now tracks the reader the same way folders do \u2014 the item being read glows blue and the list scrolls to keep it in view as playback moves down your inbox. Works for any playback that lands on an inbox item, not just the \u25b6 button.","THE LISTENING AREA\u2019S TOP PANEL ALWAYS TAKES YOU TO WHAT\u2019S PLAYING: tapping the panel already jumped to the item when it was part of your listening list \u2014 now, when the reader is playing something that isn\u2019t in that list (the inbox, or a folder played directly), the tap takes you to where the item actually lives: its folder, or the main screen for an inbox item, landing right on the glowing box."],t.s)
B.aqf=new A.t(629,B.Xe)
B.YK=s(["DELETING WHAT\u2019S PLAYING NOW STOPS IT: listening to a folder and deleting that folder used to keep right on playing from a snapshot. The reader now watches the library \u2014 delete the folder you\u2019re listening to and playback stops; delete a single item mid-listen and it skips to the next one. Unrelated changes (titles, listen flags) never interrupt playback.","PLAY THE INBOX: a \u25b6 button at the top-right of the main screen reads your inbox top-down \u2014 the list exactly as you see it, newest first. Same rules as playing a folder: it ends a live dictation first, loops at the end, and plays each item\u2019s recording (or reads it aloud when there\u2019s no audio)."],t.s)
B.apj=new A.t(628,B.YK)
B.a0_=s(["YOUR OLD UNFILED NOTES ARE IN ONE PLACE NOW: the flood of items that appeared after the visibility fix turned out to be weeks of real notes an old filter had been silently hiding. They\u2019ve all been filed into a new \u201cBacklog\u201d folder at the bottom of your folder list \u2014 nothing deleted, everything searchable, review them whenever you like. Your inbox is back to just current items.","EVERY NOTE HAS A PROPER TYPE AGAIN: nearly all of your items had been saved with a blank internal type (the hidden cause of this whole episode). All 3,800+ have been repaired to proper notes \u2014 so the Notes/Tasks/Meetings chips count them correctly \u2014 and new items are always typed from now on."],t.s)
B.avj=new A.t(627,B.a0_)
B.a6b=s(["THE EMPTY \u201cTRANSCRIBING\u2026\u201d BOXES ARE GONE: yesterday\u2019s fix stopped real notes from being hidden, but it also surfaced some old empty shells \u2014 zero-length captures with nothing in them \u2014 showing a permanent \u201cTranscribing\u2026\u201d spinner. The inbox now decides what to show by CONTENT: anything with words shows, a recent capture still being transcribed shows, and a zero-length shell (nothing was ever spoken) doesn\u2019t appear. Nothing is deleted \u2014 real notes can never be hidden by this rule, because text always shows.","THE SPINNER STOPS LYING: a capture still waiting on its transcription after 10 minutes no longer shows a spinner as if work is happening \u2014 it says \u201cWaiting to transcribe \u2014 needs a connection\u201d with a clock. Honest, and your audio is safe the whole time; it transcribes when it gets a connection."],t.s)
B.arz=new A.t(626,B.a6b)
B.a4z=s(["FIXED: NEW ITEMS DISAPPEARING FROM THE INBOX. A note would show up when you hit Done and then quietly vanish a moment later, and the item count never went up. The inbox was only willing to display items whose internal type was one of four exact values \u2014 and items were being saved with a blank type, so the next background refresh rebuilt the list without them. Your words were never lost (they were on the server the whole time), but they were invisible. The inbox no longer filters by type at all: anything you capture shows up, and an unfamiliar type is simply drawn as a plain note."],t.s)
B.aqr=new A.t(625,B.a4z)
B.Zx=s(["VOICLEY NOW SUGGESTS WHERE A NOTE BELONGS: when a new note clearly fits one of your folders, its box shows a small chip in the bottom-left \u2014 \u201cStrategy \u203a Q3 ?\u201d. Tap it and the note files itself there. Tap the \u2715 and the suggestion is gone for good on that item. Ignore it and nothing happens. NOTHING EVER MOVES ON ITS OWN \u2014 the suggestion just sits there until you say so. It only ever suggests folders you already have, stays quiet when it isn\u2019t sure, and costs nothing extra: it rides along with the AI title call that already runs. Turn it off any time in Settings \u2192 \u201cSuggest folders for new items\u201d."],t.s)
B.asj=new A.t(624,B.Zx)
B.Yi=s(["DRAG AN ITEM LEFT TO REACH YOUR FOLDERS: hold an item by its colored bar and push it toward the LEFT EDGE of the screen \u2014 pause for a moment and the folder menu slides open, still carrying the item, ready to drop on any folder. The whole edge is the target, so there\u2019s nothing to aim at and no need to travel up to the \u2630 (which still works too). Carry the item back toward the list and the menu closes again."],t.s)
B.axh=new A.t(623,B.Yi)
B.Yw=s(["SEARCH IS HERE: there\u2019s a Search row in the menu now. Type a word or two and every item carrying them appears \u2014 notes, tasks, meetings, anywhere in your library, including items you\u2019ve filed away in folders. Results show the matching sentence so you can see why each one turned up, and the type chips (All / Notes / Tasks / Meetings) narrow them down with counts. It searches on the device, so it\u2019s instant and works with no signal. Best match first, then newest \u2014 because with dictation, the recent one is usually the one you meant."],t.s)
B.axc=new A.t(622,B.Yw)
B.a6V=s(["LONG ITEMS EDIT IN PLACE \u2014 NO MORE JUMP TO FULL SCREEN: tapping an item too long to fit used to fling you into the full-size view. Now it edits where it sits, like every other item: the box shows its ten lines and the text SCROLLS inside, so you can read and change anything past the cap without leaving the list. Adding text scrolls rather than growing the box. Full-size is still one double-tap of the item\u2019s colored bar away.","PAUSE ON A FOLDER WHILE DRAGGING AND IT OPENS: hold a dragged item \u2014 or another folder \u2014 over a folder in the left pane for half a second and its subfolders unfold, so you can drill in and drop into a child without ever putting the item down. Sweeping past a folder on the way somewhere else leaves it closed."],t.s)
B.atG=new A.t(621,B.a6V)
B.a7I=s(["CARRY AN ITEM BACK TO THE LIST AND THE MENU CLOSES ITSELF: the drag now runs the menu in both directions. Pause an item on the \u2630 and the folder menu opens; carry it back over the list and the menu slides shut so you can keep dragging where you were. Reach your folders, change your mind, come back \u2014 one motion, no taps. (Both directions need a brief pause, so the menu never flickers open and shut while you move.)"],t.s)
B.apd=new A.t(620,B.a7I)
B.Y8=s(["THE APP NO LONGER FREEZES ON A DRAG: v613\u2019s one-time ordering repair tried to renumber every item in your library in a single go \u2014 with 800+ items that locked the app up for as long as it took, with nothing on screen to say why. The repair now works in small batches: it fixes a slice per drag and finishes over a few drags, so nothing ever stalls. If you were stuck, force-closing and reopening was safe \u2014 nothing was lost."],t.s)
B.at0=new A.t(619,B.Y8)
B.a_m=s(["DRAG AN ITEM TO THE \u2630 MENU TO FILE IT IN A FOLDER (phones): hold an item by its colored bar, drag it up onto the \u2630 at the top-left, and pause \u2014 the menu slides open while you\u2019re still holding the item, and you can drop it straight onto any folder to move it there. This is the piece that was missing on phones: your folders live inside that menu, and the menu was shut for the whole drag, so there was nothing to drop onto. Hold for about half a second (a quick brush past won\u2019t open it), and dragging away closes it again with nothing moved."],t.s)
B.asU=new A.t(618,B.a_m)
B.a7v=s(["HOLDING AN ITEM NEAR THE BOTTOM NO LONGER RUNS THE LIST AWAY: picking up an item close to the top or bottom edge started scrolling the list on its own, before you\u2019d moved a finger. Those edges are scroll zones for when you\u2019re dragging something toward off-screen items \u2014 but they switched on the moment you lifted, and if your finger was already sitting in one, it just took off. They now wait until you actually drag toward the edge."],t.s)
B.aqL=new A.t(617,B.a7v)
B.a6_=s(["THE \u201cIT GOES HERE\u201d LINE IS BACK \u2014 AND NOTHING FLASHES: v615 removed the drop bars along with the gaps that were shoving the list around, which left you dragging blind. A bright line now appears on the edge of the item you\u2019re hovering \u2014 along its top if the item will land above it, along its bottom if below \u2014 drawn inside the item, so still nothing shifts. The flash when dropping on Windows is gone too: hovering used to rebuild the row from scratch, and now it only changes colour."],t.s)
B.asV=new A.t(616,B.a6_)
B.YD=s(["THE LIST NO LONGER JUMPS WHEN YOU PICK AN ITEM UP: holding an item used to shove the whole screen down a couple of lines, because a drop-gap opened between EVERY pair of items at once the moment the drag armed. Those gaps are gone. Instead, while you drag, the item you hover over lights up \u2014 drop on its top half to go above it, its bottom half to go below. Nothing shifts, the spacing stays tight, and the target is a whole item instead of a thin line."],t.s)
B.awu=new A.t(615,B.YD)
B.a5f=s(["HOLDING AN ITEM NO LONGER JERKS THE LIST AROUND: press-and-hold on the phone was starting TWO drags at once \u2014 the list trying to lift the row for a reorder, and the item\u2019s colored bar trying to lift it to move \u2014 and the two fought over your finger the whole time. Now the bar is the only thing that lifts: hold it, drag, and drop on the line between two items to place it there, or sideways onto a folder to file it. Smooth, one motion."],t.s)
B.as6=new A.t(614,B.a5f)
B.YV=s(["ORDERING, PROPERLY FIXED THIS TIME: v612 made a drag write only the item you moved, but most items had never been given a place in the ordering at all \u2014 and a few older ones shared the exact same place. Items with no place always sort above the ones that have one, so a dropped item still jumped. Your first drag now quietly gives every item its own place, in the order they\u2019re already sitting on screen (so nothing visibly moves), and separates the duplicates. After that, drops stay put."],t.s)
B.avS=new A.t(613,B.YV)
B.Xt=s(["ITEMS NOW STAY WHERE YOU DROP THEM: reordering could land an item somewhere other than the spot you dropped it on \u2014 sometimes right away, sometimes only after the list refreshed. Saving a new order used to renumber every item on screen, which quietly clashed with the items NOT on screen (anything hidden by a Notes/Tasks/Meetings filter, filed into a folder, or brand new). Now a drag writes only the item you moved, slotted between its two neighbours \u2014 so nothing else shifts and the order holds, on every device."],t.s)
B.arj=new A.t(612,B.Xt)
B.a3z=s(["\u201cNEW NOTE\u201d ACTUALLY WORKS NOW WHEN THE ENGINE MIS-HEARS YOU: saying \u201cnew note\u201d while driving often came back from the speech engine as \u201cnew notes\u201d, \u201cno note\u201d, or \u201cno notes\u201d \u2014 and every one of those was typed into your note as words instead of starting a fresh one. All of those now start a new note, as do \u201cnext notes\u201d and \u201cnew dictations\u201d. Talking ABOUT a note (\u201cI put a new note in the drawer\u201d) still doesn\u2019t trigger it."],t.s)
B.apY=new A.t(611,B.a3z)
B.a5V=s(["WINDOWS: YOU CAN DRAG AN ITEM BY ITS COLORED BAR TOO: v609 gave every item a tinted bar with a grip on it, but on the PC only the little circle actually dragged \u2014 so the bar looked grabbable and did nothing. Now grab either one: drag the bar sideways (or the circle, unchanged) to file an item into a folder. Dragging straight up or down still reorders, and click-dragging inside the text still selects text."],t.s)
B.ax4=new A.t(610,B.a5V)
B.a7F=s(["WINDOWS: NEW ITEMS SHOW UP AGAIN (and the cursor stops slipping): on the PC the app was re-connecting to sync every few seconds, and each reconnect rebuilt the screen \u2014 so items you just typed didn\u2019t appear (they WERE saved and synced the whole time) and you had to click twice to get the cursor into a box. Reconnects are now limited to at most one a minute, so the list stays put and the first click lands.","REORDERING BY BAR-DRAG IS EASIER TO HIT ON PHONES: while you hold-drag an item by its colored bar, the drop lines that open between the other items are now twice as tall on the phone \u2014 drop on one to place the item exactly there. (Sideways onto a folder still files it; same one drag, you choose by where you drop.)"],t.s)
B.avR=new A.t(609,B.a7F)
B.a6k=s(["NEW BOX DESIGN ON PHONES \u2014 THE COLORED TOP BAR IS THE HANDLE: every item box now has a lightly tinted top bar in its type\u2019s color (blue note, green task, amber meeting, clay Claude) holding the type, the date/time on the right, the headphones, and the collapse arrow. HOLD the bar and drag to move the item \u2014 into a folder, the Trash, or a new spot in the list. DOUBLE-TAP the bar to open the item full-size (the little \u2922 corner button is gone). A single tap on the bar does nothing, so no more accidental opens. The small circle handle is gone on phones; on Windows and the web it stays exactly as before."],t.s)
B.atd=new A.t(608,B.a6k)
B.a3y=s(["OPENING THE APP NO LONGER STARTS RECORDING ON ITS OWN: the app used to begin capturing the moment you opened it. It now stays quiet on open \u2014 start dictation yourself with the mic when you\u2019re ready. (If you liked open-and-record, Settings still has a toggle to turn it back on.)","MOVING AN ITEM TO A FOLDER ALWAYS TAKES IT OFF THE INBOX NOW: the full-size item\u2019s \u201c\u2026\u201d menu had two look-alike options \u2014 \u201cMove to Folder\u2026\u201d and a leftover \u201cMove to Project\u2026.\u201d Only the first actually removed the item from the inbox; the second quietly left it on the main screen. That confusing legacy option is gone, so \u201cMove to Folder\u2026\u201d is the one move \u2014 and it always clears the item off the inbox into its folder."],t.s)
B.aru=new A.t(607,B.a3y)
B.a03=s(["AUDIO PLAYS ON iPHONE AGAIN: opening an item\u2019s full view and pressing play on iPhone was failing with \u201ccould not load audio (-1002).\u201d The full-window player was handing iOS a file path the wrong way (iOS is stricter than Android about it); it now loads the file correctly. This also makes your Windows-recorded audio playable on the iPhone."],t.s)
B.avy=new A.t(606,B.a03)
B.a_o=s(["TAP ONCE TO EDIT AN ITEM: tapping into an item\u2019s text used to need two taps \u2014 the first one got swallowed. The trace showed a background rebuild dropping the cursor a split-second after the first tap; now the cursor re-grabs itself and holds, so one tap lands you in the field ready to type.","DRAG A SUBFOLDER OUT TO A MAIN FOLDER: grab a subfolder in the left pane and drop it on the thin strip at the very bottom of the folder list to promote it back to a top-level folder. (Dropping onto another folder still nests it inside; this is the way back out.)"],t.s)
B.atw=new A.t(605,B.a_o)
B.a_7=s(["NO MORE FLICKER WHEN REORDERING ITEMS IN A FOLDER: dragging an item (collapsed ones especially) to reorder it inside a folder used to flash and jump \u2014 because a background sync update was rebuilding the list under your finger mid-drag. The list now holds still for the whole drag and only settles once you drop. Smooth on iPhone and everywhere."],t.s)
B.aqe=new A.t(604,B.a_7)
B.X5=s(["WINDOWS RECORDINGS NOW PLAY ON YOUR OTHER DEVICES: audio recorded on the Windows app is finally uploaded to your account, so it plays on your iPhone and Android too. (Before, only the Windows machine that made the recording could play it \u2014 everywhere else said \u201cno cloud audio.\u201d The upload had a step that only worked on Android, so Windows recordings were silently skipped.) Existing Windows recordings upload themselves over the next little while of the app being open and signed in; recordings over 25 MB are the one exception for now."],t.s)
B.ax_=new A.t(603,B.X5)
B.YX=s(["DRAGGING A FOLDER ONTO ITS OWN PARENT IS NOW HONEST: it used to say \u201cmoved into folder\u201d even though nothing changed (it was already there). Now it just says \u201cAlready in that folder\u201d and does nothing \u2014 no false move.","ONE VERSION NUMBER EVERYWHERE: the iPhone (TestFlight) build now shows the SAME build number as Windows, Android and the web \u2014 e.g. \u201c0.1.0 (602)\u201d \u2014 instead of its own separate count. (Takes effect on the next iPhone build.)"],t.s)
B.aq5=new A.t(602,B.YX)
B.a0o=s(["MOVE SUBFOLDERS BY DRAG: you can now grab ANY folder in the left pane \u2014 including subfolders \u2014 and drop it onto another folder to move it inside, Explorer-style. (Before, only top-level folders could be dragged, so a subfolder was stuck.) On Windows, drag any folder with the mouse; on the phone, hold it. Dropping onto a folder\u2019s body moves it IN; dropping a top-level folder on the thin strip at another\u2019s top still just reorders them. It won\u2019t let you drop a folder into itself or its own subfolder."],t.s)
B.axj=new A.t(601,B.a0o)
B.a_K=s(["BACK TO THE HOME PAGE (web): using Voicley in your browser, the left pane now has a \u201cVoicley home\u201d link at the bottom that takes you back to the landing page. (On the installed Windows and phone apps there\u2019s no website to return to, so it doesn\u2019t appear there.)"],t.s)
B.are=new A.t(600,B.a_K)
B.a6D=s(["A LIVE \u201cWHAT\u2019S HAPPENING\u201d LINE: when Voicley is working in the background \u2014 finishing transcriptions, uploading to your account, or downloading an app update \u2014 a small line at the top of the inbox now says exactly what it\u2019s doing and how many items, e.g. \u201cTranscribing 2 recordings\u2026\u201d, \u201cUploading 5 items\u2026\u201d, or \u201cDownloading update \u2014 part 4 of 9\u201d. It appears only when there\u2019s something in flight and disappears the moment everything\u2019s caught up. Nothing blocks \u2014 you can keep working while it shows."],t.s)
B.arM=new A.t(599,B.a6D)
B.a0W=s(["YOUR LAST WORDS STAY IN THE RIGHT NOTE: if you dictate a sentence and immediately tap \u2713 Done or New, the tail that\u2019s still coming back from transcription now lands in the note you SPOKE it in \u2014 not at the top of the fresh one. The box waits a beat (up to ~3 seconds, and only if something\u2019s actually still arriving) for that last bit to land before it closes, so nothing jumps to the next note. On a slow connection this is the difference-maker."],t.s)
B.awK=new A.t(598,B.a0W)
B.a_J=s(["PHONE UPDATES NOW ACTUALLY DELIVER: v596\u2019s new \u201cCheck for updates\u201d button couldn\u2019t receive the app itself \u2014 the release shelf caps files at 50 MB and Voicley is 68 MB, so the very first publish bounced. Releases now ship in parts that the phone stitches back together and integrity-checks (size + fingerprint) before Android\u2019s installer opens. Same one-tap experience, now with something on the shelf."],t.s)
B.awW=new A.t(597,B.a_J)
B.a1u=s(["UPDATE FROM THE PHONE ITSELF: Settings now has \u201cCheck for updates\u201d on Android, right by the version number \u2014 exactly like Windows. Tap it and Voicley checks your account\u2019s release shelf; if a newer build is there, it downloads and Android\u2019s installer opens (one tap \u2014 everything stays in place, no cable needed). First use, Android asks you to allow Voicley to install updates \u2014 a one-time toggle. Plugging into the PC still works too."],t.s)
B.apS=new A.t(596,B.a1u)
B.a1a=s(["NEW FOLDERS ARE BORN WHERE YOU ARE: with a folder open, the pane\u2019s + now creates the new folder INSIDE that folder, Explorer-style \u2014 the naming prompt says where it will land, the parent unfolds in the tree so the new row is visible immediately, and a confirmation names where it went. The folder view\u2019s \uff0b menu gains a \u201cNew folder\u201d option too. (Before, the + always created at the TOP level \u2014 a folder made while inside Tasks landed at root, out of sight, looking like it was never created. The stray \u201cTomorrow\u201d from that: one copy is now inside Tasks, the duplicate is in the Trash.)"],t.s)
B.aqQ=new A.t(595,B.a1a)
B.a4A=s(["FOLDER ARROWS MOVED TO THE LEFT: the expand/collapse chevron now sits before each folder\u2019s icon, Explorer-style \u2014 the arrows trace the tree down the left edge, and folders without subfolders keep the space so the icons stay aligned. The right edge keeps just the count and headphones.","NO MORE BLANK NOTES: an empty note box simply evaporates when it closes \u2014 leave the folder, end dictation, click out \u2014 nothing blank is ever saved. (Notes WITH content always post, exactly as before.)"],t.s)
B.axf=new A.t(594,B.a4A)
B.Xi=s(["FOLDER VIEWS SHOW ITEMS ONLY: subfolders no longer take up rows inside a folder \u2014 the left pane\u2019s expandable tree is the one place for navigating the hierarchy. A folder holding only subfolders shows the entry box, ready for its first item."],t.s)
B.avC=new A.t(593,B.Xi)
B.Zg=s(["THE \u2715 ON THE NOTE BOX ALWAYS CLEARS IT: whether dictation is on or off, tapping \u2715 wipes whatever text is in the box (it used to do nothing when the mic was off). A deliberate discard \u2014 the session audio still keeps everything when recording."],t.s)
B.att=new A.t(592,B.Zg)
B.a12=s(["THE FOLDER INPUT IS NOW EXACTLY THE INBOX BOX: every folder\u2019s entry area is the same live note box the inbox uses \u2014 same look, same anatomy, same rules (Done/\u201cnew note\u201d posts and re-arms, empty box reused, talk or type, click-out posts) \u2014 always there, idle or recording. The only differences: a green MIC inside the box (tap to dictate into the folder, tap the red stop to turn it off), everything you close files INTO that folder, and leaving the folder turns dictation off."],t.s)
B.aqA=new A.t(591,B.a12)
B.a05=s(["DICTATE STRAIGHT INTO A FOLDER: every folder\u2019s entry box now has a green MIC \u2014 tap it and the folder becomes your inbox: the same live note box takes over (same rules \u2014 Done/\u201cnew note\u201d posts and re-arms, empty box is reused, talk or type), and every note you close files into THAT folder automatically (the box\u2019s footer shows the folder name). LEAVING THE FOLDER TURNS DICTATION OFF \u2014 the last note still finishes transcribing in the background. No bottom control bar in folders: on/off lives in the box, so the list keeps the space."],t.s)
B.avt=new A.t(590,B.a05)
B.Z2=s(["\u201cNEW NOTE\u201d ON AN EMPTY NOTE JUST KEEPS IT: if the current note box is still empty and you say \u201cnew note\u201d (or hit the pill), the same empty box is reused \u2014 no more stack of blank notes. The cursor re-arms in it, ready to dictate. (Clicking OUT of a blank note still posts it as a regular item \u2014 that rule is unchanged.)"],t.s)
B.avD=new A.t(589,B.Z2)
B.X6=s(["HOLD A FOLDER ANYWHERE TO MOVE IT (phone): press and hold any folder in the left pane \u2014 it lifts with a buzz. Drag up or down and drop to reorder (the list scrolls at the edges, and the order syncs everywhere). Want the folder MENU instead? Hold and just let go without dragging \u2014 the options sheet opens like before. This is the standard Android pattern: hold = lift, drag = move, release = menu."],t.s)
B.asf=new A.t(588,B.X6)
B.a_g=s(["FIX: MOVING A FOLDER NOW SCROLLS THE LIST TOO \u2014 while you\u2019re dragging a folder in the left pane, hold it near the top or bottom and the folder list scrolls under it, so you can drop it at a spot that was off-screen. (The scroll bands only reacted to ITEM drags before \u2014 folder drags were invisible to them on every platform. Verified working on the phone.)"],t.s)
B.avq=new A.t(587,B.a_g)
B.Xj=s(["DRAG AN ITEM PAST THE EDGE AND THE LIST SCROLLS: while you\u2019re dragging an item in the inbox or a folder, hold it near the top or bottom of the list and the list scrolls under it \u2014 so you can drop it at a spot you couldn\u2019t see when you picked it up. Works on Android AND Windows/web (the item lists never had this anywhere \u2014 only the folder pane did)."],t.s)
B.aub=new A.t(586,B.Xj)
B.a1v=s(["DRAG FOLDERS ON ANDROID: hold a folder\u2019s ICON in the left pane for a beat and it lifts \u2014 drag up or down and drop to reorder, right on the phone. Drop on a folder to slot in above it, or on the strip at the very bottom to send it last. Tap still opens the folder and holding the NAME still opens its options, unchanged. And since the order syncs now, the arrangement you make on the phone shows up on Windows and the web too."],t.s)
B.aq3=new A.t(585,B.a1v)
B.a3I=s(["YOUR ORDER NOW FOLLOWS YOU EVERYWHERE: the inbox order AND the left-pane folder order are now saved to your account instead of per-device \u2014 arrange them once, on any device, and the Fold, Windows, and the web all show the same arrangement. Reorder anywhere and the others follow. (One-time effect: until your first drag, folders show name-sorted and the inbox newest-first; your first re-drag sets the shared order for every device.) New items still always appear at the top of the inbox."],t.s)
B.as8=new A.t(584,B.a3I)
B.a5F=s(["FIX: NEW ITEMS LANDED IN THE MIDDLE OF THE INBOX \u2014 found it. The inbox\u2019s saved manual order was keyed by temporary numbers that reshuffle every time the app starts, so after a restart the whole list could scramble, and a brand-new item\u2019s number could collide with an old saved slot and get seated mid-list (looking \u201ceaten\u201d \u2014 it was there all along, just buried). The order is now keyed by each item\u2019s permanent id: new items always appear at the TOP, and your arrangement survives restarts. One-time effect: the old scrambled order is cleared, so the inbox shows newest-first once \u2014 re-drag anything you want placed and it now sticks."],t.s)
B.avw=new A.t(583,B.a5F)
B.a6Y=s(['FIX: ITEMS NO LONGER "DISAPPEAR" WHILE RECORDING. The list was blocked from refreshing the entire time the mic was on, so anything you saved during a session stayed hidden until you turned recording off \u2014 which looked like items being eaten. Now new and synced items appear on the dashboard live, even mid-recording, without ever disturbing the note you\u2019re dictating. (Nothing was ever lost \u2014 the words were saved; the screen just wasn\u2019t showing them.)'],t.s)
B.awD=new A.t(582,B.a6Y)
B.a6o=s(["EASIER TO CLICK THE CIRCLE: the dot beside each item is unchanged and stays put, but the clickable area around it is now much bigger (the whole strip, not just the 12-pixel dot) \u2014 so selecting an item, in the inbox, folders, and the canvas, takes far less aim."],t.s)
B.arh=new A.t(581,B.a6o)
B.a4R=s(["SELECT TEXT WITH THE MOUSE (desktop): on Windows/Mac/Linux the left-swipe \u201cdelete\u201d gesture no longer fights you when you drag across text inside an item \u2014 swipe-to-delete is a touch gesture, so it\u2019s off on a mouse. Delete on desktop with the Delete key, right-click, the trash target, or the box\u2019s delete corner.","CLICK EMPTY SPACE TO SCROLL: clicking the white space around the boxes in the inbox or a folder now drops the cursor out of whatever box you were editing, so the wheel and Page keys scroll the list instead of the text field."],t.s)
B.aqy=new A.t(580,B.a4R)
B.a_k=s(["MOVING FOLDERS STAYS CALM (desktop): dragging a folder in the left pane no longer shoves every other row apart \u2014 the list holds still and a thin line shows exactly where the folder will land. Drop on a row to place it just above that row; drop on the strip at the very bottom to send it to the end.","EMPTY FOLDERS GET THE ENTRY BOX: open a brand-new or emptied folder and the same \u201ctype a thought\u201d box is right there at the top \u2014 start capturing immediately instead of staring at an empty screen."],t.s)
B.auu=new A.t(579,B.a_k)
B.a5L=s(["FIX: THE FOLDER PANE NOW AUTO-SCROLLS WHILE YOU DRAG (desktop) \u2014 when you drag an item out of a folder toward a target that\u2019s scrolled out of view, hold near the top or bottom edge of the folder list and it scrolls, just like everywhere else. Drags started from inside a folder weren\u2019t turning that on before, so a long pane couldn\u2019t be reached; now they do (and the folder rows light up as drop targets too)."],t.s)
B.avJ=new A.t(578,B.a5L)
B.a0r=s(["CIRCLES STAY PUT: the circle handle is now pinned to the TOP of each item, so folding/unfolding never moves it \u2014 repeated double-clicks land in the same spot.","TAB MAKES A SUB-ITEM: select item(s) and press Tab \u2014 they nest under the item directly above. Shift+Tab un-nests. Never fires while you\u2019re typing.","TIGHTER LISTS: spacing between items reduced 65% across the Inbox, folders, and canvas groups.","FIX: rapidly deleting items could fold the survivors that slid under your cursor (phantom double-clicks) \u2014 the fold now stands down for a beat after any delete, same shield the chevron has had."],t.s)
B.auB=new A.t(577,B.a0r)
B.a0q=s(["CHANGE YOUR MIND MID-DRAG: while you\u2019re dragging an item, gaps now open between the rows of the Inbox and folder lists \u2014 drop on a gap and the item lands exactly there. So one drag does everything: sideways onto a folder files it; back into the list reorders it. No modes, no starting over. (Dropping between rows of a DIFFERENT folder files it into that folder at that exact spot.)"],t.s)
B.axa=new A.t(576,B.a0q)
B.a5i=s(["DOUBLE-CLICK THE CIRCLE TO FOLD/UNFOLD: double-clicking an item\u2019s circle handle now collapses an open item and expands a collapsed one \u2014 everywhere the circles live (Inbox, folders, canvas). Single click still selects; right-click still opens the menu."],t.s)
B.axr=new A.t(575,B.a5i)
B.a4N=s(["DELETE KEY DELETES THE SELECTION: select items with their circles (Ctrl+click for several) and press Delete \u2014 they all move to Trash in one stroke, restorable as always. The key never fires while you\u2019re typing in a text field."],t.s)
B.av5=new A.t(574,B.a4N)
B.a4G=s(["DOUBLE-CLICK A COLLAPSED ITEM TO OPEN IT: double-click anywhere on a collapsed row and it expands in place \u2014 no hunting for the \u2304. (Just the in-place expand, not the full-screen view; the chevron and title editing work exactly as before.)"],t.s)
B.asr=new A.t(573,B.a4G)
B.a1R=s(["DRAG FOLDERS TO REORDER, RIGHT IN THE PANE (desktop): grab any top-level folder row in the left pane and pull it up or down \u2014 gaps open between rows while you drag; drop and the order saves instantly. No buttons, no separate screen. (Same saved order the Folders screen uses, so they always match. Phones keep the Folders-screen reorder \u2014 press-and-hold there means folder options.)"],t.s)
B.awR=new A.t(572,B.a1R)
B.a4m=s(["FIX: A DICTATION COULD POST TWICE \u2014 once as itself (with audio) and again as a text-only copy about 15 seconds later, if you clicked out of the box shortly after finishing. The finished text is intentionally kept in the box briefly for cloud processing; one exit path didn\u2019t know it was already saved and posted it again. It knows now \u2014 clicking out just blanks the box. If you EDIT the leftover text before clicking out, it still posts (your edit is new content)."],t.s)
B.awv=new A.t(571,B.a4m)
B.a71=s(["FIX: EMPTY CANVAS ON A SECOND PC \u2014 a fresh device could race sync and create its own empty \u201cCanvas\u201d folder, leaving its canvas blank while your items sat in the original (this hit the office PC today; nothing was lost). Every device now agrees on the ORIGINAL Canvas folder, any accidental duplicates are automatically merged into it and removed, and the app no longer creates the folder just by drawing the sidebar \u2014 only when you actually use the canvas."],t.s)
B.aqT=new A.t(570,B.a71)
B.a7s=s(["CLICK EMPTY CANVAS = NEW NOTE, instantly \u2014 no menu, cursor ready, exactly like OneNote\u2019s click-and-type. The \uff0b button keeps the full menu for tasks, meetings, Claude messages, and groups.","EVERY ITEM IS A CONTAINER NOW (OneNote-confirmed): single items wear the same anatomy as groups \u2014 a top bar that moves them, a bordered body. And dropping an item ON or NEAR another item or group joins them: near a single item \u2192 the two become a group; near a group \u2192 it joins that group. Dropping directly on an item\u2019s words still nests it as a sub-item \u2014 near = peer, on-the-text = child."],t.s)
B.aq9=new A.t(569,B.a7s)
B.a4W=s(["GROUPS NOW WORK LIKE ONENOTE \u2014 NO NAMING: \u201cNew group\u201d creates instantly, no prompt. The region shows just a move bar (no title) until you deliberately rename it via right-click. And the fastest way to group: drop an item\u2019s circle onto another card\u2019s MOVE BAR \u2014 the two instantly become a group right there (drop on the box body still nests it as a sub-item).","REORDER INSIDE GROUPS: while you drag, slim gaps between a group\u2019s rows open up \u2014 drop on a gap and the item lands exactly there. Works for moving items within a group, between groups, or in from the loose canvas. Group order matches the folder\u2019s order everywhere."],t.s)
B.as_=new A.t(568,B.a4W)
B.a63=s(["GROUPS ON THE CANVAS \u2014 OneNote-style: pick \u201cNew group\u201d from the canvas menu (tap anywhere or \uff0b), name it, and a titled region appears. Drop items onto the region and they move into the group; drag an item\u2019s circle back out to empty canvas and it leaves the group. The header bar moves the whole group at once; right-click it to rename or delete.","GROUPS SYNC: a group is a real folder under Canvas, so its contents follow you to every device (the region\u2019s position stays per-device for now, like the cards)."],t.s)
B.awx=new A.t(567,B.a63)
B.a7n=s(["THE CANVAS USES THE WHOLE SPACE: the reading-width cap is gone on the canvas \u2014 it now stretches across everything right of the folder pane.","CIRCLES ON THE LEFT: canvas cards now carry their circle to the LEFT of the box, matching every other item in the app. The slim move bar sits across the top of the box \u2014 drag it to place the card.","TAP ANYWHERE TO CREATE: click any empty spot on the canvas and pick a type \u2014 note, task, meeting, or Claude message \u2014 and it\u2019s born right where you clicked (filed to the Canvas folder, cursor ready). The \uff0b button offers the same menu."],t.s)
B.auw=new A.t(566,B.a7n)
B.ZY=s(["THE CANVAS NOW WORKS LIKE A ONENOTE PAGE: drop one card\u2019s circle onto another card and it\u2019s ABSORBED \u2014 its floating card disappears and it renders inside the parent (\u201c\u25b8 N sub-items\u201d, expandable, any depth). Drag a nested item\u2019s circle onto empty canvas and it SPLITS back out as its own card right where you dropped it.","CREATE ON THE CANVAS: the \uff0b button (top right) births a typed note directly on the canvas \u2014 filed to the Canvas folder, cursor ready in its box. Canvas cards are also editable in place now, like folder boxes.","SNAP GRID: cards settle onto an invisible 20px grid when you release them, so layouts stay tidy \u2014 hold Alt while releasing for free placement (OneNote\u2019s exact modifier). Dropping a card\u2019s circle on empty canvas also repositions it there."],t.s)
B.asq=new A.t(565,B.ZY)
B.a_j=s(["THE \u2261 PANCAKE HANDLE IS GONE \u2014 EVERYWHERE: the last holdout (folder rows on the Folders screen, and the row handles inside folder views) now uses the same OneNote circle as every item. One glyph, one meaning, the whole app: see a circle, you can click to select and drag to move."],t.s)
B.apc=new A.t(564,B.a_j)
B.a7e=s(["SELECTION NOW HIGHLIGHTS THE WHOLE ITEM: a selected item wears a blue outline around its entire box (plus the blue circle), everywhere \u2014 Inbox, folders, lists, canvas cards.","FILING FROM THE INBOX NOW LEAVES THE INBOX \u2014 including Claude items: dragging items from the Inbox into a folder previously left Claude-type boxes still sitting in the Inbox (a deliberate carve-out so in-flight Claude messages stay visible), which read as \u201clinked, not moved\u201d and \u201cthe item is in there twice\u201d. A deliberate drag now always takes the item off the Inbox; it lives in its folder. New Claude replies still appear in the Inbox as before.","ONE COPY PER FOLDER \u2014 confirmed and test-pinned: adding an item to a folder it\u2019s already in is a no-op, and moving one in collapses into the existing copy. An item can exist in many folders, but only once in each."],t.s)
B.ato=new A.t(563,B.a7e)
B.a5h=s(["MULTI-SELECT WITH THE CIRCLES: click an item\u2019s circle handle to SELECT it (it turns blue), Ctrl+click to add more \u2014 then drag any selected circle and the WHOLE selection moves together: into a pane folder, a subfolder, onto another item to nest, onto the Canvas, or to Trash. The drag card reads \u201cN items\u201d and one drop files them all. On phones, plain taps on the circles multi-select (no Ctrl needed). A drop clears the selection.","Works everywhere the circles live \u2014 Inbox, folder views, lists, and canvas cards. (Canvas note: the card\u2019s circle now selects/files it; drag the little bar beside it to move the card around the canvas.) Clicking a circle no longer opens the item \u2014 the \u2922 button is the full-window door."],t.s)
B.as7=new A.t(562,B.a5h)
B.a2v=s(["RIGHT-CLICK FOLDER OPTIONS ON WINDOWS: right-clicking any folder \u2014 in the left pane, on the Folders screen, or a subfolder inside a folder \u2014 now opens the same options sheet as press-and-hold (rename, new subfolder, play list, delete\u2026). Items already had right-click; folders now match."],t.s)
B.apm=new A.t(561,B.a2v)
B.YE=s(["THE TEST CANVAS IS HERE: a new Canvas button in the left pane opens a free-form surface where your items are movable cards \u2014 drag a card by its circle to place it anywhere, pinch/scroll to zoom, and tap a card for all the normal item behaviors. It\u2019s the first cut of the OneNote-style move-things-around view.","LINKING ITEMS TO THE CANVAS: the canvas is backed by a real \u201cCanvas\u201d folder, so every filing flow works \u2014 drag an item onto the Canvas row in the pane (it LINKS; the item also stays where it was), or use Add to folder \u2192 Canvas. Test-phase note: card positions save per device for now."],t.s)
B.aqP=new A.t(560,B.YE)
B.a7h=s(["YOU CAN ALWAYS SEE WHEN THE MIC IS ON: a slim red pill now floats at the bottom of EVERY screen while capture is live \u2014 a pulsing dot, the mode (\u201cdictating\u201d or \u201ccommands only\u201d), and \u201cN waiting to sync\u201d when a live recording is holding the upload queue. Tap it to jump to the live box. Born from tonight: the mic ran unnoticed for half an hour and quietly held 30 items back from syncing \u2014 that can\u2019t sneak by anymore."],t.s)
B.awc=new A.t(559,B.a7h)
B.a2R=s(["DELETING NO LONGER SHIFTS THE LIST: while you\u2019re actively deleting, background arrivals (synced items, recovered recordings) now wait \u2014 the list stays frozen under your fingers and everything lands the moment you pause for a couple of seconds. Nothing is lost; it just doesn\u2019t barge in mid-cleanup.","PULL DOWN TO SYNC: pull the Inbox down and release \u2014 it force-syncs right there (ending an idle recording first if one is holding the connection, same as Settings \u2192 Re-sync now). Handy after dictating a batch on one device: pull down on the other and it all arrives."],t.s)
B.awm=new A.t(558,B.a2R)
B.a5H=s(["THE HANDLE CIRCLES ARE NOW VISIBLE AND EVERYWHERE: v556 drew them at 8px \u2014 too small to see next to an open box, which read as \u201cno icons at all\u201d. They\u2019re 12px now, and the circle COLUMN is permanent: every item, open or collapsed, phone or desktop, Inbox or folders, carries the same circle in the same spot.","COLLAPSING NO LONGER SHIFTS THE GRID: with the handle column constant across states, folding a box changes only its height (animated) \u2014 nothing jumps sideways and the list stays put."],t.s)
B.apo=new A.t(557,B.a5H)
B.a2O=s(["THE ONENOTE CIRCLE IS THE HANDLE EVERYWHERE \u2014 open items too: every item, open or collapsed, in the Inbox, folders, and listening views, now wears the small circle as its move handle. The six-dot \u2261 is fully retired (the live New-note box and just-created items match too). Same drags: up/down reorders, sideways files into a folder, click opens."],t.s)
B.aqg=new A.t(556,B.a2O)
B.a0U=s(["COLLAPSED ROWS, AS YOU ACTUALLY ASKED (my earlier misread \u2014 sorry): a collapsed item is just the circle handle, the title, and the \u2304. The delete / full-window / remove icons are GONE from collapsed rows \u2014 they live on the open box and in the right-click menu.","CIRCLE HANDLES EVERYWHERE: folder and listening views now swap the \u2261 for the small circle on collapsed items too (before, only the Inbox did)."],t.s)
B.asO=new A.t(555,B.a0U)
B.a_w=s(["THE ONENOTE ICON IS ON EVERY LINKED ITEM: open boxes now carry a small notebook icon next to the \u2922 \u2014 tap it to jump straight to the item\u2019s OneNote page (only shows when the project is linked). Collapsed rows keep your four-icon set; right-click still offers Open in OneNote there."],t.s)
B.atN=new A.t(554,B.a_w)
B.a6B=s(["COLLAPSED ROW ICON SET COMPLETE: exactly four controls and nothing else \u2014 \u2304 open, \u2296 remove-from-folder (nested items only), \u2922 full window, and Delete. The \u2296 was the missing one."],t.s)
B.asA=new A.t(553,B.a6B)
B.a2H=s(["COLLAPSED ROWS CARRY THE SAME BUTTONS AS OPEN BOXES: \u2922 full window and Delete now sit on the collapsed row too (left of the \u2304) \u2014 open or delete an item without unfolding it first. Same icons, same actions, both states."],t.s)
B.axi=new A.t(552,B.a2H)
B.a4Q=s(["OPEN-BOX BUTTONS IN THEIR CORNERS (your layout): fold \u2303 at the top right (matching the collapsed row\u2019s \u2304 in the same corner), full-window \u2922 at the bottom left, Delete at the bottom right, and the \u201c\u2192 destination\u201d label is gone. The title row is unchanged.","THE FOLD-INSTEAD-OF-DELETE BUG IS DEAD FOR GOOD: folding now happens ONLY on the \u2303 chevron \u2014 the old click-anywhere-on-the-top-strip fold is retired, so a click that misses a button can never fold a box again. And folding now animates (the box visibly shrinks into its row), so a real fold no longer looks like \u201cdeleted then recreated collapsed\u201d."],t.s)
B.ax3=new A.t(551,B.a4Q)
B.a1S=s(["PASTE FROM ONENOTE \u2192 ITEMS: copy paragraphs in OneNote (or anywhere), paste into the New-note box, and a pill appears: \u201cSplit into N items\u201d. One tap makes each paragraph its own item \u2014 same top-to-bottom order as the source, each with its AI title, with a one-tap Undo for the whole batch. Ignore the pill and it stays one normal note \u2014 splitting only ever happens on your tap."],t.s)
B.apV=new A.t(550,B.a1S)
B.a3h=s(["RAPID-FIRE DELETING NO LONGER FOLDS BOXES: the trash button on an open box was a tiny target sitting inside the click-to-fold strip \u2014 deleting items in quick order, the list shifts after each delete and the next fast click hit the strip, folding a box instead of deleting it. The trash button now has a proper finger-sized target, and for a third of a second after any delete the fold-tap ignores clicks entirely (trash clicks still land instantly). Machine-gun cleanup is safe."],t.s)
B.arR=new A.t(549,B.a3h)
B.a31=s(["COLLAPSED ITEMS GOT THE ONENOTE LOOK (your design): a collapsed item is now a clean borderless row \u2014 a small circle on the left (that circle IS the drag handle on desktop: drag it up/down to reorder, sideways to file into a folder), the title in the middle (still editable in place), and the expand chevron moved to the far right. Open items keep their full box exactly as before."],t.s)
B.atY=new A.t(548,B.a31)
B.Zf=s(["DELETE BOUNCE-BACK \u2014 EVERY DELETE PATH NOW COVERED: v546\u2019s guard only protected swipe-deletes; deletes from the right-click menu, item boxes in folder/listening/files views, and drag-to-Trash skipped it, so those could still resurrect for a beat mid-cleanup. All delete surfaces now stamp the same short-lived marker the background refresh respects. Deleted means deleted, from anywhere."],t.s)
B.atU=new A.t(547,B.Zf)
B.a7O=s(["DELETED ITEMS NO LONGER BOUNCE BACK MID-CLEANUP: when you deleted several items in a row, a background refresh could land carrying a snapshot from just BEFORE a delete \u2014 and that item popped back into the list (in its shrunken default look, which read as \u201cit collapsed instead of deleting\u201d). Deletes now leave a short-lived marker the refresh respects, so a deleted box stays gone. If a delete ever genuinely fails, the item resurfaces within two minutes rather than being hidden forever \u2014 nothing can be lost silently."],t.s)
B.atx=new A.t(546,B.a7O)
B.a_u=s(["THE INBOX COUNT NOW SYNCS \u2014 RELIABLY THE SAME ON EVERY DEVICE: the one remaining per-device piece of the count was the \u201cdismissed from Inbox\u201d list, which lived in local settings \u2014 every cleanup on one device made the numbers drift (your 59 vs 46). Dismissals are now synced like everything else: dismiss anywhere, hidden everywhere, and each device\u2019s old local list merges in automatically on first run. Every count change also writes a diagnosis line (visible / total / filed / dismissed) so any future mismatch names its cause in one glance. NOTE: needs the one-time backend migration (011) + sync-rules re-deploy before installing."],t.s)
B.aux=new A.t(545,B.a_u)
B.Z9=s(["DUPLICATE BOXES CLEANED UP: notes and Claude items from the bug era could show TWICE \u2014 the un-linked box from a broken close plus its saved copy arriving via sync (v540 kept both on purpose: never lose words). Now, when a box\u2019s own save arrives, it simply replaces the un-linked box \u2014 one item, once. Nothing in your data was ever duplicated; it was two views of the same saved item. (Genuinely repeating the same words still gives each its own item.)"],t.s)
B.as0=new A.t(544,B.Z9)
B.a5t=s(["BAD NETWORK NO LONGER BURNS RECOVERY RETRIES: when your phone can\u2019t reach OpenAI at all (the cellular dead zones), each failed recovery attempt was still spending one of a recording\u2019s 3 auto-retries \u2014 permanently benching it even though nothing was ever billed or judged. Caught live in your 12:51 log. Network-unreachable failures are now free; only real OpenAI rejections count. This is what let the un-transcribed backlog build up over the outage days \u2014 combined with the new Recover button (below), it can\u2019t happen again."],t.s)
B.avT=new A.t(543,B.a5t)
B.a1l=s(["RECOVER UN-TRANSCRIBED AUDIO \u2014 new button in Settings (next to Re-sync): 582 recordings on the server kept their audio but never got words \u2014 mostly the bad-network days, when the automatic retries burned out their 3-attempt safety cap and gave up for good. The button resets those caps on this device and re-runs the recovery scan immediately: silence is skipped (never billed, never fake words), oversized sessions stay manual, and it steps aside instantly if you start dictating. Recovered text fills into the items as it lands. Costs nothing until you tap it."],t.s)
B.aww=new A.t(542,B.a1l)
B.a0E=s(['THE \u2713 DONE EATING BUG \u2014 ROOT CAUSE FIXED: your 12:20 test caught it in the act. Every \u2713 Done (and Enter) close had an internal race: the close answered its own "where did the note save?" question twice, the second answer blew up mid-save, and the explosion skipped the steps that link the box to its saved words, give it its title, and cut its audio. The box was left an unlinked shell \u2014 title-less, headphone-less \u2014 and the next background refresh threw the shell away. That is why notes \u201cwent away\u201d right after Done. The race is gone: closes now finish every step, every time. (Spoken \u201cnew note\u201d closes were never affected \u2014 and none of your words were ever lost: every one of those notes is still in the library and OneNote.)'],t.s)
B.as1=new A.t(541,B.a0E)
B.a17=s(["NOTES NO LONGER VANISH WHEN YOU HIT DONE (or say \u201cnew note\u201d): while sync was churning against its limit, the note\u2019s save could land a beat late \u2014 and a background list refresh in that gap rebuilt the Inbox WITHOUT the not-yet-saved note, so it blinked out and only a blank box remained. The refresh now carries every in-flight note over until its save lands. (The words were never lost \u2014 the audio and recovery path had them \u2014 but now the box itself stays put.)"],t.s)
B.atc=new A.t(540,B.a17)
B.a7R=s(["RIGHT-CLICK MENU ON WINDOWS: right-click any item box \u2014 in the Inbox, folders, the listening area, Trash \u2014 and the \u22ef actions open right there: Open in OneNote (when linked), Add to folder\u2026, Move to folder\u2026, Delete (to Trash, with Undo). Same flows as the full view\u2019s three-dots button."],t.s)
B.av0=new A.t(539,B.a7R)
B.ZW=s(["THE STRAY-TYPING BUG IS FIXED (Windows): your log caught it \u2014 my v527 focus-guard was over-fighting: when you clicked into an item, it misread the click as the focus thief it was built to fight and yanked typing back to the New box, while the item still showed its cursor. The guard now only acts at the moment dictation starts and ALWAYS yields to your click. Typing goes where the cursor is.","ITEMS OPENED FROM THE LISTENING AREA ARE NOW FULLY EDITABLE: the opened view already had the editable title (v533) \u2014 now the TEXT edits in place too (saves as you type, closes save too), and a trash button deletes (to Trash, with Undo). Same behaviors as everywhere else."],t.s)
B.aqF=new A.t(538,B.ZW)
B.ZQ=s(["LISTENING TOP BAR: tapping it now takes you to the playing item IN THE LIST (scrolls there, opens its subfolder) \u2014 it no longer pops the item open in full view on top. Your correction of yesterday\u2019s change."],t.s)
B.atl=new A.t(537,B.ZQ)
B.XS=s(["NO MORE EMPTY WINDOW AFTER AN UPDATE: the Windows updater relaunches Voicley while the old copy is still shutting down and holding the database \u2014 the new copy opened data-less until you restarted it by hand. The app now waits out the old process (retrying the database open for a few seconds), so the first launch after an update comes up with all your data."],t.s)
B.arC=new A.t(536,B.XS)
B.a7m=s(["\u201cOLD FORMAT\u201d ITEMS FIXED \u2014 no more title-less, headphone-less boxes: posting a note while dictation was ON could flip the session ownership mid-save, and the new box never got connected to its saved row \u2014 so it showed without a title, without the headphones toggle, and CLICKING IT OPENED THE BIG VIEW instead of editing in place (your two reports were the same bug). The connection now always completes; every posted box is a full regular item immediately. (No data was ever lost \u2014 the rows were saved correctly all along.)","CTRL WHILE DRAGGING NOW FLIPS THE LABEL EVERYWHERE: hold Ctrl mid-drag and the card switches live \u2014 \u201cMove\u201d becomes \u201cLink\u201d (from a folder) or \u201cAdd\u201d (from the Inbox), and the route line reads \u201cLink into \u2026\u201d / \u201cAdd to \u2026\u201d. Release Ctrl and it flips back."],t.s)
B.apQ=new A.t(535,B.a7m)
B.a6J=s(["THE STRAY SCROLLBAR ON THE LEFT IS GONE: the live box\u2019s text area scrolls bottom-pinned (newest words stay in view), and that kind of scroller draws its bar on the LEFT \u2014 on Windows it showed as a odd little bar left of the boxes. It now stays on the right where scrollbars belong, and the duplicate one Windows auto-adds is silenced.","THE \u201cPHANTOM ENTER\u201d WHILE EDITING FULL-SIZE IS FIXED: after every auto-save (about a second after you pause typing), the little \u201cSaved\u201d tick was INSERTING a line above your text for two seconds and then removing it \u2014 the whole transcript hopped down and back up, which read exactly like an enter being added and removed. The tick now sits beside the title and only fades in and out \u2014 your text never moves. (Nothing in \u201cMoving racks and organizing space\u201d was ever altered \u2014 saves store exactly what you typed; the hop was purely visual.)"],t.s)
B.at4=new A.t(534,B.a6J)
B.a_s=s(["RENAME ITEMS FROM THE OPENED VIEW: opening a meeting (or note) from the listening area \u2014 or any list \u2014 showed no title at all, so there was nowhere to rename it. The opened view now carries an editable title right at the top: tap, type, done. Hand-set titles stick, same as everywhere else.","TAP THE TOP BAR TO GO TO WHAT\u2019S PLAYING: in the Listening area, tapping the bar that names the current item now takes you TO it \u2014 the list scrolls to its spot and the item opens full-view, exactly as if you\u2019d tapped its box.","PHONE CALLS PAUSE PLAYBACK: when a call comes in while recordings are playing, playback pauses \u2014 and picks back up on its own when the call ends. (Unplugging headphones also pauses, so audio never blasts from the speaker.)"],t.s)
B.axg=new A.t(533,B.a_s)
B.a1D=s(["OPENAI COSTS TRIMMED \u2014 the re-title throttle: while you edited an item, every ~1-second typing pause was quietly firing an AI call to refresh its title (and dictating into an item fired one per phrase). Now the title refreshes ONCE, when you leave the item. Same behavior you see \u2014 the title is up to date by the time you\u2019re looking at the list \u2014 at a fraction of the API calls. (Saving your words is untouched: edits still save as you type.)"],t.s)
B.auj=new A.t(532,B.a1D)
B.a0T=s(["SYNC USES FAR LESS DATA (PowerSync limit fix, round 1): the AI titler was re-uploading an item\u2019s ENTIRE text every time it attached a title \u2014 on every closed note, every meeting (the longest texts), and up to 6 old meetings per app launch. Titles now sync as just the title. The cloud-audio backfill also wrote each recording twice where once works. Same features, a fraction of the sync traffic \u2014 this stretches the free tier and keeps the paid tier cheap as the library grows."],t.s)
B.aqZ=new A.t(531,B.a0T)
B.a2J=s(["THE DRAG CARD NO LONGER HIDES BEHIND THE FOLDER PANE: on Windows, the \u201cMove to \u2026\u201d card that follows your cursor was disappearing in the gap between the item boxes and the left folder pane \u2014 it was rendering in a layer clipped to the main window, so it slid *underneath* the pane. It now rides in the top-most layer over the whole window, so you can see it the entire way to the folder you\u2019re dropping on."],t.s)
B.auf=new A.t(530,B.a2J)
B.a2G=s(["THE INBOX NO LONGER JUMPS AROUND WHILE YOU DELETE: your phone\u2019s log showed the cause \u2014 while you were deleting a bunch of items, a burst of items syncing in from another device kept yanking the list back to the top, one jump per arrival. Now a background sync download never scrolls your inbox; only YOUR own new dictations and notes bring the list to the top. Deleting stays put where you are. (Nothing was ever lost \u2014 deletes go to Trash with Undo.)"],t.s)
B.arX=new A.t(529,B.a2G)
B.Z8=s(["THE DRAG CARD NOW SAYS \u201cMOVE\u201d OR \u201cLINK\u201d THE WHOLE TIME: while you drag an item, the card under the cursor shows a clear pill \u2014 \u201cMove\u201d, \u201cLink\u201d, or \u201cNest\u201d \u2014 so you always know what a drop will do. It used to show that only when dragging an item that was already inside a folder; dragging from the Inbox just said the vague \u201cAdd to\u2026\u201d. Now every drag states the action, and it flips live to \u201cLink\u201d when you hold Ctrl (where linking applies) and back on release."],t.s)
B.ap5=new A.t(528,B.Z8)
B.Z1=s(['TYPING INTO THE LIVE BOX ON DESKTOP NOW WORKS: on Windows, going into dictate mode showed a blinking cursor that looked ready \u2014 but that caret was just a marker showing where dictated words land, NOT a live text field, so your typed keys went nowhere until you clicked the box. The composer now mounts the real, typable field the instant dictation starts, and HOLDS its focus against the window glitch that was stealing it a split-second later (the same "keeper" your saved items already use). Dictated words land at that same cursor. (Phone is unchanged \u2014 tap to type.)'],t.s)
B.aqd=new A.t(527,B.Z1)
B.a4L=s(["THE CLAUDE FILTER COUNT MATCHES THE LIST AGAIN: you emptied the Claude view and the chip still read \u201c2\u201d, then \u201c13\u201d \u2014 it was counting deleted messages the list itself was already hiding (in-flight Claude messages that stick to the window on purpose were never being subtracted when you deleted them). Deleting a Claude message now drops it from the count too, so an empty view reads 0."],t.s)
B.arQ=new A.t(526,B.a4L)
B.a39=s(["EDITABLE TITLES IN THE LISTS: tap an item\u2019s title right in the Inbox or a folder and type \u2014 it saves on its own, without touching the body. A title you set by hand STICKS: editing the item\u2019s text afterward won\u2019t let the AI re-title over it.","DELETING NO LONGER JUMPS THE LIST TO THE TOP: removing an item used to scroll the whole Inbox back up (a reseed was re-labeling the surviving items as \u201cnew\u201d). The list now stays put where you were."],t.s)
B.at8=new A.t(525,B.a39)
B.YM=s(['THE SILENCE-PAUSE NO LONGER EATS YOUR DICTATION: last night\u2019s trace showed it park the mic after a 90-second pause and then silently drop 669 things you said \u2014 that was the "dictation regressed." Now (your pick) when the mic parks on silence, the moment you start talking again it AUTO-RESUMES and your words land \u2014 it only ever skips a truly silent gap, never an ongoing dictation. Tapping Commands-only or "end dictation" still stays parked on purpose.'],t.s)
B.awE=new A.t(524,B.YM)
B.YY=s(['"NEW CLAUDE COMMAND" ACTUALLY FIRES NOW: tonight\u2019s trace showed the engine hearing it as "New clog command" and "New Claude quad command" \u2014 neither of which the matcher accepted, so your commands fell through as plain text and everything merged into one blob (the "structure is horrible"). Now it accepts "clog"/"clawed" and, crucially, handles the engine double-transcribing the name ("Claude quad") \u2014 the command fires through the garble.',"A Claude note\u2019s box now has a faint clay-tinted background, so it\u2019s distinct from a regular note at a glance \u2014 not just the border."],t.s)
B.asc=new A.t(523,B.YY)
B.a4r=s(["THE CLAUDE SEND BUTTON MOVED to the bottom-right of the box \u2014 the exact spot the \u2713 Done button sits for regular notes. Same place, same finish-this-entry gesture.","SEND NO LONGER SHUTS OFF DICTATION: hitting Send ships your Claude message AND keeps the mic rolling with a fresh box, just like \u2713 Done does. (It used to end the session \u2014 that was a safety measure against a hot mic, which the silence-pause now handles instead.)"],t.s)
B.ast=new A.t(522,B.a4r)
B.Zr=s(["SILENCE NOW PAUSES CAPTURE (your pick): go quiet for ~90 seconds while dictating and the mic parks itself in Commands-only \u2014 the purple line on the box says so. Room conversation can no longer pile into your note behind your back (today\u2019s \u201cswallowed\u201d items were exactly that: minutes of room talk merging into the box). Say \u201cnew note\u201d or tap Dictate and you\u2019re rolling again; commands keep working the whole time, and the audio is always in the recording."],t.s)
B.atQ=new A.t(521,B.Zr)
B.a6d=s(["THE AUTO-MEETING FLIP STANDS DOWN WHILE YOU\u2019RE NOTE-TAKING: it exists for one case \u2014 a first dictation that runs long because it\u2019s secretly a meeting. It had been swallowing an afternoon of short notes into one giant \u201cmeeting\u201d item (that\u2019s where your \u201ceaten\u201d text went \u2014 check Meetings, it\u2019s all there). Once you\u2019ve closed even one note in a session, the flip never fires.","Windows: the drag card now says WHAT\u2019S ABOUT TO HAPPEN \u2014 \u201cMove to Kitchen\u201d, \u201cAdd to\u2026\u201d, \u201cLink into\u2026\u201d (live as you press Ctrl), \u201cNest under an item\u201d, \u201cMove to Trash\u201d \u2014 plus where it\u2019s coming from."],t.s)
B.asv=new A.t(520,B.a6d)
B.Xq=s(["ENTER RIGHT AFTER SPEAKING KEEPS EVERY WORD: \u2713 Done and Enter now flush the in-flight dictation first \u2014 the grey words you can see get up to ~1.5s to commit before the note posts, so nothing at the tail is lost to fast fingers. The grey draft counts as content too: Enter on a box with only grey words posts them instead of doing nothing.","The Done/Send/\u27a4 buttons are GRAYED OUT until there\u2019s something to post \u2014 no more buttons that look live on an empty box.","Fresh items can be deleted immediately, even mid-dictation: a just-created box no longer refuses the trash while its background save finishes \u2014 the delete wins, and the save honors it."],t.s)
B.aw_=new A.t(519,B.Xq)
B.a_4=s(["JUST-CREATED ITEMS LOOK LIKE EVERY OTHER ITEM: a fresh item briefly rendered without its \u2261 handle on Windows, so its box spanned wider than the rest. The handle column is now always there \u2014 a dimmed placeholder holds the exact space until the real grip arrives a moment later. Boxes always look the same."],t.s)
B.apt=new A.t(518,B.a_4)
B.a7r=s(["REORDER THE INBOX ON WINDOWS: drag an item\u2019s \u2261 UP or DOWN and it moves in place \u2014 the same axis split as folders (sideways still lifts it to file into a folder, Ctrl links). Your order sticks; new arrivals still land on top. On the phone this already works: hold any box a beat and move it."],t.s)
B.awt=new A.t(517,B.a7r)
B.Zu=s(["The on-device diagnostic logs now HEAL THEMSELVES: after an update or storage hiccup they could silently stop recording \u2014 which is why several of today\u2019s bug reports came back untraceable. Every app start now stamps the log, and a failed write re-opens the file and retries instead of going quiet. (This is the eyes for fixing everything else you report.)"],t.s)
B.atC=new A.t(516,B.Zu)
B.a2K=s(["ITEMS YOU CREATE ARE NEVER AUTO-DELETED AGAIN: the end-of-session cleanup used to destroy any item that was still empty when the mic turned off \u2014 including ones you\u2019d created on purpose (and ones that LOOKED filled but whose words had routed elsewhere). That rule is retired: a created item survives, empty or not, exactly like the click-out law says. Empty ones are one manual delete away, with Undo."],t.s)
B.awn=new A.t(515,B.a2K)
B.a13=s(["ONE \u2713 DONE, EVERYWHERE AN ENTRY ENDS: every folder\u2019s entry box now carries the same \u2713 Done pill as the Inbox (posts the thought filed into that folder, blanks for the next) \u2014 and an item you\u2019re dictating INTO shows \u2713 Done too: it finishes that entry, the words merge into the item, and dictation keeps rolling with a fresh box. Toward the standing model: every place you can enter a thought has an always-open area and one obvious way to end the entry, typing or speaking."],t.s)
B.avQ=new A.t(514,B.a13)
B.a7c=s(["THE \u2713 DONE BUTTON NOW DOES WHAT ITS LABEL SAYS: it posts the note and DICTATION KEEPS GOING \u2014 your next words open a fresh box. It had been silently switching the session into Commands-only, which is the mode that DROPS plain speech: that is exactly how dictated paragraphs were \u201ceaten\u201d on Android today. (The dropped words\u2019 audio was never lost \u2014 full recordings of each session land in Files.)","Whenever Commands-only IS on, the entry box now says so in purple \u2014 \u201cCommands only, speech isn\u2019t captured, tap Dictate or say new note\u201d \u2014 so words can never silently vanish again. The \u2922 and Claude Send keep their close-and-stop behavior."],t.s)
B.ax8=new A.t(513,B.a7c)
B.a_O=s(["THE DRAG CARD TELLS YOU THE ROUTE: while dragging an item, the floating card now reads \u201cfrom Inbox\u201d, and the moment you hover a folder it shows the full move \u2014 \u201cInbox \u2192 Kitchen\u201d, \u201cIdeas \u2192 Trash\u201d \u2014 alongside the Move/\u2303 Link chip. No more guessing where a drop will land.","Windows: the app now checks for a new build every 10 MINUTES (was 2 hours) \u2014 this morning a data-loss fix sat published for an hour while the old build kept running. Release builds on the main machine also install locally the moment they publish when the app is closed."],t.s)
B.av8=new A.t(512,B.a_O)
B.YW=s(["THE DONE BUTTON ALWAYS SAVES NOW: with the mic off, \u2713 Done / Enter / \u2922 on the entry box were doing nothing \u2014 typed text sat unsaved and could be wiped by the next mode switch. Now they post your note as an item and blank the box for the next one, mic or no mic. (With v510\u2019s guard, that\u2019s both halves of the typed-note data loss closed.)","Removing a Claude item from the Inbox no longer deletes it from the Claude messages folder \u2014 the trash on those boxes now just clears them off the Inbox (Undo included); the item stays in its folder.","Windows: drag an item\u2019s \u2261 SIDEWAYS and it lifts instantly to drop on any folder \u2014 no hold, no timer; up/down still reorders. This was why folder-to-folder drags felt broken.","Windows: the left pane now shows folders in YOUR order \u2014 the one you set by dragging on the Folders screen \u2014 instead of alphabetical."],t.s)
B.asL=new A.t(511,B.YW)
B.a1x=s(["TYPED WORDS CAN NO LONGER BE LOST TO A MODE SWITCH: text typed into the entry box while the mic was off used to be silently destroyed the moment a session started (turning Commands or Dictate on). Now starting a session treats that typed note like a click-out \u2014 it posts as its own item first, always. This was the last remaining way the app could delete words you gave it."],t.s)
B.axm=new A.t(510,B.a1x)
B.a2m=s(["DELETING FROM ONE FOLDER NO LONGER TOUCHES ITS OTHER HOMES, everywhere: the trash on rows in the Listening List now unlinks just that folder when the item also lives elsewhere (Trash only when it\u2019s the last home) \u2014 and both ways give you a 6-second Undo.","Drop items ONTO SUBFOLDERS inside a folder: the subfolder rows light up as drop targets, same as the left pane \u2014 drop moves the item in, Ctrl+drop links it.","Windows: the bar between the folder pane and the main window is now a real DIVIDER \u2014 grab it and drag to size the pane how you like; it remembers.","Windows: holding the \u2261 to lift an item free now takes a quick quarter-second instead of nearly half \u2014 dragging to a folder feels immediate."],t.s)
B.app=new A.t(509,B.a2m)
B.a1p=s(["THE ALWAYS-OPEN ENTRY BOX: the Inbox\u2019s top box never disappears \u2014 speak into it or type into it any time, and ENTER (or \u2713/Send) posts the thought and blanks the box for the next one. Shift+Enter makes a new line; an empty Enter does nothing.","Every folder \u2014 including ones you create later \u2014 has its own entry box at the top: type a thought, Enter files it straight into that folder, ready for the next."],t.s)
B.avb=new A.t(508,B.a1p)
B.a7g=s(["The Listening List\u2019s \u201cAll\u201d now means exactly what it says there: everything MARKED TO LISTEN \u2014 your tagged folders and loose tagged items as one playable entry, count matching the \u201citems will play\u201d total. (v506 briefly played the whole library; corrected.)"],t.s)
B.asK=new A.t(507,B.a7g)
B.a3u=s(["ALL MOVED TO THE LISTENING AREA, done right: a pinned \u201cAll \u2014 your entire library\u201d entry at the top of the Listening List, always in sync with everything you have (it IS the library \u2014 folders in order, then loose items). Play now plays from it: your whole collection, no tags needed. Yesterday\u2019s All folder is retired from the folders area automatically. Tagged folders below keep their own play buttons."],t.s)
B.aq_=new A.t(506,B.a3u)
B.a6K=s(["Collapsed rows: the \u25b8 arrow is back on the far left (tap it to open) and title editing is back in place \u2014 with the cleaner look kept (no headphones/trash on the line). Open boxes fold from their \u25be or anywhere on the top strip. Every folder and subfolder screen carries the fold-all/open-all toggle top right."],t.s)
B.auP=new A.t(505,B.a6K)
B.X0=s(["ONE-TAP OPEN AND CLOSE: collapsed items are now a clean title line \u2014 no chevron, no buttons \u2014 and tapping ANYWHERE on the line opens the box; tapping an open box\u2019s top strip (badge/date row) closes it. Folders get the fold-all/open-all toggle at the top right too, scoped to that folder. (Collapsed-title editing traded for the one-tap open \u2014 titles still edit in the open box.)"],t.s)
B.av1=new A.t(504,B.X0)
B.Yq=s(["THE \u201cALL\u201d FOLDER: every new item and folder links itself into a folder named All, automatically, from any device \u2014 tap its headphones and your entire ongoing library is ONE entry in the listening program. Filing an item elsewhere never pulls it out of All (links, not moves). Going-forward from today; say the word if you want history backfilled too."],t.s)
B.atk=new A.t(503,B.Yq)
B.a0a=s(["OUTLINE ITEMS \u2014 items inside items: drop any item\u2019s \u2630 onto another item and it nests underneath (hold Ctrl to LINK it there instead, so the same item lives in several places \u2014 it wears a \u29c9 badge with its home count). A \u201cN sub-items\u201d line under a parent unfolds them, indented, to any depth, in the Inbox and in folders. The \u2296 removes a sub-item from that spot only; nothing is ever lost.","The full-size view always shows the item\u2019s DATE now \u2014 today\u2019s items were showing time only.","The Claude Send button tells you WHY when it can\u2019t send: signed out (fix in Settings) vs offline (it retries itself every 2 minutes)."],t.s)
B.awC=new A.t(502,B.a0a)
B.a_I=s(["DELETE AND EXPAND SWAPPED PLACES on every box: the trash now sits top-right, the \u2922 full-window button bottom-right \u2014 and on the live box the X moved left of the \u2922. Same actions, corners exchanged."],t.s)
B.asW=new A.t(501,B.a_I)
B.a7b=s(["RELEASE 500 \ud83c\udf89 \u2014 the \u2713 Done (\u201cnew note\u201d) pill moved to the bottom-right corner of the live box, where your thumb expects it. Same act: the note posts, the mic keeps rolling, your next words open a fresh box."],t.s)
B.avr=new A.t(500,B.a7b)
B.a4V=s(["MOVE ITEMS BETWEEN FOLDERS RIGHT IN THE LISTENING LIST: drag an item\u2019s \u2630 onto any folder header there and it MOVES into that folder (the header highlights as you hover). Same move rules as everywhere \u2014 moved, never copied, never lost."],t.s)
B.axo=new A.t(499,B.a4V)
B.a1y=s(["A \u2713 ON THE BOX YOU\u2019RE DICTATING INTO: tap Done \u2014 \u201cnew note\u201d in the label is the spoken twin \u2014 and the note posts as a finished item while dictation keeps running; your next words open a fresh box. Every visible control teaching its voice command is the new pattern."],t.s)
B.axq=new A.t(498,B.a1y)
B.a3E=s(["HOME IS NOW INBOX: the main stack is where every thought arrives before you file it \u2014 so it\u2019s named what it is. Nothing else moved."],t.s)
B.awe=new A.t(497,B.a3E)
B.a3i=s(["\u201cPLAY FOLDER\u201d WORKS MID-DICTATION: saying it (or tapping a folder\u2019s play) while the mic was live used to answer \u201cnothing to play\u201d \u2014 the mic guard was lying about a full folder. Now wanting to listen ends the dictation for you, everywhere: the mic stops, your last words finish transcribing in the background, and the folder starts reading."],t.s)
B.avp=new A.t(496,B.a3i)
B.a76=s(["REORDER YOUR LISTENING PROGRAM: hold any folder in the Listening List and drag it up or down \u2014 your order is the PLAY order (Play-all follows it top to bottom instead of the alphabet). A newly tagged folder joins at the end until you place it."],t.s)
B.apU=new A.t(495,B.a76)
B.a4U=s(["CLAUDE MESSAGES CAN\u2019T GET STUCK ANYMORE: two holes closed. Sync\u2019s recording-pause could become permanent if its resume was ever missed (the Windows desktop booted paused and stayed that way \u2014 thousands of rows queued, messages never sent); a watchdog now heals any stale pause within 2 minutes while a real recording still keeps sync off. And the Claude sender now runs on its own clock, independent of sync entirely \u2014 a written message delivers within ~2 minutes of sitting still, from any device, no matter what sync is doing."],t.s)
B.aqn=new A.t(494,B.a4U)
B.YI=s(["CLOSE THE WINDOW YOU\u2019RE DICTATING INTO: the fold chevron now shows on the box that has your cursor while dictating \u2014 tap it and that box saves, folds to its title, and your words flow straight back into the live box at the top. The mic never stops."],t.s)
B.aq7=new A.t(493,B.YI)
B.a4x=s(["CLEANER PHONE ROWS: the \u2261 handle is gone from items on the phone \u2014 hold the item itself to reorder (that shipped in v490/491), so the handle was just noise. Desktop keeps it: there it also drags items into folders. Folder rows keep their \u2261 everywhere."],t.s)
B.avI=new A.t(492,B.a4x)
B.a1q=s(["HOLD TO REORDER ON THE HOME SCREEN TOO: press and hold any item on Home and move it up or down \u2014 your order sticks on this device. New dictations still land on top so the newest thing stays visible, and the live dictation box stays planted while it runs."],t.s)
B.awH=new A.t(491,B.a1q)
B.Y_=s(["HOLD ANYWHERE TO REORDER: press and hold any item in a folder \u2014 collapsed or open \u2014 and it lifts to reorder right there. The \u2261 still moves it to a folder when held, and holding on the words still selects text."],t.s)
B.awN=new A.t(490,B.Y_)
B.Xr=s(["ONE HANDLE DOES BOTH: in a folder, the single left \u2261 now reorders when you drag it and MOVES TO A FOLDER when you hold it a beat first, then drag to the left pane (Ctrl at drop still links). The second grip inside the box is retired.","TYPE INTO A NEW ITEM IMMEDIATELY ON WINDOWS \u2014 the real fix, from the real trace: the \uff0b menu\u2019s closing animation was silently stealing the cursor a third of a second after the new box grabbed it, right after the old focus-keeper stopped watching. The keeper now outlasts the steal (and yields instantly if you click somewhere on purpose), and a brand-new empty note on desktop opens with its typing field live \u2014 first keystroke lands, no click needed."],t.s)
B.aq8=new A.t(489,B.Xr)
B.Xz=s(["THE OPENAI BILL GUARDS: multi-hour session audio no longer transcribes itself (tap Retry on the item when you actually want it \u2014 that\u2019s what turned into $21 today), failed uploads stop auto-retrying after 3 tries, a \u201cbudget reached\u201d verdict from OpenAI pauses background transcription for a day instead of hammering it, and automatic recovery now uses the half-price model. Dictation and live captions never depended on OpenAI \u2014 a tripped budget can\u2019t touch them.","Collapsed rows lose the color square \u2014 the border already tells you the type."],t.s)
B.avA=new A.t(488,B.Xz)
B.Yu=s(["UNDO AFTER DELETE: deleting an item now shows \u201cMoved to Trash \xb7 Undo\u201d for 6 seconds \u2014 one tap brings it straight back. The Trash stays your 30-day net either way."],t.s)
B.aqt=new A.t(487,B.Yu)
B.a6c=s(["Collapsed titles edit in place \u2014 tap the title on a folded item, type, done. Saves the title only; your words are never touched."],t.s)
B.asJ=new A.t(486,B.a6c)
B.a5J=s(["REORDER ITEMS IN FOLDERS ON WINDOWS TOO: the \u2261 handle now reorders on desktop just like the phone, and the box\u2019s grip still drags items into folders \u2014 both at once.","The \u201caudio isn\u2019t available\u201d message now tells you WHY: no cloud copy exists for that item (recorded on Windows or before audio sync), versus signed out or offline \u2014 different problems, different fixes."],t.s)
B.avl=new A.t(485,B.a5J)
B.a6n=s(["COLLAPSE ALL / OPEN ALL: the toggle sits at the top right of the main screen, across from the menu \u2014 one tap folds every item to its title line, tap again opens them all.","Collapsed items carry their headphones and delete buttons in the usual spots \u2014 listen or trash without opening the box.","The folder headphones icon is back to full size (shrinking it was never the fix \u2014 the hidden button floor was)."],t.s)
B.auz=new A.t(484,B.a6n)
B.a6X=s(["FOLDER ROWS FINALLY MATCH HOME\u2019S HEIGHT: the headphones button on every folder row had a built-in minimum height that silently blocked all the tightening \u2014 replaced; folders in the pane now sit as short as the rows above them."],t.s)
B.avN=new A.t(483,B.a6X)
B.a3D=s(["Collapsed items trade the type label for a small color square \u2014 more room for the title on one line."],t.s)
B.asI=new A.t(482,B.a3D)
B.a2o=s(["THE FOLDER SCREENS GOT THE DENSITY TOO: the Folders list and the folder rows inside folders were still at their old tall height while the left pane tightened \u2014 now they match (a third shorter). That was the \u201cfolders haven\u2019t changed at all\u201d miss.","Collapsed items wrap to two lines when the title needs it."],t.s)
B.aut=new A.t(481,B.a2o)
B.Yj=s(["Folder rows sit at their tightest everywhere \u2014 zero gap between rows, uniform top to bottom."],t.s)
B.apC=new A.t(480,B.Yj)
B.a7S=s(["COLLAPSE ANY ITEM TO ITS TITLE: every box now has a \u25b8 arrow at its top left \u2014 tap it and the item folds to one line (badge + title); tap again to reopen. Remembered per device. The live box you\u2019re dictating into never collapses."],t.s)
B.arq=new A.t(479,B.a7S)
B.a_M=s(["FOLDERS SIT CLOSER TOGETHER \u2014 on the phone too now, and desktop tightened one more notch. Noticeably more folders on one screen."],t.s)
B.arw=new A.t(478,B.a_M)
B.Xk=s(["CLAUDE MESSAGES SHOW ALL THREE TIMES: the status line now stamps each stage as it happens \u2014 \u201cSent 2:41pm \xb7 Received 2:43pm \xb7 Done 2:50pm\u201d \u2014 on the full-size view, synced across devices. Messages from before today show their latest time only (the history starts now)."],t.s)
B.aqh=new A.t(477,B.Xk)
B.a64=s(["SEND BUTTON ON EVERY UNSENT CLAUDE ITEM: any Claude message that hasn\u2019t gone out yet \u2014 typed in a folder, or stranded by a bad connection \u2014 now wears the Send pill right on its box. Tap it and the message goes immediately instead of waiting for the 2-minute auto-send."],t.s)
B.asR=new A.t(476,B.a64)
B.a0k=s(["READ ALOUD WHILE DICTATING: the button isn\u2019t grayed out anymore \u2014 tapping it mid-dictation asks \u201cEnd dictation and read aloud?\u201d, ends the recording cleanly (transcription finishes in the background), and starts reading.","NEW ITEMS TAKE THE CURSOR FOR REAL: a created item (Claude included) kept losing its cursor a frame after grabbing it \u2014 you had to click in manually. The cursor is now held until it sticks; just start typing.","THE DRAG CARD SHOWS MOVE VS LINK: dragging an item out of a folder shows a \u201cMove\u201d chip on the floating card \u2014 press Ctrl mid-drag and it flips to \u201c\u2303 Link\u201d live, so you always know what the drop will do.","MORE FOLDERS ON SCREEN (Windows): the left-pane rows are ~30% tighter on desktop. Phone rows stay finger-sized.","Claude items show WHEN: the full-size status line stamps the current stage with its time \u2014 \u201cDone \xb7 12:19pm\u201d. (Per-stage history for all three comes with a small server migration.)"],t.s)
B.asQ=new A.t(475,B.a0k)
B.a2g=s(["DRAGGING BETWEEN FOLDERS NOW MOVES: drag an item out of a folder onto another folder in the pane and it moves there \u2014 out of the old, into the new, exactly like Windows Explorer. Hold CTRL while dropping to LINK instead (the item lives in both). Drags from Home or lists still just file; if the item is in several folders, only the one you dragged from changes."],t.s)
B.arL=new A.t(474,B.a2g)
B.YN=s(["SEE \u2261, CAN DRAG \u2014 everywhere: the reorder handle moved to the LEFT of the folder icon (your call \u2014 the right side was crowded, and a left grab keeps the name visible while you drag), and it now shows on subfolder rows AND item boxes inside folders too. Those could always reorder \u2014 invisibly, by long-press; now the handle is the one drag surface and long-press is purely the options menu. In-folder order syncs across devices.","WINDOWS: hover over any left-pane row \u2014 folders included \u2014 and a tooltip shows the full name when it\u2019s cut off."],t.s)
B.atW=new A.t(473,B.YN)
B.ZU=s(["The folder options box now shows a folder icon beside its title \u2014 at a glance you know the word at the top is the folder\u2019s name."],t.s)
B.atn=new A.t(472,B.ZU)
B.a3w=s(["FOLDER REORDERING HAS DOORS NOW: tap the FOLDERS header in the left pane (or the new \u21c5 icon beside its +, or \u201cReorder Folders\u2026\u201d in any folder\u2019s \xb7\xb7\xb7 menu) to open the list where you drag folders into order. v469 built the room; v471 builds the doors.","NEW CLAUDE MESSAGE from any folder\u2019s \uff0b menu: type your message instead of speaking it \u2014 it files into that folder plus the Claude messages archive, and sends itself once you stop typing for 2 minutes (a half-typed message never sends early)."],t.s)
B.atE=new A.t(471,B.a3w)
B.WU=s(["SEND BUTTON ON CLAUDE MESSAGES: the box you're dictating a Claude message into now has an explicit Send pill at the top \u2014 tap it and the message posts right there, no need to click away. Same trip as before: Sending \u2192 received \u2192 done, reply in the box."],t.s)
B.apT=new A.t(470,B.WU)
B.a7H=s(["DRAG FOLDERS INTO ORDER: grab the \u2261 handle on any folder row (Folders screen) and slide it up or down \u2014 finger on the phone, mouse on Windows. The left pane follows the new order instantly, and Move Up/Down stays in the \xb7\xb7\xb7 menu for one-handed use."],t.s)
B.awG=new A.t(469,B.a7H)
B.a1o=s(["CLAUDE MESSAGES CAN\u2019T DIE IN LIMBO ANYMORE: a message that never reached Claude (signed out, offline, server hiccup) now re-sends itself automatically once you\u2019re signed in \u2014 same rhythm the status checker already runs. Your words were always saved; now they always arrive too."],t.s)
B.auM=new A.t(468,B.a1o)
B.Xh=s(["THE MIC CHECKS IN: if a recording runs two hours without a single touch, Voicley asks \u201cStill dictating?\u201d \u2014 Keep recording or Turn Off, your call, never automatic. Born from the two overnight sessions that transcribed the TV till morning.","The Windows app now re-checks for updates every couple of hours while it sits open \u2014 no more running days-old builds because the window never closed."],t.s)
B.ask=new A.t(467,B.Xh)
B.a2F=s(["EXPAND THE NOTE YOU\u2019RE CREATING: the live box now has the \u2922 button too \u2014 one tap closes the note and opens it full-size (capture pauses, exactly like opening any other item)."],t.s)
B.arO=new A.t(466,B.a2F)
B.a3n=s(["DELETE THE NOTE YOU\u2019RE CREATING: the live box (Claude ones included) now has an \u2715 at the top \u2014 tap it to wipe the note and keep dictating fresh. The words go on your explicit tap; the session recording still keeps all audio.","FULL-SIZE SCROLL STAYS PUT: scrolling an item no longer snaps back on its own \u2014 background refreshes were resetting the view to the top."],t.s)
B.arH=new A.t(465,B.a3n)
B.a5l=s(["The full-size header\u2019s time sits at the top right with proper breathing room from the edge \u2014 visible, compact (\u201c12:21am\u201d), box-style."],t.s)
B.asN=new A.t(464,B.a5l)
B.a62=s(["The full-size header\u2019s date/time is back on the right \u2014 box-style, compact (\u201c12:21am\u201d)."],t.s)
B.aqa=new A.t(463,B.a62)
B.a0Y=s(["MAXIMIZING A CLAUDE BOX OPENS THE RIGHT ITEM: filed-but-visible Claude messages were missing from the full-screen pager\u2019s list, so maximizing one jumped to a different item entirely. The list now matches what you see, and as a safety net a box that ever goes missing from it opens alone instead of opening the wrong thing."],t.s)
B.atq=new A.t(462,B.a0Y)
B.a7l=s(["THE FULL-SIZE VIEW WEARS THE BOX\u2019S BORDER: the title + text card now has the same type-colored outline the main-window boxes wear (blue note, green task, amber meeting, clay Claude) \u2014 the full view reads as a giant version of the box."],t.s)
B.auE=new A.t(461,B.a7l)
B.a2N=s(["FULL-SIZE POLISH: the \u201cTranscript\u201d labels are gone (the text just leads), the time moved in from the far right to sit beside the headphones, and it\u2019s compact now \u2014 \u201c12:21am\u201d for today, \u201cJul 11 \xb7 5:32pm\u201d for older items."],t.s)
B.asn=new A.t(460,B.a2N)
B.a0x=s(["FULL-SIZE VIEW DECLUTTERED: Read aloud now sits to the right of the folder chips, and the duplicate date/time under the text is gone (the header owns it) \u2014 the meta line keeps just the destination."],t.s)
B.auo=new A.t(459,B.a0x)
B.a1X=s(["THE FULL-SIZE HEADER NOW MATCHES THE BOXES: type badge on the left, the listening headphones right beside it (tap to add/remove \u2014 same control as the boxes), and the date/time pinned top right. The separate \u201cAdd to listening list\u201d card below is retired \u2014 one header, one control."],t.s)
B.aqR=new A.t(458,B.a1X)
B.a_E=s(["THE FULL-SIZE VIEW KNOWS WHAT IT IS: the top bar now shows the item\u2019s TYPE \u2014 the same colored badge the boxes wear (Note / Task / Meeting / Claude) \u2014 plus the date. And there\u2019s just ONE title now: big, bold, wrapping to two lines, and editable right in place \u2014 the little duplicate title field is gone."],t.s)
B.auc=new A.t(457,B.a_E)
B.a4M=s(["CLAUDE REPLIES YOU CAN ACTUALLY READ: the reply and the Sent \xb7 Received \xb7 Done ladder now show in BOTH big views of a Claude item \u2014 the tap-open sheet AND the full window \u2014 at full reading size (the reply in body text, the ladder a step bigger), pinned right under the message so no scrolling is needed."],t.s)
B.axb=new A.t(456,B.a4M)
B.XC=s(["CLAUDE ITEMS IN THE FULL WINDOW: opening a Claude message full-size now shows the whole round-trip \u2014 my reply right under your message and the Sent \xb7 Received \xb7 Done ladder beneath it, updating live as the desk works. The title bar says \u201cClaude\u201d for untitled ones too."],t.s)
B.aqN=new A.t(455,B.XC)
B.a0D=s(["INLINE EDITS STICK NOW: editing an item\u2019s text and leaving the window no longer shows the old text when you come back \u2014 your edits were saving safely underneath, but the main window kept showing its own stale copy. Every save now updates the window at the same instant it writes the database.","Notes finished on the desktop app now show up properly in the main window on every device (they were saved with a blank type that the window\u2019s filter couldn\u2019t see)."],t.s)
B.ara=new A.t(454,B.a0D)
B.a0Q=s(["DELETING AN ITEM NO LONGER JUMPS THE WINDOW OR THE CURSOR: deleting a box you were typing or dictating into used to hand the cursor to the item below it \u2014 the list scrolled to follow it, and your dictation started landing in the wrong note. Now a delete cleanly lets go first: the cursor drops, the list stays put, and dictation falls back to its own live box."],t.s)
B.arD=new A.t(453,B.a0Q)
B.a22=s(["YOUR PHONE STOPS FILLING UP: while charging and idle, Voicley quietly swaps the bulky raw audio of already-transcribed, already-uploaded recordings for verified compact copies \u2014 about 90% smaller per file, playing identically. Nothing is ever touched unless the transcript is safe, the upload is confirmed, and the replacement passes a duration check; every swap is listed in Settings \u2192 Item changes."],t.s)
B.asi=new A.t(452,B.a22)
B.a36=s(["WINDOWS PLAYS AUDIO NOW: the play button in the full item view (and everywhere else on desktop) actually plays your recordings \u2014 Windows was missing its audio engine entirely, so the button did nothing. The reader also plays real recordings on Windows now instead of always reading with the robot voice."],t.s)
B.atD=new A.t(451,B.a36)
B.a7T=s(["THE WHOLE TREE IN THE LEFT PANE: folders with subfolders now expand in place \u2014 tap the \u203a chevron to unfold a folder\u2019s subfolders right in the pane, nested to any depth, indented so you can see the shape of your library. The pane remembers what you left open. Rows keep everything: tap to open, long-press for actions, drag-to-file, counts, headphones."],t.s)
B.art=new A.t(450,B.a7T)
B.a08=s(["SPEAK THE DESTINATION: \u201cnew note in cash flow\u201d opens a fresh note that FILES ITSELF into that folder when it closes \u2014 the live box shows \u201c\u2192 cash flow\u201d so you can see where it\u2019s headed. Fuzzy name matching forgives filler and truncation; a genuinely new name creates the folder.","READ ANY FOLDER BY VOICE: in Commands only, \u201cread the cash flow folder\u201d (or \u201cplay ideas\u201d) reads that folder top to bottom. \u201cPause/resume reading\u201d, \u201cskip\u201d, and \u201cprevious item\u201d control playback."],t.s)
B.aue=new A.t(449,B.a08)
B.a6Z=s(["CLAUDE ANSWERS IN THE BOX: when the desk session finishes acting on your message, its one-line answer appears right in your clay box under the Done footer \u2014 \u201cClaude: Filed to brainstorm \u2713\u201d. Every message becomes a tiny completed conversation you can read from the phone."],t.s)
B.apf=new A.t(448,B.a6Z)
B.a81=s(["Sending a Claude message now turns dictation OFF automatically (unless you asked for a new note in the same breath) \u2014 no more open mic collecting the TV after your message ships.","The \u201cplay the listening folder\u201d command accepts your own phrases now \u2014 its row in Settings \u2192 Voice commands has the \uff0b phrase button like the rest."],t.s)
B.auI=new A.t(447,B.a81)
B.a0j=s(["Android now shows the real release number \u2014 \u201cv446\u201d \u2014 in App info and system dropdowns instead of the meaningless \u201c0.1.0\u201d. Settings footer matches."],t.s)
B.axd=new A.t(446,B.a0j)
B.a15=s(["Typing into a Claude box works like every other box now \u2014 its status updates were quietly rebuilding the screen under your finger and stealing the cursor. The screen no longer repaints while your cursor is in any box.","Network log dates are readable now \u2014 brighter and a step bolder."],t.s)
B.apx=new A.t(445,B.a15)
B.a09=s(["\u201cNew Claude command\u201d now works too \u2014 message, note, or command, whichever word comes out.","The Network log now reads human-first: each incident leads with its plain-English story at full size, and the raw technical detail sits under its own \u201cTECHNICAL\u201d divider \u2014 collapsed until you tap it."],t.s)
B.ax6=new A.t(444,B.a09)
B.a0X=s(["DICTATION RELIABILITY: the engine is now told to listen for \u201cClaude\u201d and \u201cVoicley\u201d by name (far fewer clod/quad mishearings at the source).","Words spoken while the connection is down are RECOVERED: when a dead network outlasts the live buffer, the missing stretch is re-transcribed from the kept audio when you stop, and the words join the session\u2019s recording. Tonight\u2019s 8-minute outage would have healed itself.","The live box stopped flashing: the grey draft now only grows as you speak \u2014 the polished rewrite lands once, when each passage finalizes, instead of repainting every half-second guess.","Fixed: starting a Claude message, saying nothing, and then \u201cnew note\u201d made the NEW note a Claude box \u2014 the leftover addressing now dies with the empty box it belonged to."],t.s)
B.apy=new A.t(443,B.a0X)
B.a_e=s(["\u201cNew Claude message\u201d \u2014 the pause-after-the-phrase case: saying the command and then pausing was splitting the word \u201cmessage\u201d off the end and the command never fired. A complete phrase now always wins. \u201cNewclaud\u201d as one squashed word counts too."],t.s)
B.apa=new A.t(442,B.a_e)
B.a0l=s(["\u201cNew Claude message\u201d now survives being split across transcription chunks \u2014 the exact failure from tonight\u2019s log (the engine heard \u201cNew clod\u201d \u2026 \u201cmessage\u201d as two pieces) is caught and reassembled, so the command fires even when the recognizer breathes mid-phrase."],t.s)
B.arZ=new A.t(441,B.a0l)
B.a_v=s(["Claude messages ACTUALLY appear on the main screen now \u2014 the screen\u2019s loader had a pre-v434 list of item types that silently dropped them (they lived in the folder but never on Home). Your existing messages all show up after this update.","The Android back button, for real this time: Android 13+ routes the button around the fix v438 used; that routing is now configured so back steps back everywhere. Third time\u2019s the proven one."],t.s)
B.asD=new A.t(440,B.a_v)
B.a3g=s(["\u201cNew Claude message\u201d now actually works: it fires mid-dictation like \u201cnew task\u201d (closing your current note and opening a fresh Claude box), and it forgives how the engine hears the name \u2014 \u201cnew clot message\u201d, \u201cnew quad message\u201d and friends all count."],t.s)
B.asB=new A.t(439,B.a3g)
B.a7d=s(["Claude messages now STAY on the main screen (and in the Claude chip) after they complete \u2014 they were vanishing into their folder seconds after sending because the desk session got fast. The \u201cClaude messages\u201d folder remains their permanent archive.","The Android back button truly behaves like the app\u2019s \u2039 back now \u2014 v436\u2019s fix registered at the wrong layer and Settings still minimized; this one intercepts the button itself. Dialogs still close first, and on Home the button still minimizes as Android expects."],t.s)
B.apq=new A.t(438,B.a7d)
B.a_L=s(["YOUR OWN WORDS for voice commands: in Settings \u2192 Voice commands, every command now has a \u201c\uff0b phrase\u201d \u2014 add the wording that comes naturally (\u201cthat\u2019s all\u201d for end note, \u201chey Claude\u201d for message for Claude\u2026). Your phrases ADD to the built-ins, they never replace them, and they\u2019re checked so no two commands can collide."],t.s)
B.apH=new A.t(437,B.a_L)
B.X9=s(["The Android back button now behaves like the app\u2019s \u2039 back everywhere: in Settings, Files, folders and the rest it steps back (or returns Home) instead of minimizing the app. On the Home screen it still minimizes, as Android convention expects."],t.s)
B.ar_=new A.t(436,B.X9)
B.YJ=s(["Create items INSIDE a folder: a new \uff0b at the top of every folder makes a note, task, or meeting that\u2019s born already filed there \u2014 and the cursor lands in its box so you can type (or tap its body and dictate) immediately.","\u201cNew Claude message\u201d now works as a spoken opener too (same as \u201cmessage for Claude\u201d) \u2014 it\u2019s the phrase that came naturally, so now it counts."],t.s)
B.asx=new A.t(435,B.YJ)
B.a6H=s(["CLAUDE MESSAGES are now real items: \u201cmessage for Claude \u2026\u201d (or \uff0b \u2192 Message for Claude) makes its own clay-colored box beside your notes, tasks and meetings. Its footer shows the round trip live \u2014 Sending\u2026 \u2192 Sent \u2192 Received \u2192 Done \u2014 so you can see the moment the desk session picks it up.","A \u201cClaude messages\u201d folder appears in the left pane automatically and collects every message; while one is still in flight it also stays on the main window so you can watch its status.","New Claude view chip on the main window counts today\u2019s messages; edits you make inside any item now show up in its main-window box."],t.s)
B.auq=new A.t(434,B.a6H)
B.a6v=s(["NEW voice command for the road: start a dictation with \u201cmessage for Claude\u201d and everything you say goes to your Claude session when the note closes (\u201cend note\u201d or Off). The end-of-note \u201csend to Claude\u201d still works too. Both listed in Settings \u2192 Voice commands."],t.s)
B.ar5=new A.t(433,B.a6v)
B.a7E=s(["Full-screen items now use the same text size as the boxes on the main window (both follow your transcript size setting \u2014 Speakers blocks included).","Fixed: hitting Stop on the Android listening notification left Play dead. Play now restarts the list right where you stopped it."],t.s)
B.aqY=new A.t(432,B.a7E)
B.a6h=s(["THE ITEM GUARD: nothing can destroy an item unless you told it to. Every delete now carries its reason; automatic cleanups may only remove items that are verifiably empty at the moment of deletion (the bug that killed real notes mid-save is impossible now); and any code that tries to destroy an item without permission is refused and flagged.","NEW in Settings \u2192 Diagnostics: \u201cItem changes\u201d \u2014 a plain-English history of every delete, restore, and cleanup on this device, with its reason. Anything BLOCKED shows in red with a count on the Settings row, so a misbehaving cleanup gets caught in the log instead of in your library."],t.s)
B.asl=new A.t(431,B.a6h)
B.a0t=s(["RECOVERY: the items that disappeared in the cleanup incident come back on their own \u2014 this build re-matches each restored item to its recording file (which never left your phone) and re-transcribes it. Leave the app open and idle for a couple of minutes, then check your folders.","\u201cEnd dictation\u201d / \u201cstop dictation\u201d now turns dictation fully Off \u2014 the same as tapping Off. For the middle state (pause but keep listening for commands), say \u201ccommands only\u201d.","Faster catch-up: interrupted transcriptions now keep draining while the app sits idle, instead of three per app-open."],t.s)
B.awh=new A.t(430,B.a0t)
B.Xd=s(["New voice command: say \u201ccommands only\u201d (at the end of a thought) to pause dictation without closing your note \u2014 the spoken twin of the middle button. \u201cResume dictation\u201d picks it back up. Also listed in Settings \u2192 Voice commands."],t.s)
B.at9=new A.t(429,B.Xd)
B.a7P=s(["URGENT fix: yesterday\u2019s automatic cleanup could wrongly remove recordings that were still waiting to upload when the library was opened on a new device (they looked empty from there). The cleanup is removed entirely. Disappeared items are restorable and are being restored."],t.s)
B.auX=new A.t(428,B.a7P)
B.a0c=s(["NEW: a Network log in Settings (under Advanced \u2192 Diagnostics). Every network hiccup \u2014 DNS failures, stalled captions, dropped connections, failed uploads, unreachable sync \u2014 is recorded with a plain-English explanation of what happened, and each issue type shows how many times it has occurred. Bursts group into one entry."],t.s)
B.awB=new A.t(427,B.a0c)
B.a2Q=s(["Dictation and command accuracy restored: live speaker detection (added yesterday for the Speakers view) was quietly taxing the recognizer, so it\u2019s off the live stream. Who-said-what returns via a smarter after-the-fact pass soon."],t.s)
B.arv=new A.t(426,B.a2Q)
B.a_q=s(["Web: your synced items can now actually appear \u2014 the web version was reading a separate empty database from the one sync fills; they\u2019re one and the same now, exactly like on your phones."],t.s)
B.at5=new A.t(425,B.a_q)
B.a2u=s(["The sync sign-in got a show-password eye \u2014 tap it to see what you typed before hitting Sign in. (The website\u2019s web-app login got the same.)"],t.s)
B.au_=new A.t(424,B.a2u)
B.a1F=s(["The web version (preview) can now sign in to Library Sync \u2014 same account as your phones, in Settings."],t.s)
B.aqV=new A.t(423,B.a1F)
B.a2j=s(["The \u201c+\u201d create menu now works from Commands only \u2014 even with a task or meeting box open. Creating switches you into capture for the new item, same as the spoken command."],t.s)
B.auF=new A.t(422,B.a2j)
B.a6t=s(["The 0:00 items are explained and cleaned up: they were meeting/note placeholders orphaned when a session was killed mid-way. A launch sweep now purges day-old empty ones automatically \u2014 anything with words or audio is never touched.","Fixed a narrow loss window: typing into a blank note and hitting Off within a second could destroy the just-typed words. The blank-note cleanup now waits out the save and never touches a note your cursor is in."],t.s)
B.aqw=new A.t(421,B.a6t)
B.a4J=s(["Fixed: a flowing note could wrap past 10 lines and scroll inside its box \u2014 wrapped length now counts toward the 10-line cap, so long notes become \u201cmore\u2026\u201d cards like they should.","This update dialog now shows each release number above its changes."],t.s)
B.awA=new A.t(420,B.a4J)
B.Zd=s(["Items now cap at 10 lines everywhere (notes, tasks, and meetings alike) \u2014 long ones show their first 10 lines with \u201cmore\u2026\u201d and open full-screen. Steadier scrolling too."],t.s)
B.asT=new A.t(419,B.Zd)
B.a52=s(["Creating items moved UP: a \u201c+\u201d now sits at the end of the top labels row \u2014 tap it to create a Note, Task, or Meeting. The dedicated buttons at the bottom are gone, so the bottom is just the Off/Commands/Dictate control and the list gets more room."],t.s)
B.av6=new A.t(418,B.a52)
B.a_c=s(["NEW: speakers. When a recording has more than one voice, its full-screen view shows the conversation broken out by speaker \u2014 \u201cSpeaker 1 / Speaker 2\u201d blocks with each person\u2019s words, and a play button on every block that plays exactly that stretch of audio.","Applies to NEW recordings made with the live (Deepgram) engine from this version on \u2014 older items don\u2019t have speaker data."],t.s)
B.apI=new A.t(417,B.a_c)
B.a5S=s(["Fixed: long items scroll again on Android \u2014 dragging anywhere on the text now moves the page (the text area was swallowing the drag)."],t.s)
B.apb=new A.t(416,B.a5S)
B.Yz=s(["Settings now lists every voice command: tap \u201cVOICE COMMANDS\u201d to see the current phrases, what each does, and their alternate wordings."],t.s)
B.ave=new A.t(415,B.Yz)
B.a7a=s(["Saying \u201cstop dictation\u201d now turns off dictation, same as \u201cend dictation\u201d (also: \u201cstop note\u201d, \u201cstop dictating\u201d)."],t.s)
B.auL=new A.t(414,B.a7a)
B.a_T=s(["Fixed: while dictating, tapping an item to move the cursor there now works on the FIRST tap. Putting the cursor in an item no longer snaps the list to the top (which was scrolling the item away and eating your first tap)."],t.s)
B.auk=new A.t(413,B.a_T)
B.a23=s(["Fixed: deleting an item no longer scrolls the main list. The list now holds its place when you remove a row instead of jumping down."],t.s)
B.at3=new A.t(412,B.a23)
B.Za=s(["Fixed: only ONE item ever shows as selected. The focus ring now moves to the item you click instead of piling up on previous ones (was most visible on Windows).","Windows now tells you automatically when an update is ready \u2014 an \u201cUpdate available\u201d box pops up on launch (and when you click back into the window), just like the Android update notice. No more digging into Settings to find it."],t.s)
B.auh=new A.t(411,B.Za)
B.a_2=s(["Fixed: one tap puts the cursor in an item again (adding the focus ring was briefly stealing the first tap)."],t.s)
B.aqH=new A.t(410,B.a_2)
B.a5O=s(["The focused-item ring now shows on the MAIN list too (it was only wired into folder views)."],t.s)
B.aw4=new A.t(409,B.a5O)
B.a0y=s(["The item holding your cursor now wears the same colored ring the new-note box has \u2014 red and breathing while capturing, calm accent otherwise \u2014 so you can always SEE where dictation goes.","Editing or dictating into a note refreshes its AI title automatically."],t.s)
B.av_=new A.t(408,B.a0y)
B.a5C=s(["Fixed: typing into a new note and then tapping an existing item no longer conjures another empty note at the top \u2014 the typed note posts and that\u2019s it."],t.s)
B.arV=new A.t(407,B.a5C)
B.a07=s(["The new note box matches regular items exactly: same size (1-line, grows with content), the move handle beside it on desktop, and no microphone icon \u2014 the bottom control is the capture toggle."],t.s)
B.arG=new A.t(406,B.a07)
B.a5e=s(["The message inside a new note box is gone \u2014 a new note is just an empty box with a cursor.","Tapping \u201cNew note\u201d repeatedly creates multiple blank notes (each tap posts the previous blank and opens a fresh one).","Any note still blank when you turn the system Off is destroyed \u2014 including blanks that posted to the list. Type or dictate anything into one and it stays."],t.s)
B.avP=new A.t(405,B.a5e)
B.a6G=s(["Clicking out of a new note now ALWAYS makes it a regular item \u2014 even a blank one posts (as an empty \u201cTap to add text\u201d item). Going back in works like any other item: tap it, get a cursor, dictate.","Fixed the dead tap on the live note box \u2014 tapping it now reliably opens the editor with a cursor."],t.s)
B.ax5=new A.t(404,B.a6G)
B.a0u=s(["Clicking between items never changes the mode anymore: the cursor only picks WHERE words go \u2014 the Off / Commands only / Dictate control (and the voice commands) are the only things that turn dictation on or off."],t.s)
B.avz=new A.t(403,B.a0u)
B.a28=s(["A just-posted note is editable IMMEDIATELY \u2014 tapping back into it works the moment it appears (its database row now lands before the slow audio processing, not after).","Fixed words being EATEN right after you tapped from a new note into an existing item \u2014 the close was briefly flipping the session into commands-only and dropping what you said next. Dictation mode now stays on across that move."],t.s)
B.awV=new A.t(402,B.a28)
B.a_X=s(["Dictate truly creates nothing now: the note box can no longer summon itself \u2014 not on the button, and not from the live preview while you dictate into an existing item (that was the phantom \u201cnew note\u201d at the top). Only \u201cnew note\u201d creates a note.","Tapping back into the new-note box reliably takes the cursor and routes your words there.","Play in the Listening list now stops an active dictation for you and starts playing \u2014 no more \u201cstop any recording and try again.\u201d"],t.s)
B.auZ=new A.t(401,B.a_X)
B.Xo=s(["Fixed: the Android notification could claim \u201ccommands only\u201d while you were dictating \u2014 it now mirrors every mode change (including dictating by placing your cursor in an item), and a session that starts in Commands is labeled correctly from the first moment."],t.s)
B.avE=new A.t(400,B.Xo)
B.a7M=s(["Fixed: tapping Dictate no longer pops an empty note box \u2014 nothing appears until you actually have words (or say \u201cnew note\u201d). Rule 1 for real this time.","The middle segment now reads \u201cCommands only.\u201d"],t.s)
B.asF=new A.t(399,B.a7M)
B.a5p=s(["The purple \u201cListening for commands\u201d label is gone \u2014 the highlighted Commands segment on the bottom control already shows the mode."],t.s)
B.aw8=new A.t(398,B.a5p)
B.a6y=s(["New 3-way control at the bottom: Off \xb7 Commands \xb7 Dictate. Your current mode is always highlighted, and switching is one tap. Dictate turns dictation on (words land wherever your cursor is); Commands listens for voice commands only; Off is instant \u2014 the 3-second countdown is gone.","\u201cnew note\u201d is the only thing that creates a note \u2014 it opens at the top with the cursor in it. A blank note you click away from stays put; a blank note is discarded when you turn the system Off.","New voice command: dictate a message and say \u201csend to Claude\u201d \u2014 it\u2019s delivered to Roger\u2019s Claude inbox with a \u2713 confirmation.","Live captions never give up: on flaky network the app now keeps retrying for the whole session and captions come back by themselves (it used to quit after ~12 seconds and stay text-dead).","The screen stopped moving: no more shifting when the keyboard opens, no frame flicker toggling dictation (the desktop jump), and only the box holding the cursor looks \u201clive.\u201d","The \u201cVoicley updated\u201d notification now opens the app when you tap it."],t.s)
B.avc=new A.t(397,B.a6y)
B.a7k=s(["Diagnostics: the main list records exactly what scrolled it, to pin down the \u201clist moves when I delete\u201d report."],t.s)
B.awi=new A.t(396,B.a7k)
B.Zn=s(["A blank new note is destroyed when you turn the system Off; the four dictation rules are locked into the spec."],t.s)
B.au8=new A.t(395,B.Zn)
B.a2M=s(["The Dictate button never creates a note \u2014 it just turns dictation on; words land wherever your cursor is."],t.s)
B.arY=new A.t(394,B.a2M)
B.a2E=s(["Switching to Commands no longer vanishes a just-opened blank note \u2014 it stays, paused, and Dictate resumes into it."],t.s)
B.arF=new A.t(393,B.a2E)
B.a3d=s(["The bottom control is a 3-way toggle now \u2014 Off \xb7 Commands \xb7 Dictate \u2014 replacing the single On/Off button."],t.s)
B.awg=new A.t(392,B.a3d)
B.a_r=s(["A new note takes the cursor at session start too \u2014 an item you\u2019d tapped earlier can no longer steal the first words."],t.s)
B.awa=new A.t(391,B.a_r)
B.a0G=s(["Creating a new note puts the cursor in it, ready to dictate; a blank note you click away from stays at the top."],t.s)
B.awM=new A.t(390,B.a0G)
B.a6S=s(["Off stops instantly \u2014 the 3-second \u201cOff in 3\u20262\u20261\u201d countdown is gone."],t.s)
B.apZ=new A.t(389,B.a6S)
B.a4Z=s(["Moving the cursor into an item ends the live note cleanly: it posts as a regular item and your words follow the cursor."],t.s)
B.arA=new A.t(388,B.a4Z)
B.ZO=s(["Live captions never give up on a bad network \u2014 retries continue for the whole session and captions return by themselves."],t.s)
B.ard=new A.t(387,B.ZO)
B.a7q=s(["Only the box holding the cursor looks \u201clive\u201d \u2014 the new-note box calms down when you dictate into another item."],t.s)
B.ax9=new A.t(386,B.a7q)
B.Zl=s(["Diagnostics: every dictated phrase logs where it landed, so a \u201cmy words vanished\u201d report is answerable from the log."],t.s)
B.atH=new A.t(385,B.Zl)
B.a5x=s(["Putting the cursor in an item while recording resumes dictation there (it used to stay paused)."],t.s)
B.arW=new A.t(384,B.a5x)
B.YF=s(["The window frame no longer appears/disappears when toggling dictation \u2014 the desktop screen stops jumping."],t.s)
B.arJ=new A.t(383,B.YF)
B.a_Y=s(["A new note opens 2 lines tall, then grows with your words (the 15-line cap is unchanged)."],t.s)
B.ati=new A.t(382,B.a_Y)
B.a0Z=s(["The \u201cVoicley updated\u201d notification opens the app when tapped; the screen no longer shifts entering edit mode; the mic meter and status moved below the On/Off button."],t.s)
B.aw6=new A.t(381,B.a0Z)
B.a1O=s(["New voice command: \u201csend to Claude\u201d \u2014 dictate a message and it\u2019s delivered to the Claude inbox, with a \u2713 confirmation."],t.s)
B.au5=new A.t(380,B.a1O)
B.a3e=s(["New \u201cAsk Claude\u201d in the nav: dictate or type a message and chat with Claude, right in Voicley (add your own Anthropic API key once \u2014 stored securely on device). First step of sending messages to Claude from the app."],t.s)
B.ary=new A.t(379,B.a3e)
B.Xp=s(["Fixed the note box flickering on and off while \u201clistening for commands\u201d \u2014 the live preview no longer flashes a box as background speech comes and goes."],t.s)
B.atR=new A.t(378,B.Xp)
B.a5M=s(["Fixed: turning On really shows no note now \u2014 the session opens directly in \u201clistening for commands\u201d from the first frame, instead of briefly popping a blank note."],t.s)
B.avW=new A.t(377,B.a5M)
B.a3r=s(["Turning On no longer creates a blank note \u2014 it simply turns the system on and listens for commands. Say \u201cnew note\u201d (or tap a note) to start transcribing."],t.s)
B.aqz=new A.t(376,B.a3r)
B.a5d=s(["Fixed a crash + vanishing dictation when you started recording while the listening reader was playing: the reader now fully stops (releasing its audio) the instant you turn dictation on, so the mic always has a clean path."],t.s)
B.aqs=new A.t(375,B.a5d)
B.a58=s(["The per-item microphone buttons are gone: the system is simply On/Off now, and dictation follows your cursor \u2014 tap any note while recording and your words land there."],t.s)
B.awY=new A.t(374,B.a58)
B.a0p=s(["Dictation now follows your cursor: while recording, tap into any note and your words land there; tap back to the new-note box and they go there. No per-item mic needed \u2014 the cursor decides."],t.s)
B.auU=new A.t(373,B.a0p)
B.a_F=s(["One cursor at a time: tapping into an existing note now hides the live box\u2019s blinking cursor, so there\u2019s only ever a single cursor \u2014 the first step toward \u201cdictation goes wherever the cursor is.\u201d"],t.s)
B.av2=new A.t(372,B.a_F)
B.ZK=s(["Listening now has real system controls: a media notification + lock-screen transport (play/pause/next/previous), and it responds to Bluetooth media buttons \u2014 including a tap on your Ray-Ban Meta glasses to pause/resume. Works for both spoken (TTS) and recorded playback."],t.s)
B.auK=new A.t(371,B.ZK)
B.Ys=s(["Fixed: you can type into a fresh new note again \u2014 its tap target was too thin to focus. It\u2019s a comfortable size now (still not tall).","The Listening player is now two rows: the current item\u2019s overview on top, the play controls below. Tap the overview (\u25ce) to jump the list back to whatever\u2019s playing."],t.s)
B.arU=new A.t(370,B.Ys)
B.a3c=s(["Settings is tidier: \u201cDictation destination\u201d and \u201cRecording segmentation\u201d moved into the collapsible Advanced section at the bottom, so the everyday controls lead."],t.s)
B.awf=new A.t(369,B.a3c)
B.a5y=s(["Play in the Listening list now stays ON THE LIST and follows the playing item down through folders and subfolders \u2014 it no longer jumps the first item into the full-screen player.","A fresh \u201cnew note\u201d box is the right height again \u2014 a blank note no longer opens very tall."],t.s)
B.arN=new A.t(368,B.a5y)
B.a7Z=s(["The Listening list now shows ONLY what will play \u2014 an item you\u2019ve un-toggled inside a folder no longer lingers in the list.","Playback now traverses the list in the exact order you see it: a folder\u2019s own items first (top to bottom), then into its subfolders \u2014 so the view follows straight down the list as it plays."],t.s)
B.ar2=new A.t(367,B.a7Z)
B.a5z=s(["New voice command: while listening for commands (after \u201cend note\u201d), say \u201cplay the listening folder\u201d (or \u201cplay my list\u201d / \u201cstart listening\u201d) and Voicley ends the session and starts reading your listening list \u2014 hands-free."],t.s)
B.atK=new A.t(366,B.a5z)
B.a7K=s(["The Listening list now FOLLOWS playback through the whole hierarchy: as each item finishes, the view scrolls to the next one, and when playback moves into a subfolder that folder opens automatically \u2014 the playing item is always in view."],t.s)
B.atP=new A.t(365,B.a7K)
B.a30=s(["A new note shows a BLINKING CURSOR from the moment it appears \u2014 always on, right where the next words will land (no keyboard pops up).","The mic label no longer claims \u201cnot available\u201d before you\u2019ve said anything: a quiet room now reads \u201cListening \u2014 waiting for sound\u201d; red is reserved for a mic that actually cut out."],t.s)
B.avL=new A.t(364,B.a30)
B.a2r=s(["Your cursor now SURVIVES the mic tap: tapping a note\u2019s mic (or the live box\u2019s) no longer hides the caret \u2014 you can see exactly where the dictation will land."],t.s)
B.awQ=new A.t(363,B.a2r)
B.a3F=s(["Dictating with the cursor inside a word no longer splits it \u2014 the new words land right AFTER that word.","New notes are the right height: a closed note no longer keeps invisible trailing blank lines that made a 1\u20132 line note render very tall (existing tall items flatten too)."],t.s)
B.aul=new A.t(362,B.a3F)
B.a1B=s(["Boxes show up to 15 LINES of text again \u2014 the original spec: a long note lists its first 15 lines with a \u201cmore\u2026\u201d button into the full window (the old rule collapsed anything past ~2 sentences to a 4-line preview)."],t.s)
B.awk=new A.t(361,B.a1B)
B.a_N=s(["Dictated words STAY where they land: a note growing past the \u201clarge\u201d threshold mid-dictation (or mid-typing) no longer swaps its box out from under your cursor \u2014 the transcribed text no longer appears and then vanishes."],t.s)
B.arg=new A.t(360,B.a_N)
B.a1t=s(["Dictating into an item now changes NOTHING on screen: the mic meter/chips stay hidden, the list doesn\u2019t shift, and your box, cursor and keyboard hold perfectly still."],t.s)
B.apN=new A.t(359,B.a1t)
B.a2x=s(["Fixed for real: tap an item\u2019s mic and the box holds perfectly still \u2014 your cursor stays exactly where you put it, and dictation lands there, not at the bottom."],t.s)
B.ax2=new A.t(358,B.a2x)
B.a70=s(["The mic on an item works the simple way again: for notes under ~15 lines, tapping it changes NOTHING \u2014 same box, same text, and your dictation lands right at your cursor, freely mixed with typing."],t.s)
B.aqO=new A.t(357,B.a70)
B.a1e=s(["Edit mode fixed for real: every note\u2019s text is now fully visible and reachable while editing \u2014 the field follows your cursor and scrolls when needed. Longer notes go to the full window automatically."],t.s)
B.ar7=new A.t(356,B.a1e)
B.a6R=s(["Tapping an item\u2019s mic no longer shrinks the box \u2014 the note\u2019s text stays fully visible while you dictate into it (large notes keep their preview height).","This \u201cWhat\u2019s new\u201d popup is easier to read \u2014 bigger text."],t.s)
B.arK=new A.t(355,B.a6R)
B.a2s=s(["Long notes no longer fight the list: a big item shows as a compact preview (title + first lines + \u201ctap to open\u201d), and its reading/editing lives in the full window. Short notes still edit right in place."],t.s)
B.aqx=new A.t(354,B.a2s)
B.a5T=s(["Dictation now lands WHERE YOUR CURSOR IS when you\u2019re typing \u2014 and the live grey preview flows inline with your words instead of flashing on a line below.","The reader now speaks each item\u2019s TITLE before its text."],t.s)
B.au4=new A.t(353,B.a5T)
B.a5_=s(["Listening is now ONE simple switch per item: the headphones tag decides if it plays \u2014 uncheck any item (even inside a playing folder) and it\u2019s out of the playlist; a folder\u2019s headphones just flips everything under it. Playback follows your folder order."],t.s)
B.aqI=new A.t(352,B.a5_)
B.Yy=s(["The listening list now shows how many items each folder will play (whole subtree) and a total under the play controls \u2014 the numbers always match what actually plays."],t.s)
B.ar3=new A.t(351,B.Yy)
B.Zt=s(["The listening list\u2019s controls moved to the TOP: Play-all sits there when idle, and while playing it becomes the full transport \u2014 previous \xb7 play/pause \xb7 next \xb7 stop, with the current title."],t.s)
B.apG=new A.t(350,B.Zt)
B.a2n=s(["Silent recordings no longer clutter Home as \u201cTranscribing\u2026\u201d \u2014 they stay in Files with their audio, and a manual retry can still transcribe one."],t.s)
B.asy=new A.t(349,B.a2n)
B.a5Z=s(["Put your top-level folders in YOUR order: long-press a folder \u2192 Move Up / Move Down \u2014 the order sticks on this device. (Drag-to-reorder polish comes next.)"],t.s)
B.aqE=new A.t(348,B.a5Z)
B.a2P=s(["The Android notification now shows what\u2019s happening live: \u201cDictating\u201d while your words are captured, \u201cPaused \u2014 commands only\u201d while command-listening."],t.s)
B.apO=new A.t(347,B.a2P)
B.a7A=s(["The full-sized item view now leads with the TEXT \u2014 the transcript sits right at the top, with date, folders, player and summary below it."],t.s)
B.at2=new A.t(346,B.a7A)
B.a3j=s(["The folder pane no longer flashes when you toggle a note\u2019s listen switch (especially on Windows) \u2014 it holds steady through background syncs."],t.s)
B.ari=new A.t(345,B.a3j)
B.a1Y=s(["Fixed the main home list jumping around / snapping to the top on every background sync \u2014 it now stays put so you can scroll and focus (especially on Windows)."],t.s)
B.apz=new A.t(344,B.a1Y)
B.a6E=s(["Home screen scrolls again: a long note\u2019s box no longer grows past the screen and traps the scroll \u2014 it caps at 15 lines, with the full text one tap away (\u2922)."],t.s)
B.aqo=new A.t(343,B.a6E)
B.a_V=s(["Voice/shortcut triggers (Hey Google, the launcher shortcuts) no longer switch you to Whisper \u2014 your engine stays put, and the default is always Deepgram (the live one)."],t.s)
B.aql=new A.t(342,B.a_V)
B.a1W=s(["When you dictate INTO a note, its box now glows red and pulses with your voice, and the mic turns red \u2014 just like the live box \u2014 so it\u2019s obvious that note is the one recording."],t.s)
B.apg=new A.t(341,B.a1W)
B.Xx=s(["Settings \u2192 \u201cFill in missing titles\u201d now also RE-TRANSCRIBES items that have audio but no text yet \u2014 so text-less notes in your folders/recent get a transcript and a title."],t.s)
B.ass=new A.t(340,B.Xx)
B.a0h=s(["A dictation that hasn\u2019t finished transcribing (e.g. you were offline) now shows on Home as \u201cTranscribing\u2026\u201d instead of disappearing \u2014 its audio is always safe and it fills in when the transcript lands."],t.s)
B.apW=new A.t(339,B.a0h)
B.a4u=s(["Open straight into command-listening: a new \u201cListen for commands\u201d shortcut (long-press the icon) / voicley://command link \u2014 point a \u201cHey Google\u201d Routine at it."],t.s)
B.apl=new A.t(338,B.a4u)
B.Z_=s(["A folder\u2019s headphones are now a master switch: one tap turns listening on (or off) for EVERYTHING under it \u2014 every subfolder and item."],t.s)
B.aws=new A.t(337,B.Z_)
B.YP=s(["Dictating into a note now lets you TYPE while the mic runs \u2014 talk and type at the same time, right in that box, exactly like the live one."],t.s)
B.apP=new A.t(336,B.YP)
B.ZZ=s(["Dictate into a note IN PLACE \u2014 tap a note\u2019s mic and your words now stream into THAT box (no separate \u201cnew\u201d box); \u201cend note\u201d saves them to it."],t.s)
B.at7=new A.t(335,B.ZZ)
B.a6g=s(["Item box now keeps the date/time and drops the transcription length (the length shows in the full-window player)."],t.s)
B.aw7=new A.t(334,B.a6g)
B.a4s=s(["The dictation time moved off the item box into the full-sized view \u2014 the box is cleaner (just the length up top)."],t.s)
B.as9=new A.t(333,B.a4s)
B.a4H=s(["Toggling a note\u2019s listen switch no longer flashes the folder pane on Windows."],t.s)
B.aug=new A.t(332,B.a4H)
B.Zw=s(["The note\u2019s mic now works exactly like the live box: tap to dictate into the note, then tap again to pause/resume (mic \u2194 mic-slash)."],t.s)
B.arc=new A.t(331,B.Zw)
B.Xf=s(["The note\u2019s mic \ud83c\udf99 now sits to the right of the text, in the same spot as the live dictation box."],t.s)
B.aqj=new A.t(330,B.Xf)
B.a4F=s(["Every note now has a mic \ud83c\udf99 \u2014 tap it and keep dictating into that note; your new words append to it. (Audio of the added part comes next.)"],t.s)
B.asa=new A.t(329,B.a4F)
B.Y6=s(["When a listening playlist reaches the last item, the reader now says it\u2019s restarting from the beginning, then loops."],t.s)
B.avK=new A.t(328,B.Y6)
B.X_=s(["Deleting an item from a folder now leaves it in its other folders \u2014 it only drops out of the one you deleted it from."],t.s)
B.awL=new A.t(327,B.X_)
B.a0A=s(["Say \u201cresume dictation\u201d (or \u201cresume dictating\u201d) to pick your note back up \u2014 and the command chip now says so."],t.s)
B.awI=new A.t(326,B.a0A)
B.a_H=s(["While dragging an item to file it, hover the top or bottom of the folder list to scroll \u2014 reach folders that were off-screen."],t.s)
B.axn=new A.t(325,B.a_H)
B.a65=s(["Tap an item in a folder or on the home screen to edit it right there \u2014 the \u2922 button opens the full window."],t.s)
B.atF=new A.t(324,B.a65)
B.a5G=s(["After each update, a quick \u201cWhat\u2019s new\u201d note tells you what changed."],t.s)
B.atg=new A.t(323,B.a5G)
B.Xu=s(["Edits you make in the full-window now show in your lists right away."],t.s)
B.as4=new A.t(322,B.Xu)
B.ZC=s(["Play a folder right in the list \u2014 the current note highlights and stays on top."],t.s)
B.apK=new A.t(321,B.ZC)
B.Yn=s(["Folders that hold audio-only notes now play their recordings."],t.s)
B.atz=new A.t(320,B.Yn)
B.a3o=s(["\u201cDictation\u201d is now \u201cNote\u201d everywhere \u2014 say \u201cnew note\u201d (the old \u201cnew dictation\u201d still works)."],t.s)
B.avh=new A.t(319,B.a3o)
B.a4t=s(["Type into your task and meeting boxes while they\u2019re recording."],t.s)
B.asp=new A.t(318,B.a4t)
B.a2p=s(["Talk-or-type: type into the live note box, with a mic button to turn capture on and off."],t.s)
B.avx=new A.t(317,B.a2p)
B.ZS=s(["The reader no longer traps you \u2014 move around the app while it plays."],t.s)
B.aty=new A.t(316,B.ZS)
B.a2a=s(["\u201cTurn On / Turn Off\u201d buttons, a clearer drag handle, and a steadier folder pane."],t.s)
B.avk=new A.t(315,B.a2a)
